[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114e2c5c-e9dd-526c-ae0c-f4bd4455b974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278752} {:answer/id #uuid "43b3f900-4df0-5da7-a38e-25a5c0664f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279199} {:answer/id #uuid "45f90586-57c8-5cb9-a773-850a108afbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279216} {:answer/id #uuid "513449f1-9cde-56ba-bba6-c67edd180332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279332} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2cc5a0-0ccb-5606-a4dc-e9f5799eb33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280363} :answer-field/id #uuid "7440ee8d-881a-5b79-93db-d064eac73e7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46d9a0e8-afba-50ff-93a4-8a5c09128d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162802" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d80d2d-e154-51fd-a594-5eba0a5e6b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278765} {:answer/id #uuid "14b8499c-89bd-5bc3-931b-c40e7ce37aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278777} {:answer/id #uuid "72b205e0-a52e-5333-be4c-5c18dbe481a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3aff6a-8638-5f77-8507-0d3f55b19bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280102} {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea93f9f9-61f9-5ba5-a529-0a7b5d54ca7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "915ccbd6-d5b8-5b79-ba41-8aee577baac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162886" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a49d17-1ccd-5d21-8067-3cc28c01dfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279659} {:answer/id #uuid "9498af25-8e31-5391-9c5d-57b5a039adb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279913} {:answer/id #uuid "9af2af40-8464-54ca-9c05-b548baff602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279964} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e4b95-0c02-5828-a503-1bc5e276aa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} :answer-field/id #uuid "98a966d4-0bb1-5c55-b273-7cce49dd0df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c852df75-6989-5a7d-bc7f-0618226da77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162887" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "167e6409-5e3e-5efb-a712-07b360471d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278790} {:answer/id #uuid "2732ae5b-753f-525a-b5e9-1120f08e3c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278940} {:answer/id #uuid "775abca6-570c-5895-b34d-8941c17f178b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279658} {:answer/id #uuid "88d3bab5-4d08-59c8-a45a-f21f13e573d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279814} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d35eb7-48e3-5f6c-b3b0-c9bb57e1a8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278940} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17aea6f-d602-5f51-a2f7-34b15c79c4b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b4076e3-ab6e-5bdd-85c0-2781a4c652d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162977" :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{y}\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x={e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023f3c24-7fe0-5840-a5d6-809d8e39927d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278602} {:answer/id #uuid "6b891af7-6f5f-5eaf-950c-57035bf987c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb07a70-2283-5402-854b-2e586af0ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280242} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f1470b-6007-5e6b-b19f-f373d9e47ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280489} {:answer/id #uuid "f75a797c-d0fa-553b-9ecc-505014a0d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280489} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bae934-f9e0-5c93-a239-1690cff765fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24e60831-e83f-5ffa-a929-6a3941104ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162981" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 4$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab53738-993e-59ba-a1e8-6bb955f46de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279684} {:answer/id #uuid "a6831946-5019-5b85-9063-1b4dab620eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280066} {:answer/id #uuid "b280e2f2-55be-5bf6-95bb-797c1636d7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280169} {:answer/id #uuid "c23fb48f-217d-54f4-a91c-cc54d14ec0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280299} {:answer/id #uuid "d18bc7ea-ff7c-57a0-a463-a74cf375f0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280169} :answer-field/id #uuid "4410fe5e-400a-5a84-991b-82e4ac1cc320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b911aeca-0e09-5dc0-b2cb-6f0f1115cce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163017" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3}.$ Given that the rod's mass is $\\frac{1}{4}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{15}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a71c1b3-91c2-5f52-845f-04435742cc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278966} {:answer/id #uuid "829887b6-f8aa-5df6-8883-a0dedb9aa152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279761} {:answer/id #uuid "ac514e81-2c67-5a54-adf6-8edfee4cc4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280118} {:answer/id #uuid "d14a622b-c91d-516a-8818-4f3b6665541f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280439} {:answer/id #uuid "d87e9950-be36-5abb-9c69-4e617ff6b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279761} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b4281-3410-59bf-b913-719f822ced96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48efa884-8150-5e6b-8d0a-1ebe0194039d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163019" :question/problem "![](images/88/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c78a1ef-5fb0-5540-89b2-4659460ab533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279277} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed3829a-89a5-519d-80bf-3569b4e61c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279308} {:answer/id #uuid "55157823-eaaf-53df-99da-d2f03b152f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279366} {:answer/id #uuid "92c1b59c-089e-517f-88ba-5a375cc226ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279895} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9775fb-c043-54c2-9e71-eafa3d190c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279366} :answer-field/id #uuid "45aaf83c-f68b-5099-ab34-db20ecc9dffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "085351ec-f493-50f6-a73c-ef9589568633" :question/math-academy-id "q-163022" :question/problem "![](images/54/54e5dd5fdea01c02b968a6040068e190676841cb8d7b7fc05c561e02d522bbf1.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{15}{32}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{15}{32}\\right)}=\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8a0fdf-12c7-5b75-8043-96dd8c6d8fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278864} {:answer/id #uuid "1f40f2ae-7d70-5921-ab4c-c447d83fe8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278883} {:answer/id #uuid "8af6b14a-43f6-593e-b744-d2368f888a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279833} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2dccae-b734-557e-9cdd-90e267c1800d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279966} {:answer/id #uuid "a3164c47-e49b-5e33-902a-2689fc2ac96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279966} :answer-field/id #uuid "e89be084-9afd-58bd-bbcb-cf93be0efaa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b02297fd-4e01-5d21-a6d0-693013c589ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-163024" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d64311921135cf8dea0ec70bb0a83909fb1dba52664f4d6080de596be1693d5e.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" :question/worked-solution "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}8{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-4{x}^{-2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{4}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $3\\,\\text{kg}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0072a6bc-bcf0-53b9-9a48-720f5054cc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278587} {:answer/id #uuid "0524c95c-8812-5349-9386-45c8f675ab97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278637} {:answer/id #uuid "3972def6-c7d8-515a-87e9-0534bfbd5657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279096} {:answer/id #uuid "83e4f599-c3c2-5dcc-b944-f86afba9dcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279775} {:answer/id #uuid "e104cfe2-82e9-570e-8703-bd68c8b20aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6d0f0d-92a3-5001-9ef4-52626e87f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90019212-5ef4-5914-92e3-b64c1a7668c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163026" :question/problem "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot (8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8x-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{48}{5}\\right)}{12}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c45a66-453c-5275-b9d1-243fc57b861d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278792} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4c9d86-7a5b-5a80-a11f-9027f99643d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279701} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8d313f-0419-56ec-b0aa-bc81ec3ecf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279868} {:answer/id #uuid "95dd5555-f7fd-5a4a-932f-f5bacbc9faad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279923} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b491bb-063e-511e-845c-f58d32a7572a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279701} :answer-field/id #uuid "605ef4a4-3f07-5257-aa5b-f1700c179ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32fa56f7-ac3c-5e91-abfd-f4c07339b203" :question/math-academy-id "q-163027" :question/problem "![](images/32/32500120ed36acde90f5b27c44a6cf45f2d4766bacb5eb097debc0cd7117f27f.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" :question/worked-solution "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x,\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(27-\\frac{9}{2})-(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $22\\,\\text{kg}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2463d915-966b-5efc-a567-b8578c3f3231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278917} {:answer/id #uuid "426114e3-d2ec-5b9f-b0b6-dd8fbf47c25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279187} {:answer/id #uuid "5d072d07-4a13-5f68-a349-82886da95020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279444} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3bff1-7c1f-5cfc-b1d5-cf4f68569adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280393} {:answer/id #uuid "e21b1fc3-e2d8-52e1-8dd3-fcd1dce43ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280590} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cffed6d-aeb8-5c15-a9e7-973d387a3db0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "109a4584-2208-5339-8501-61e59c46616d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163030" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca0ff396606039accf8c3acb4fd1b8e505dd16d2fe9b765fcd5c043d1538a573.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" :question/worked-solution "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=2{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\text{kg}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e1ea63-2c2a-5910-a535-44e8d580dcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278962} {:answer/id #uuid "480eac55-4d0a-5c10-9961-86cbedbd7a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279235} {:answer/id #uuid "7a026ea2-2b0f-5ae4-8223-2b8335f638e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279679} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9074c3-f066-5d7c-91d2-d2c4e58f0ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279730} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5330e1-4f07-54e7-a37c-1325ebc7a149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279843}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278962} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7c562f-41de-5340-9bdd-96b4ae84e756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c12a191-792b-5050-84d6-8d815e68ff76" :question/math-academy-id "q-163037" :question/problem "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 4,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{4}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $3\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073582cf-dc1f-5b20-a131-1b7fc3196575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278658} {:answer/id #uuid "2053e66b-0682-51df-8cdd-6dab423d5701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278890} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6ff2e8-dbd5-5488-b8be-01957e879cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279257} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ddf3bf-95f4-5b0d-a582-57d35a10863f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280029} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6932ff-76b6-526e-a623-987589fa1ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280248} :answer-field/id #uuid "3653de4a-c148-58af-a630-33787988c4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f548775-b0fd-5f5a-8099-718c65af1a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-163039" :question/problem "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=3x+1,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x+1,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot (3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{4}{2})-(1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2} \\\\  & =8.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $8.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c02838-9aa2-5198-a50d-54b9208db8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278821} {:answer/id #uuid "24c066a6-06e4-563f-ae44-77ea55af4f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278918} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbcd1f5-1d85-592b-bfff-6ac48084fe86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278985} {:answer/id #uuid "30198471-9ceb-5ca3-9fc9-3a8167eef240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279012} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dcd3f3-fa9b-5c25-bfaa-3547a3d527a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278918} :answer-field/id #uuid "5988fc1a-a700-5e4a-a1be-91a38529db6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0b7e3197-bf33-52b1-86e4-d8f2b5f29aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163041" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f88ca365b75320795e9d38170a8586399d759696638a4bd78c73ca6c6f44b1a.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=4-x\\,\\text{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=4-x,\\,a=0,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2158cc46-d0e1-5acf-bcf7-b53376dc28a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278893} {:answer/id #uuid "3f46b364-3904-5336-a7f8-40e116401c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279152} {:answer/id #uuid "9c47ff61-ba31-55ac-82b8-47bbcc2c3100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.25\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279980} {:answer/id #uuid "a62b53a8-b7e3-5f51-8cde-be30446031b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280064} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5fca7f-2f8d-56ca-932b-6b37e06bcdca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279980} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4a8ae0-f6f9-5407-b703-c15c9fe61a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edceef54-191f-5d57-a61a-4e3776dcf8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163042" :question/problem "![](images/88/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=6x+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (6x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $2.25\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073fe6ea-f7c7-5229-b831-86759e10e5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278659} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce8a52d-6f86-5584-a93a-9de23e0e6b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279442} {:answer/id #uuid "647ed6c8-f729-53b3-9e0d-f33cc021ed04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279506} {:answer/id #uuid "83170239-eb49-5115-9869-6b2e9537e8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279766} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ed58c4-5db9-5a4f-8a79-131cd0b0bdcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279506} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dd3ea61-a560-5eed-894b-f1f4ef3f8e93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1482f171-c56c-5bf1-b60d-7a3b492a5920" :question/math-academy-id "q-163053" :question/problem "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}$ is enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28a3f0e1eda23169a649276a9cde34d3d96cdd75d5b2bc71c3535604612526e5.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}15{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}15{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5/2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}(1-|x{|}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}-6{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{3}{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0a81d8-13d7-582a-8b41-a392595376a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278989} {:answer/id #uuid "4317249d-bf8c-51ae-832f-3ccae3822035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279194} {:answer/id #uuid "48a08e81-e9f4-555b-bc1a-45947a7f7606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279240} {:answer/id #uuid "5c59dc60-4505-5c4d-9a47-aa9bbfb5b511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279440} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3398bc-3678-517d-9759-8a6544ab7136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279194} :answer-field/id #uuid "586bad13-066b-5c7d-b740-b16bd4206a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "78ee316a-eb04-5e68-af98-0ccef9f21d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-163055" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-6$ given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x &=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058f3f49-dd9b-5bcf-91d7-3a13b16a9e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278643} {:answer/id #uuid "77ba5e2f-a104-5d77-9899-41ca53308fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279660} {:answer/id #uuid "c21732aa-50b9-5374-b1cf-aafdcf9b532e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280296} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a28a67-2379-5f88-bd30-3f1a0be7c1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280454} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bfa4ea-979d-50bf-aa25-25d0dbfd8906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280752} :answer-field/id #uuid "2417477b-102b-5b64-a662-c9071feb4de4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "950af147-dd8f-5a17-bc09-5c26666bfffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163062" :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\le y\\le 0\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=2{e}^{y}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=2{e}^{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 2{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{0}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}2x[{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{0}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}2x\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}2x(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}2x-2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{2}{3})-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e521e4f-0860-5a65-9ac2-eda32bfca8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279305} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff0cfbd-f9da-5245-a475-255ea08605cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279318} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdb2853-701b-5307-ad7e-d512948651d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279873} {:answer/id #uuid "ab21d0b4-eff6-548f-b2b3-7f4952880828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280108} {:answer/id #uuid "fd58d3b2-06c6-5e92-89a3-b6bb6ae53691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279305} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47ab8ca-3bde-5fba-a4a7-31651f3af0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe9f4bda-1254-5b20-b5e7-335406a2dc03" :question/math-academy-id "q-163066" :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=5xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=5xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 5xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y{\\int }_{0}^{3y}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y(9{y}^{3}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}45{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[9{y}^{5}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f5a574-0cca-57fa-a4fd-7d5cf83c8022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc014a-0203-582f-8cb4-ea9a4980b836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279135} {:answer/id #uuid "5d71a5fd-a1a5-578b-b16d-463e3b9ca06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279450} {:answer/id #uuid "8070acd2-0eb1-5b3d-8631-c79cba8f6cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279743} {:answer/id #uuid "b07a55f4-358b-52f0-8ce8-2a341242e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb211f7-a024-5d85-9487-56111b032805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "636e23a3-a873-5229-81ee-5d1161e02dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163070" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({\\sin }^{5}⁡(0)-{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a396f5b6-38a4-5482-9879-8445e818f551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280043} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f1fd63-e8c8-5316-8fc9-37c6465175d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{6}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280047} {:answer/id #uuid "d610e460-01e5-559a-bb12-51fb3e5027e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280481} {:answer/id #uuid "db16381b-5389-51b6-8b6a-3b6b7c0cbe58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{9}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280526} {:answer/id #uuid "ea064c47-985f-55ea-84ef-0621857af059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280647} :answer-field/id #uuid "6676760e-b558-5efa-bb47-3af634a82513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "930d6994-09ee-58f8-aa72-d60045e82fbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163087" :question/problem "![](images/32/32500120ed36acde90f5b27c44a6cf45f2d4766bacb5eb097debc0cd7117f27f.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}-x,\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot ({x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $\\frac{34}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488d7ff-b23d-57ca-ac1f-3b003b1d4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278628} {:answer/id #uuid "19abce14-7fbc-5539-b049-f6588065cc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} {:answer/id #uuid "1b20a70e-e52e-59a8-a425-efc1714f3b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278843} {:answer/id #uuid "4be49a5e-04bf-5a10-ba8f-0ff99825ca37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279271} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c391e8-39ad-5fb2-9766-ec1eb8cec68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278820} :answer-field/id #uuid "11825cc6-3e2c-51ee-8c64-1a71cc1b22e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7670cca-4082-532e-925a-af41e094c0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163122" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00586df5-9953-5e7a-a58f-2329aa16a468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278586} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf84ed5-f917-50b8-8e4d-80368c472356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279121} {:answer/id #uuid "4237198b-add2-521b-b160-d585a0924755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279184} {:answer/id #uuid "58055d99-9594-5371-bd9f-20b8d761809d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279400} {:answer/id #uuid "86941ae5-17f0-581b-91b1-2673bcdf9949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279121} :answer-field/id #uuid "945c0f7a-70d5-5057-8a4c-71bc5f6f818a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e73bf43d-4842-564c-b994-8b4ed88b9ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163146" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+\\frac{8y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[4y+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =12+12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e916b0-4053-558e-b9e2-fb010d0079e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279101} {:answer/id #uuid "6717d5eb-a675-55ba-ab48-3b932471e1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279525} {:answer/id #uuid "67a4e5bc-094b-5bc0-b24d-26314e5a86e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279532} {:answer/id #uuid "e735fad1-0d3b-5a5c-9df6-04260dc4278f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280629} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a3f08e-da67-596e-a0f8-03c03a5fd812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279525} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a7cb95-9f50-5d35-89f8-e9b2f23fadd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51a515de-4536-5de9-a9e1-b88421eacc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163148" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-2y+2,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[6{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}+2y]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}+x]_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {1}^{3}+1)-(2\\cdot (-1)^{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =f(\\frac{1}{2},b)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =(6\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2b+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{7}{2}-2b \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f5fb45-2fae-517d-b34c-c987569bdec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278622} {:answer/id #uuid "4526b476-fcd9-5d37-a7e3-e30a4fe882ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279208} {:answer/id #uuid "52dfc9d3-54d9-5dc1-a00c-2582e20de483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279345} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb03689-d055-5100-8394-403fc499e7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279606} {:answer/id #uuid "942cb1e4-d459-5e88-9e20-4919448700c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279345} :answer-field/id #uuid "13279313-89f2-561e-b944-a8d52266ad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13f35429-2bb3-5bdd-9f21-f76be76ab433" :question/math-academy-id "q-163152" :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le 1\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[x]_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}1-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=1/2}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08331b96-1ea3-5297-bddf-925a7fab7e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278668} {:answer/id #uuid "375023d4-a6cf-5348-a290-c644b5d02793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279076} {:answer/id #uuid "79398d0a-7f88-5b17-bc98-845eb58089f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279675} {:answer/id #uuid "82a44769-c3fb-5a9a-b496-6d19c625f28a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279762} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7199cc-dc59-5269-b502-31dd8c1e5fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279762} :answer-field/id #uuid "31814b4c-b01e-5393-ab71-e442edf7ce20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6cb70e6-635f-52ae-9613-df9cd3db0061" :question/math-academy-id "q-163155" :question/problem "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=2y$ is enclosed between the line $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddc11fc93f034c3d650854e21f178716c941c18f12eb94123e1af2c835c47981.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=2y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16205635-c6e2-54cc-b321-158a614700bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278788} {:answer/id #uuid "40f6af20-b691-5a75-9ab5-3967bde5cf92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279169} {:answer/id #uuid "92070dc4-c11e-5c9f-9a77-1e1f7a591dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279892} {:answer/id #uuid "af97bdd4-d844-5c5e-ab36-f279e9a4dc11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280146} {:answer/id #uuid "bd69ec15-2dd8-58af-9b35-578926bf94d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280146} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ded9e2-7680-5857-96a0-f9ff21c5ecfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53646bcf-6387-54da-9ed9-6c682d87bd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-163165" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{9}{4}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a291c9-6951-5200-88b0-09bb5598418a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278653} {:answer/id #uuid "38784e79-8065-52f2-a332-8df2e6627d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279084} {:answer/id #uuid "4385bd71-7ea6-5a03-b0c3-a443b6ae8453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279198} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c16913-a5e6-5f22-8217-a15c256207a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280084} {:answer/id #uuid "f22e1bd3-b98c-5da9-bf27-6f18a13116d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279198} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bff334-34d6-5ecf-8482-dd41436c8ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7952674e-a3fc-593f-9da1-748cc35cbe0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163166" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{-y}\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $2$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{{e}^{-y}}^{1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x={e}^{-y}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}0-(-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257070d0-96de-5c56-8968-f3132c5ed6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278924} {:answer/id #uuid "29c35d3d-762a-5353-98f8-756b472452d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278960} {:answer/id #uuid "9c50a528-be93-5d42-9d1d-5373833eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279981} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec6f369-7250-5e34-b528-cb5dc49bdb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280001} {:answer/id #uuid "eab5e69e-ea27-5171-a0d9-9069f47edb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30.1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278960} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd949b6b-ce75-5adf-a47b-6ef31d9ee89f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e47b1623-c58d-5075-9113-1251be6ca292" :question/math-academy-id "q-163170" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(4y+1)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(4y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}36y+9+12{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[18{y}^{2}+9y+3{y}^{4}+{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18+9+3+1 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e774e3e-5483-570a-be90-3556fdfeef80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278875} {:answer/id #uuid "264c43c5-9ce5-5021-829e-191395085650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278931} {:answer/id #uuid "27a3b396-361a-5015-9461-0aae23b48259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278947} {:answer/id #uuid "4373458f-0c28-5ba9-9faf-49c7a51e957d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279197} {:answer/id #uuid "aec868ad-08a2-517d-b508-f8953c729333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280137} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ebe4cc-aac8-5388-84d5-8872f110619f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "566286c6-ab64-5382-ab24-0d1952795e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-163174" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6910defd242e1b65b7bdf8b580cd2f56df3ac43951a9f83f353003e00a849b4.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=0$, $x=4$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=3y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd5ee021dd73317a775c0ea653212f7ee5545f8c6854e6a9daabdc93b5c3b9c0.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecb2226-0daf-5797-b7eb-dc78f79bbc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279146} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3cfb43-ec77-5d28-b15f-94144c90fe3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279842} {:answer/id #uuid "a70dc1b8-51da-5b7a-9ae0-6ef0f67e66fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280069} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb1d749-2ecd-5ce9-bbfc-6b1701c7e53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280377} {:answer/id #uuid "d23e1bea-df09-57c2-8bbf-89b768b9e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280069} :answer-field/id #uuid "b251effd-5b30-539b-b02d-ba4196b3539c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ef76132-2758-54ad-96e6-4961fca66327" :question/math-academy-id "q-163178" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}kx\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{2}-0)(2-0) \\\\  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=kx=Mx.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061826f5-8e23-5cb2-953e-ec85b61d7257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278647} {:answer/id #uuid "159d9b68-bec7-565b-b078-5cf212fd676d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278784} {:answer/id #uuid "58d896b7-9f75-58d5-ae24-c13c48ef62b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279409} {:answer/id #uuid "635f7c5b-85bc-5851-b091-9cec0f663042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279495} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce6dafd-27a6-5fe8-b358-10d58bcc1905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279582} :answer-field/id #uuid "004171ef-3db6-57d5-adce-f300d0f57322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ba69e53-fc8f-5143-953e-05bbe8878807" :question/math-academy-id "q-163180" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}ky\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k[x]_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(3-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{2M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{2M}{3}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}({\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(9-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30491144-e2a9-5de8-a3cd-11a0f89cc6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279013} {:answer/id #uuid "874e8595-292b-5f4e-bd66-51a639f6022f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279801} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6b9256-f496-53ff-aad8-44a1442bdf5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279983} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9e4de9-d138-53fa-bcae-346a8056acc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280250} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a5a940-b3ec-59b0-9c37-09b3fc4d604e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279801} :answer-field/id #uuid "07946d72-168a-58f9-99a2-0417d27cc3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c204945d-1cc7-5037-a46b-3df54b66b209" :question/math-academy-id "q-163183" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc61efd6e5ef1fd46fca53f7c1e47394c6b9c95fb2455dc0c798d9936a68631b.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis, $y$-axis, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/626cbf8e182d9eebb8b48026f07c5dd012c1096cc9568465f3648710ea0d07a4.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(10y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}10{x}^{2}y+10{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})+\\frac{5}{2}{(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2{x}^{2}+4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}16-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[16x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(32-\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1a8c9b-b06f-53ad-a34d-9a181e8b043b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278840} {:answer/id #uuid "314b01d5-b5bc-556c-a0c9-e9004f3a02e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279025} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c0a7c5-ec37-5a7d-939e-e01f5e149eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280293} {:answer/id #uuid "db854b28-2888-5519-b52e-c433dae31273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280530} {:answer/id #uuid "f468f901-fab1-59cb-a76d-9c0a97011d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0941e69a-7d66-5128-844f-24c2a176eca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ff4be2e-d981-5594-842f-9759fc5e3f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-163186" :question/problem "![](images/04/043119a0637985fa55a7084a8d4b67df5031b24211ab421ec019700235ca2365.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782b4df0220d84d90d26a4ad1fd4a34da00ecf3c5d62a9416d9f0490ca765e2b.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}k{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(1-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(1-x)^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=12M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2}=12M{y}^{2}.$ Therefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}(1-x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[-\\frac{1}{6}(1-x)^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c031bd6-6215-5f80-826c-642a6d5b92aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278979} {:answer/id #uuid "52f49d5a-a68d-535e-964d-5242fa3dfa1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279346} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaf6f6a-eaf4-5db1-843e-117fcd3cb5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279429} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a59d50-27b5-5d07-be4a-b6e13b4fdaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280705} {:answer/id #uuid "f739e5d0-2f35-5623-8e59-a9551a47bd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280758} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1daf45-f02b-5a3d-b91a-e85b1f696b36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "919815e1-edf3-55d7-8a11-9de7077a701d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163189" :question/problem "![](images/37/37fde9d99a144ab65874a9fa329bc9a4ca32b0f4a6981993134e98c47a32e07f.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y=x,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f08ac68e3d8992e70cdc40eddfb9cdc39ee16a275890f3d9d7db42a682866bf6.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{x}^{2}y]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{4}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=\\frac{M}{4}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{x}^{4}y]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0195eb44-6a96-5ddf-b1f9-461d55429c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278598} {:answer/id #uuid "5cea505d-0154-58f8-af06-dc68ee8e2bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279443} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cb1efc-d120-5ffe-b526-b05f34ce5123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280062} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c8923d-e627-5e20-ac90-0473b04402a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280067} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5a3e48-f028-5967-919f-b2f7d1b6ce09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280067} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f021cea-70e8-577f-8b59-0fdc1b90130b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "811ae27f-22b7-5099-ac9f-caeaa4c509e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163199" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a39a8998e4e1ae9f6ed8095b124ba31db5f3b4ffb699db815ef03144479f798c.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=2-2x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-2x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aad890b85e9984fdab58a2fa15e00fad4b384a086346ad77f49f68a746e73b3a.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}(2-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2k{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2k{[-\\frac{1}{3}(1-x)^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{3M}{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x}{y}^{2}(y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(2-2x)^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{[-\\frac{1}{5}(1-x)^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a700cc-9a80-52fe-b948-d035b0b0b50c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278741} {:answer/id #uuid "365b10c1-9a22-5c79-b9b5-50173e8aadde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279066} {:answer/id #uuid "93ceeab5-b213-5adc-ad04-95c2bdacb462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{9}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279906} {:answer/id #uuid "97708c84-8902-5f55-921b-48e526407aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279931} {:answer/id #uuid "c877bf68-98d8-5d6e-ba06-a282245771bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280346} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2bfd6e-e15f-531c-8cb4-6aaab1b19f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8b4a4d7-db6d-5f9c-bc7b-f800dc788f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-163200" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 3=9.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{9}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{[9y+{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}(36+64-9-1) \\\\  & =10M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a4e499-61e4-5d51-8488-e60716c8b89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279063} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc2f52c-6937-5664-b1ad-a8e9f01fba53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279296} {:answer/id #uuid "77349270-7aa3-5ac9-a411-62c1c99df8b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656} {:answer/id #uuid "81467cbe-f5a5-5724-8a67-a32eb23c17bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279753} {:answer/id #uuid "e207c367-c309-58d1-92c5-7517420bb1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656} :answer-field/id #uuid "5beaea88-1fc4-552a-a4a5-3b0f75db282a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4167aa80-a0c6-5dcf-ad5c-868018ed4d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-163201" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 3=6.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[x]_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $3M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c84deba-cb38-5f3b-af0e-58521ec502a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279280} {:answer/id #uuid "a25defa0-44b9-5bda-a10f-e6ef6a677aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280035} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5bf3b6-9c4b-50ce-879e-8ec79739fdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280127} {:answer/id #uuid "ca352098-298f-5e14-a7d7-0c7272084e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280364} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1d4484-aff6-5376-8a09-a8cd9f6af638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280127} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a384dc8-494e-5973-bebc-5b4d13e8a8c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "095d8c09-ff9b-5528-8c3d-3967d18698a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163204" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{8y}{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e3253af-36cf-50a6-b6a4-e0cebd8a088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279300} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb31ddb-172c-5405-bfcb-0893d02422b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d136b5-8a1b-538d-932f-235fc197906b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280044} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c2097d-3ba9-5baa-934e-6dd265730b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280216} {:answer/id #uuid "c944cd9a-1153-596c-ae76-dc3d60055c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d2b3a3-7b86-546b-8f02-ed9d3ed11cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33038417-f976-5b04-ba1c-849f23418261" :question/math-academy-id "q-163207" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9x}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot (3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\cdot (3)}{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =f(2,b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =(2+b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =9b+18 \\\\ 9 & =9b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d50eaec-2761-511b-a299-ac2ff1333565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279131} {:answer/id #uuid "68823909-c88a-5645-9191-b732f12dac1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279542} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa1e53-51b6-5bde-8f08-eb601cf1e05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279676} {:answer/id #uuid "856764d8-02ae-52b0-88dd-114d96718896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279789} {:answer/id #uuid "cf88cb2f-c430-5ef6-8cc7-c99324e16f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280419} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf88e2d-a46f-5e96-b3db-c6aa9b189759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96817e72-a430-5366-920a-3988e4d8fc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-163217" :question/problem "![](images/de/de3753a45554be28283e74116510ff6c3b67560fce79d1570c819a215f6e10f3.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=3x$ and $y=4-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47c897bd58a23965d9c36f683553c0817afa8bc2e37ed53a432edcd62b386e55.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}(4-y-\\frac{1}{3}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(36-27) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839f5ece-276f-5816-96b9-c527a2493a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279770} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e6e3da-9c21-52c4-86e6-90e111164bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280230} {:answer/id #uuid "d898035f-95c6-5b96-a44f-c85c5e0fb62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280506} {:answer/id #uuid "f28d76a8-28fa-5a01-aefa-cb1bdd261513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280703} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd690e9-c999-5620-b45c-5474e864788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279770} :answer-field/id #uuid "faab536c-ba69-524a-89bb-9d4a9ee45c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a1f6eeb-74ef-55ae-8480-c174f3bc8d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-163219" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c683f411b11b7b353e166364c8e0fdbc0d218c07535c2f3430d832ab129702d8.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $y$-axis and the lines $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c2cec5e919c25623396b5ba5f052481623a868c7520aa8e3d1d3eb34f3a4a58.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(3-x-\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(8-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104b5f45-a5a5-53b8-81f3-1afa7e730667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278738} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1d9c0f-74a7-5840-a8f1-946c355e9f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1151a9-1581-5ce8-97ab-8d91464f04d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280107} {:answer/id #uuid "de04ac09-2b08-5de9-947d-c33d64af0400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280549} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ed382-97b6-5633-976b-856f4f15d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f8be0c-a6d9-58ec-b1bd-534c48dbccb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cf0a2c6-6793-5c24-82b1-b8c9fc0536bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163225" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1ef5062d5bc045c1e1f7fa58c513315852f74aa15539c001662b0055583f01b.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2$ and the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(32-1)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =31\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17226919-ce93-5049-994f-31d7cd5c10fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278798} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b17cc-de83-57bb-914f-013137289a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} {:answer/id #uuid "78cff65f-0d69-5e60-9e1c-47253821dc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279669} {:answer/id #uuid "8d83c82c-bb2f-532f-ab7d-a58f6e2cc4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279853} {:answer/id #uuid "e875a915-69ff-5a94-9c30-f7fd03b5fb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0fe541-fc86-58e2-a9d5-645ba6b20816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2726afac-54d3-5734-a6e0-8fa5ce77d367" :question/math-academy-id "q-163228" :question/problem "![](images/04/044395de3b30755d0c83d489709c38420827f6984af740bc56a3dc5bfaa33794.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-4)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eeb6dc-6a62-5d90-a207-0acfc7258433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278919} {:answer/id #uuid "349f0582-d339-5e4d-9545-b31c93cda4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279045} {:answer/id #uuid "538264a8-2746-5c22-950d-ad36b0c19129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8065a-0247-55f8-99df-fad5f0147068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280342} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ebd0da-d30a-58a4-863a-0db44212f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2db863-9898-51d6-ba39-8b881e966e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6b312d9-6efa-541f-ad5d-be93b575260c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163230" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad7c939aded3f5e5e1e2f3b5e5743609e331625479c25f6a55659be730ed1cf5.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt[5]{2}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15y.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt[5]{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}\\cdot (-{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b0ade-efaa-5fe6-9755-cb0ea40e9586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279038} {:answer/id #uuid "67a63d06-0c0e-5908-ac74-1d7efd261625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279533} {:answer/id #uuid "9955f41f-f95d-5bf4-a6d2-fe762a8f2486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279946} {:answer/id #uuid "afc5f9a7-e607-52fc-bb1c-ce3001c8f5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280148} {:answer/id #uuid "afcb8747-6fa1-5f79-8c5a-c6844fed36bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279946} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a31a6fc-3289-5194-b854-0c3b7246d9e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ec0c814-755c-501d-ad78-db2aa0c4e9e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163235" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+6{y}^{2}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot 2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "535f04ee-70e9-5f01-b34b-53660081decb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279349} {:answer/id #uuid "94c9f393-f7e3-5725-b98c-409c0939e18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279914} {:answer/id #uuid "be2b3b90-dc97-502a-b811-4f9ef9fa7d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280264} {:answer/id #uuid "cb391e7e-7c21-53a9-b284-5d3daf858d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280371} {:answer/id #uuid "ed47a95d-b3d6-55c8-bf2e-23e1f2592039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280371} :answer-field/id #uuid "14fa30c2-c058-5d5d-bcd3-afbb3922e2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec31a85b-303d-5111-a311-f0cb89d6a30f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163242" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1a19303937f1d515ca820516e10a73974b00f83884b05abb58dee49c81207f4.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=8\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{8\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1423879f-45e2-5f3a-bf42-98ceec2f0d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278771} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0e119-d0b2-54a1-9a0c-77fb3087de25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279430} {:answer/id #uuid "b756c4f9-63a7-586c-a61f-d37beaab985f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280202} {:answer/id #uuid "dadffaa0-9dbd-5f20-98a2-b1ad72baf426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280525} {:answer/id #uuid "db6569e4-0abd-57d4-8672-9919fc65d2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278771} :answer-field/id #uuid "69710f6b-ffa0-54e7-80a3-cc71b4b02c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e73324e-535c-5fbc-b61e-13f64e0e8c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163243" :question/problem "![](images/04/044386c438976bf17e2f51241659f167f7eedfb6509d0a60f3f70ba91457e71f.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first, second and third quadrants bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03807599-de78-5ad7-a7e9-4c7bb13ddb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278614} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4be14e-90f4-58b3-9526-ee12c5821967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279575} {:answer/id #uuid "86a4b742-abbd-5e07-b5fb-d64388299e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279794} {:answer/id #uuid "c659f522-be42-55a2-8ae8-cad12771bb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280330} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc430d-d0d0-5a02-ad45-9f948009eaa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279575} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1213e4f-6a9b-5d11-ae9d-60df7a2d94b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ba106c5-525d-577c-b0dd-66ad242d632a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163255" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=12x{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{4}}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{∬}_{D}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x/4}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=x/4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{256}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{512}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07614726-b468-5358-bc84-48ba609cfd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4450b-952d-5300-8f14-fe1403fe104c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278776} {:answer/id #uuid "5f42bacb-1dcd-5151-b6dd-29e307c8e188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279465} {:answer/id #uuid "7821b0ff-67a0-50c1-b841-8830b1518869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279664} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5dd293-f356-5370-a825-276c22c64e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23f56f9-33c4-58f2-aed2-d65d0e9b71dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9470a948-0cd8-55e7-86d6-65b250b7e800" :question/math-academy-id "q-163260" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ on the portion of the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/25/25c1f00e588ddaa130f854d9f4e9dfe9b1158d7952a01bae8108ea4b94d3c347.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c31261-cc86-5031-9910-a0aa56fc4198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278948} {:answer/id #uuid "5db55013-4b0a-5959-a032-9861dd889ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279451} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81935b-92c2-57e0-9a61-a16c44433fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279602} {:answer/id #uuid "d2535b83-0fc4-52bc-8212-51a7b54adf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280449} {:answer/id #uuid "eed986c8-cc1c-5bc7-ba5f-edb0558ecf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279602} :answer-field/id #uuid "790be945-96ff-57ad-8c99-be25eeb95a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "57923a25-8079-5f42-9ca5-ab678216d9c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163269" :question/problem "![](images/df/df939119a0cb5b9a5fdafc5158ecd299e91477247d7d6e920e2e167921ba4bdd.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=1,\\,{m}_{3}=7.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+1+7 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6+1+28 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{23}{10}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2b8f1f-7729-5b9e-addf-b86ad9d1455c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279420} {:answer/id #uuid "79fbf9f8-8a86-5b39-9a86-7e3842b1f784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279678} {:answer/id #uuid "8111a08b-ff83-5bc9-9d2b-1503285ef025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279751} {:answer/id #uuid "98dc5b1a-7412-5534-b15f-27bd6e395d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279943} {:answer/id #uuid "e65be020-81a6-50f4-a6bd-6b618a6199c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280623} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca52d9c-fb5a-56de-94ca-a0c51d308197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89ecec92-3277-515e-84b9-2e6789dffe43" :question/math-academy-id "q-163270" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d66682c96a14fb37ea0eb2deb46190fbe651e013a05c16a95577423b49b0ab9.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=1,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=6.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-4,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4-6+18 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af12a33-41c9-5077-ae0b-2b3cd46f5153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279262} {:answer/id #uuid "b26f5b66-dee5-5c00-95cb-0c44c6175b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280168} {:answer/id #uuid "c5681503-02aa-5d3e-a186-3e9dace92ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280327} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2688d6-1d14-55a1-a83c-ff996d5b4f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280362} {:answer/id #uuid "e425a24a-2778-5d28-9efe-f9b538727fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280604} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4965030-428e-5f01-8ad3-cc497f8cc6e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2667dfdb-7185-5a1f-87a1-02e96e4c8952" :question/math-academy-id "q-163271" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa8065896ec5efdc71402581c78674e4681fd02df2f7b945b0a182ae271fedfc.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=6,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =6+2+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-18+4+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2327cc74-9d09-538f-a7ca-9620713b3b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278904} {:answer/id #uuid "382f6e14-6d8d-51da-9799-aec22d1c9089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279081} {:answer/id #uuid "58b51146-7aff-521f-9876-56b0470303c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279408} {:answer/id #uuid "83c04626-10f6-5a26-8913-ba3ae3a0655e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279772} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdec778-684b-5e4b-acfa-8558f907369f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279081} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f154ea9-9bd8-5795-b069-3a0a9e735ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf86aa62-6e5b-574f-8b87-7dd9140bd898" :question/math-academy-id "q-163273" :question/problem "![](images/df/df6c2234a0b62d04157db126350e9b71f861af34ab42769245e553a8e228cb2b.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=4,\\,{y}_{2}=2,\\,{y}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+6+2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{16}{6}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232955c8-373f-5dc3-bcea-e22f168e3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278905} {:answer/id #uuid "8011e930-219f-5786-9911-9d757201c119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279742} {:answer/id #uuid "851b9a69-7f4e-50f1-90db-8c249ed639c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279787} {:answer/id #uuid "86fa17bd-dd27-5a9a-bb29-ed8f02f3d111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279798} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a478f0-0b34-5eaf-a5e8-cc12c442fefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280455} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb31a051-05a3-5e09-92c9-6f2f7932d2b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "37b99aa7-cb84-5819-bd41-054f6b6fecd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163276" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e55b3a19251f3470401c081b24a90e90674844ce47d617dffd68bf161e2b7199.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=3,\\,{m}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=3,\\,{y}_{2}=5,\\,{y}_{3}=2,\\,{y}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+2+3+1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3}+{m}_{4}{y}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6+10+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{26}{8}=\\frac{13}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27060319-319a-5c93-87a4-021b464e69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278938} {:answer/id #uuid "96cddbff-aa96-5c15-b451-85bc3b5899ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279926} {:answer/id #uuid "d6297845-0a0a-51e3-9056-3d94de569441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280482} {:answer/id #uuid "d932caf8-3377-5328-b82d-2c72ffe1d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280511} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6871d0-cf0d-5786-9a5c-fd24e0463ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf3c23-4651-531a-8ea1-05778a87081e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4da84d34-65b6-5264-9689-a8f568b0485b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163278" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{\\cos }^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({\\cos }^{5}⁡\\pi -{\\cos }^{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02228a55-f0cd-5d20-a57e-0a6be96e6db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278600} {:answer/id #uuid "20802c9f-44b7-54c4-98fc-e21f7cad1d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278891} {:answer/id #uuid "2f618d22-048b-597a-97ca-eb05ca9ee64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279004} {:answer/id #uuid "34a613ef-9f35-5a47-9476-0c26e7c87ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279046} {:answer/id #uuid "d964673e-ff68-55cd-ae5e-82c200ba663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b55bdf5-772a-5e7e-b2a0-07c63cb6c150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "128ce1a7-9b5d-51fb-9a3d-78ee6a5214c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163279" :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0007cf9c-0395-5509-b616-4be695ec13cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278584} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6a9d20-68f0-5b58-918b-8ef34081db13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279005} {:answer/id #uuid "842bc963-2d06-5322-bb34-77f3b33fea49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279777} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fdd69b-b6ba-5d0d-9e9d-54a97ec7d638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280090} {:answer/id #uuid "b466adf4-9e43-5073-97cf-93330bff4141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279005} :answer-field/id #uuid "14124592-bf34-56d3-a109-e9e7b9325d95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "319f1b6f-8e13-5060-954e-d52a8b4d9a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-163280" :question/problem "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{32}{5}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a531e418-a76d-53a0-8633-2087bb2f0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280055} {:answer/id #uuid "abd0a9f3-627a-5abe-a1f0-41938425fb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280114} {:answer/id #uuid "d072327c-0e2d-5987-9c07-e7b6d1e3769e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280426} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfd01d1-85fc-517d-be56-fb1560da46df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280537} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bb34c6-47ba-5ceb-ad0d-71a63f5e63cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280776} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab546d7-f4d5-5da9-b797-80f69c700d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9939c00-b562-5295-851c-72bc554d376f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163281" :question/problem "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and is enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis for $1\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $2$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b6/b6e8af58b8e7ba5f909d226b895bb39b3fc94e53686e4718eb32c9bb4ee61c08.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e379653-24de-50d6-ab7c-c1801720325a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278867} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3f8bb4-3330-50a7-bc4b-0218e96b8169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279108} {:answer/id #uuid "4140d095-beba-58d1-b687-bcd88e6f6722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279173} {:answer/id #uuid "495a23ee-a0c4-502e-8521-ff19c028dd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279247} {:answer/id #uuid "b79d1511-6180-5940-87c0-7f9449fe8c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279108} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bce71d-4bb1-54a4-b8f2-124bb97a8cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6a2919a-fe73-5a7d-8a74-b011c8911292" :question/math-academy-id "q-163282" :question/problem "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y$ and is enclosed between the curve $x=2y$ and the $y$-axis for $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{3}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/30/3097e540292efd601743ba8de24acffc3b51469e77561b2bccbd799d1125e1ed.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14{y}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{y}^{4}}{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c004ead-ef86-5712-84a8-fae87b823d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278708} {:answer/id #uuid "4fac001b-722a-500c-87b9-46e2afa47d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279315} {:answer/id #uuid "5438f644-4aff-5aec-b8d5-9550b3487724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279357} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1eeb13-4a18-5f06-b0ab-8e0cfaca415f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279584} {:answer/id #uuid "cfef53e2-4360-53e0-acbf-d77b7df6d577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279584} :answer-field/id #uuid "46454292-fc9c-58eb-8d36-2abd8028480a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb3eeda9-913d-5b5e-9942-179e17b6de33" :question/math-academy-id "q-163284" :question/problem "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis for $-2\\le y\\le 0.$ Given that the area of the plate is $\\pi ,$ find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/14/14c292e8a224b59c143d0e8a57d43b554ce298cd4f44fc2169f516d683e8c35a.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},\\,-2\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{4-{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}4-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{[4y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[0-(-8+\\frac{8}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eee0da7-06ef-56b6-9c7d-3e4c929d706f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278880} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa15328-b58f-5bea-9235-d808b9338c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279605} {:answer/id #uuid "84b3faa4-0e39-57e0-bb95-495223d99c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279784} {:answer/id #uuid "a90f84e7-80da-50b1-8f55-4c86153c18f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280091} {:answer/id #uuid "f386ca7b-ec1a-5c33-8f54-9d3b226a2d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280722} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbf7c44-6b94-546f-bf57-e5f702d64210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e7bc910-4add-5a81-9d2e-b1278da454ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-163289" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 4}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $\\frac{16}{3}$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b81f67e-4537-58c8-b3cd-d19e5fa3c297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278847} {:answer/id #uuid "2345b620-038a-5dd1-98c5-cb031b7a6dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278907} {:answer/id #uuid "40333bc3-a45e-579e-a582-b416055d8c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} {:answer/id #uuid "5293ba89-96b0-54ba-a17c-3ee395860fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279344} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f10d88-6aa6-5f5f-9caa-d4548f12ec96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} :answer-field/id #uuid "89161b1d-3f89-547b-abef-9049dcf783b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e32d479-ba99-5277-86cf-01366333f094" :question/math-academy-id "q-163493" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y-1$ Find the moment of inertia of this plate about the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y-1)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2+y+1)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2)^{3}+(y+1)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{[y(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{2}(x-2)^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{4}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bc3abb-b97c-51e3-bac4-38ebd3f9974a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279205} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a89bf-1165-5025-aad4-e1717fcca8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49M}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279716} {:answer/id #uuid "835796e1-86ce-58fc-9beb-727a52f0b56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21M}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2a138f-9f8d-5c08-9c3b-a5703b1f5cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{93M}{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279826} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6cc418-6bd6-5e7a-9c5b-a65d6a49bf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51M}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc71749-05e0-5682-9015-b3a92d11c706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77c3fd9b-a588-5227-b546-d84306dcef4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163496" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9732e0b81ba2e171a0464996e7b0d27aa47a0b32da59fa40cbba0cdbf8402a.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}k{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r\\cdot {r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5914eb67-5ec2-5758-b284-5b5a7d6dbc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{70}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279410} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f7c451-b33c-5308-abfc-cbdde077a138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280344} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0dd7bb-5672-503c-a1aa-1180891f80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280417} {:answer/id #uuid "d106f0b4-585d-55db-88ba-8f17f309bc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280435} {:answer/id #uuid "f44b136e-4fd5-52d1-8857-56190fd5c18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280344} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b94ac81-5f5d-5c3c-9669-4cbb5a12e74e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5ef6472-c270-5d02-97a0-991db154ba3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163501" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y-x.$ Find the moment of inertia of this plate about the line $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-(-2)|=|x+2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-x)|x+2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-x)(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y(x+2)^{2}-x(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(x+2)^{2}-xy(x+2)^{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(3-2x)(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(-2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{-{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}+12x]}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+2+12)-(-8+\\frac{40}{3}+8-24)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((14-\\frac{13}{6})-(-24+\\frac{40}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{135}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9db8c3-273b-5052-b24e-dbcb83902b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278718} {:answer/id #uuid "7030943f-0c02-5cef-9c65-b51526072903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279608} {:answer/id #uuid "712b709d-786f-5ed6-b5f6-baf28f45d2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279619} {:answer/id #uuid "74dea547-8fd9-53e0-9fc4-5e087b220e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279643} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c8ab57-08dc-54ee-80e9-9761fe9cf6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279643} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8851e66-e4ff-5d6f-aa2e-4c31fae8826b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "832add81-6866-5228-aac7-83e3b7ca8301" :question/math-academy-id "q-163503" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $\\pi $ square units, find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{[4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22024028-60ef-57ba-80e5-a10b54fd0d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278900} {:answer/id #uuid "49d75631-f16e-58af-a616-c85049b24db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279252} {:answer/id #uuid "5814599d-213d-5520-b36f-aeca6ad5e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279402} {:answer/id #uuid "64d3a450-651c-5f80-9061-2e3fa2495812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279508} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8f993c-923e-5c4b-939b-76840e0bc356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278900} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dab0124-a6c5-5670-8ca1-31480fbfab00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0280bbe2-b195-5319-b55c-c6602327e942" :question/math-academy-id "q-163504" :question/problem "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x={y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-2\\le y\\le 0.$ Given that the area of the plate is $\\frac{8}{3},$ find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/a8/a8c57b12334a23542bae73ba1ac6848e69830b9663a1f83b6340f6812974f416.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}y\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y[x]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y({y}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (0-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0512041b-6e94-54d8-b2b1-bb423b0072e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278636} {:answer/id #uuid "09cede45-e652-52cd-9942-6adee37bbb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278685} {:answer/id #uuid "4d655bb0-fd8a-5ea2-8365-03a452f535d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279290} {:answer/id #uuid "63424d09-3dec-5e1f-a5a1-20d8c79e636f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279494} {:answer/id #uuid "af245288-f63d-53a8-9f42-a880a2a457d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278685} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e469acb-81dd-5f0e-a21a-1702a12e3e77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8339842-425a-5173-a9da-9623af429ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163563" :question/problem "![](images/74/74c26773d49442671fcf6526764215523ca0231a7889671330a7ac539f3402fc.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=2y$ and $x=3-y$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{6}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c9efcbae53bdd2620b0bf7009ce4f4f2a20a38f393cac008899621ec391d261.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\left(\\frac{y}{6}\\right)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\left(\\frac{y}{6}\\right)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\frac{y}{6}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4){[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}]}_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)[\\frac{(3-x)^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{48}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)(36-24x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{4}-36{x}^{3}+144{x}^{2}-240x+144\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{80}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{16}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}-3+8-10+6)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09464c3b-da90-5a6c-9243-df8532755011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278682} {:answer/id #uuid "2b09ecee-0c34-51af-8fea-3b0ee7b7a38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278972} {:answer/id #uuid "6c336ea9-2bb6-5cc9-877e-5612b94986a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279573} {:answer/id #uuid "d7259175-6cdf-5b65-ada1-892d4ebdb534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280492} {:answer/id #uuid "fe92c4b7-bd9d-531a-8bc2-20af5b7d15a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278972} :answer-field/id #uuid "63074783-de58-5b7b-b714-845dc52814d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a58e9d5-c3e2-5c20-9c17-c9c38a808e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-163568" :question/problem "![](images/55/55a781e535e1b469bc5da659eff8f82afd27a1e8bb92ccd36c2fce5a9dd49b07.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{4y}{3}$ and $x=4-\\frac{4y}{3}$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=2-x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{3}{4}x\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{3}{4}x},\n$$\n\n \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f332ebf554ac0e1b9e5099244aa50de0beef38c92efcba04e95e73abd21ecb6.png)\n\n    \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}(2-x)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}{(2-x)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}[{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}[y]_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}(3-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{3x}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{(2-x)^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(0-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28052605-ef85-5d61-b60d-35c652016958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278950} {:answer/id #uuid "30e535dc-d841-51b2-9346-6dd8f8151f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279019} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4a7fcf-e348-50c3-a268-aaa5fcf0d810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279712} {:answer/id #uuid "cd62681a-f336-57f7-9271-562c7877f73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280402} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cb850d-af3a-58fe-9a74-754886c3ca4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280620} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb299df-44bd-5725-bf66-0524bf139842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c58b6231-e237-5de8-8e27-d0ade4f5cce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163576" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 3=6.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(8-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8c3f43-d62e-5000-aa73-65488d3b0b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278693} {:answer/id #uuid "124a91eb-fdf5-5e37-9d33-f96d11c8d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278758} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbbd3c2-db9a-55d6-97d8-e5b35b4b7f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279281} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c9571-4624-5e8c-9ba9-3cb3cfed6da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279604} {:answer/id #uuid "d0235cb5-d166-53f6-8c2a-e428ad59a7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280423} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cfd582-049a-5c5a-85d6-152e3b8591a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c52b9e07-a1a1-59e3-9b59-f3ce7c050afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163578" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(1+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{10M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{10}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d652bb7-2c2a-59df-a30e-8a26cac188ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278716} {:answer/id #uuid "78b8c404-1b74-574e-aaff-1cac4f74dbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279668} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f6880e-c432-5d49-a344-3611d4439c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280038} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd4a4e6-f500-5149-b052-7702a8b7faf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280245} {:answer/id #uuid "d680d34e-7009-583e-b502-5b6983be161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278716} :answer-field/id #uuid "5840af3f-f19a-5638-befc-d037ec15bab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de217583-4705-57c2-81c7-b02676db7154" :question/math-academy-id "q-163582" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{7}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(7+9-\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{40}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{20}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034914f9-db0c-5329-8807-7f74c407fe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278609} {:answer/id #uuid "13b4c953-94ab-5677-a022-5e574a0b27fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278768} {:answer/id #uuid "14d90e82-4648-5f87-8c43-b205ff9308b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278779} {:answer/id #uuid "a936ae5c-1b7c-5010-9ae2-c9ed295af47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280092} {:answer/id #uuid "cc90c737-c547-5bd0-ac51-370920434287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278609} :answer-field/id #uuid "631ab36d-7ea9-523f-9c1e-50cda6b16350" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b6b9a33-7948-5bd9-8b27-53be2933fe8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163583" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f847a5551c0bdc5aea20673814ba6eddc10daca7661cca2b2db76c953672225.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=y-1$ and $x=7-y$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y-1}{9}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 7-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b86b9a595786e544159e08fef12952c88e1bf9f796fb4ae3089e877dc9119b3f.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-3|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{9}\\right)|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{9}\\right)(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}\\frac{y-1}{9}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}{[\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{18}]}_{x+1}^{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[\\frac{(6-x)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[\\frac{12(3-x)}{18}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{(x-3)^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002526ef-633f-58c1-b274-926fab0583fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278585} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4d382c-e48d-56b6-a202-5855a6a6237f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278828} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff14144-3b71-5ff7-9955-7cf660069ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279319} {:answer/id #uuid "6a73fd6a-b57c-5d90-82df-33f07d919538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279557} {:answer/id #uuid "d531cf97-31f6-5e67-b62f-84741a52f530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279319} :answer-field/id #uuid "d33324cc-c3c5-54e0-813a-456f2972fde2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f86bb819-7fea-5f54-bd5e-c24d69056f8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163584" :question/problem "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ is enclosed between the line $x=y$ and the line $x=1$ for $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cfa58d15b2a1f05aa4d4d088d44b44d6b3225084f627bc8078617d85d801957.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{y}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[{\\int }_{1}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[x]_{x=1}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}-y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-2)-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fe1d58-696d-5801-846b-4606facb1790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278634} {:answer/id #uuid "25ee5860-9bda-526e-bbdd-a5847511bb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278929} {:answer/id #uuid "47b6317d-7da1-5aab-9ed3-b9b41c973f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279234} {:answer/id #uuid "db28513a-71bd-51e9-8dc5-90eb817afd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280527} {:answer/id #uuid "fc36ee44-244b-55b3-878c-0cc2acd7ee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280527} :answer-field/id #uuid "af93d820-8c9e-5b5d-981c-2e952953e275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b5e602d-da1f-5649-9bd5-641c23ed74ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-163594" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186d7b54-9bd8-5d11-9531-f6a71e93a075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278807} {:answer/id #uuid "3123b7ca-d40c-5a3f-9f98-6e63415985f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279022} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac43d7e-86dc-5795-8a22-dbc422ca5a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279831} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdb7243-3730-53b1-89b2-148b6e07ef81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279987} {:answer/id #uuid "d708a39e-f2ac-5bfb-ada1-0904857be453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280490} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52c417e-4191-554a-91d8-c604aad807bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad1b682f-b157-528a-b0f7-855122eb923c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163600" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the line $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=\\sqrt{x}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12M(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =12M\\cdot \\frac{7}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{21M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f9db5c-e164-5da8-8198-fa6bb66ab5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278599} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4abe59-3a00-5413-9a13-f76e53a05f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278827} {:answer/id #uuid "27295108-8d28-5e44-bd4c-fd9f5f5c1dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278939} {:answer/id #uuid "2a222fa5-d9c5-5841-9ffa-a9c00a82be6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a04f08e-efba-5b0c-ad94-d016db6f7419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278827} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1724ad2-5623-51fe-a835-78c2ea54e619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd3dc29c-e508-5e62-812f-85ffd3f21aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-163609" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=3x,$ $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{45}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{M}{45}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{5}+9{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}[2{x}^{6}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}\\cdot (128-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03680dad-3bcf-5b29-abba-9ff2a1e3d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278612} {:answer/id #uuid "5e763cc2-b9bb-5930-8d85-81b17df9e5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279458} {:answer/id #uuid "7915aba8-9f3f-5ecd-a4e8-b8564ca12b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279672} {:answer/id #uuid "a75d52ce-0daf-59d4-ab59-5619ccbc76f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280074} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f7c1a9-3d3e-52db-86b1-4f9a0a3192a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280470} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fe166b-63e4-522d-a911-75e796a5f2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41aab4a9-697e-5db4-a3bf-1e7d24bbc956" :question/math-academy-id "q-163613" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the line $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{28M}{3}x{y}^{3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{28M}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}x{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{6}x{y}^{6}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{19}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{20}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7M}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3d51b7-d2a1-5f5c-a8bc-237dc2ebcf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278825} {:answer/id #uuid "3164c091-f58c-54eb-aad1-8e0c43c91c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279026} {:answer/id #uuid "5162df6b-de8d-5520-abbe-dd93a6e59038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279333} {:answer/id #uuid "c37d6da3-3c86-5674-9e53-305507ca31b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280312} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cc174a-d9b8-5495-89b1-4ebe90c0ea93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279026} :answer-field/id #uuid "6784d72f-0415-5f04-8903-764281948f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25ae87ad-380a-5a1b-8a33-907df3e00ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163614" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=20Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 20Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{7}-{x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}-\\frac{1}{11}{x}^{11}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{11}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{88} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5M}{22}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "01def8ae-3360-54d7-9e0a-d962e127a177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{1}{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281706} {:answer/id #uuid "05b526ee-e645-545d-9d38-eba3bf488f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281738} {:answer/id #uuid "06556ef1-f7c9-50f6-9c43-5e14baf287ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281744} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} {:answer/id #uuid "10eecc99-bb73-5a9d-991a-8f7cfa34640e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{7}{6},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281846} {:answer/id #uuid "124fd0b5-f2f2-5de6-8fc6-a1be5efe1917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281864} {:answer/id #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281872} {:answer/id #uuid "14e0f6aa-69fa-5a2a-a5a2-05425a8ac3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281883} {:answer/id #uuid "198b8e84-f193-531e-9d0d-8ec40e368ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{5},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281922} {:answer/id #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281934} {:answer/id #uuid "1cced431-2ae3-55b2-96db-c772a52758f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281950} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbb49e3-e06a-56a0-aa65-1f58ccd5aa9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281974} {:answer/id #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282054} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdbe9de-f24a-5608-bccc-61146baa9524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282115} {:answer/id #uuid "3245cd99-1cea-5944-af24-6439cf38eaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(25,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282142} {:answer/id #uuid "360bb19a-a68d-594f-ac04-f07e75deb3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5\\sq