:answer/id #uuid "fdddd448-e8ec-5b9c-96a1-a79e72942810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df692c1e-d1d5-5935-aa4f-58e66111c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438034} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4152bd-b840-569a-98b3-f7a6bbc4543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438035} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c4ce857-699d-5650-bbce-c9a33e567439" :answer-field/key "$\\tan ⁡(x+\\pi )=\\tan ⁡(x+n\\pi ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebcf390-41da-5232-afcf-76ca3cd35d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438036} {:answer/id #uuid "c855dbc6-62a7-535d-acf4-3e0422823e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438036} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9ad80d7-8062-5402-b3db-57b743c9edc8" :answer-field/key "The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b955e63-485d-5f8d-acaf-14b8077b8a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438038} {:answer/id #uuid "2efbdf03-740a-56f5-983f-e118131d7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438039} :answer-field/id #uuid "97cebb1e-e8e5-51e9-a969-4786401b7b78" :answer-field/key "$\\tan ⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\tan ⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4be11add-d875-5219-b83f-33d16bf37abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-28675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan ⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement $\\tan ⁡(x+\\pi )=\\tan ⁡(x+n\\pi ).$ is true. The function $y=\\tan ⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $\\tan ⁡(x+\\pi )=\\tan ⁡(x)=\\tan ⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $” is false. The function $y=\\tan ⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\tan ⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\tan ⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ is true. The function $y=\\tan ⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle \\tan ⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\tan ⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=\\tan ⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64fab536-011b-5495-af75-b22e97ee61ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295842} {:answer/id #uuid "87e9cce5-fd91-5d27-9de1-74ed14eca40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296000} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e90731-2073-5376-a7f3-d74585469490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296130} {:answer/id #uuid "d917947c-0694-5b63-b083-5660cef98161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2df703-693b-5e97-a3f0-fa4ca9c37fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295842} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae41aa8c-95fb-5956-b88c-4aa3ce7bb65c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97f191d4-6a4f-5a4f-8505-bbd4c324e609" :question/math-academy-id "q-29438" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53c921c9-46e0-5bef-95de-0f5b588ebca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295749} {:answer/id #uuid "85d07a4f-56af-578a-a679-6e632e293884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295983} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7e58fb-f78c-5c59-97d6-77d4f59a186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296016} {:answer/id #uuid "ab14ced1-375e-5455-909c-7a3ced2bd998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "ee73340c-4d12-5b82-9ec4-7452a202940f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} :answer-field/id #uuid "79362fdd-d341-5e89-bee7-6cb695806517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "670b4725-4f5f-5e5b-81ee-7bebf6dbdf85" :question/math-academy-id "q-29556" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4bc189-56e8-506b-afae-8c32583fd169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295423} {:answer/id #uuid "4809f8aa-d435-582b-a813-64757ceea05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295678} {:answer/id #uuid "598e8e48-0407-5a12-b4c7-8a90c76b22d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295784} {:answer/id #uuid "addb0989-293c-52f5-817b-1eac8428806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a2dd69-37ef-5e8e-92ea-d1b66a49d028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "380886ef-0fbf-54bc-9579-e4e21dd4f7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-29673" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1})^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bd7f17-ab32-5370-92e4-2c424857d4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295668} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa0b422-cd22-5a1d-9b1e-fc3b0fec4206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt[3]{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296113} {:answer/id #uuid "a8df53ed-dfab-5400-9e39-0f11c871eb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt[3]{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296156} {:answer/id #uuid "cef9375b-09ce-5562-9390-f6e2d91c89a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296354} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0dc99e-e1e1-515f-bf48-ed2f135dba0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\sqrt[3]{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296626} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8b2292-328d-5882-8739-6f5ed9a71d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d4f351a-f228-5a07-9853-4d4d86412f5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-29674" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{-\\frac{4}{3}+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{1}}) \\\\  & =3(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt[3]{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9d9c4e-2687-5a00-b777-4684cc0de27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295338} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffdef35-a30a-5f30-99b8-4a995eac8ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295632} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bb394a-fe2f-5d30-b8bc-9546189fbccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eee9c9-1fa3-56af-a61c-95a972738e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296285} {:answer/id #uuid "e36988ba-6cfe-5f34-b0a1-f31a0c88f51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17078ea-5afe-5ed2-9a45-a07ca7d4a2b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ee241ee-6c9b-581f-8e3e-00ecaf1e5967" :question/math-academy-id "q-29734" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{10}(\\frac{x}{5}-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{5}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}-5x)|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(5)^{2}}{2}-9(5)-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(10)^{2}}{10}-5(10)-\\frac{(5)^{2}}{10}+5(5)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{25}{2}-45]+[10-50-\\frac{25}{10}+25] \\\\  & =-50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04428be0-686e-5e54-8f4a-3feb9541a499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295286} {:answer/id #uuid "20f44553-3c6b-546a-bd20-ddf483dd3f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295451} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac3b511-b33d-502f-ac79-1ac7b15d0fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295937} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb81092-39a4-56f9-85a7-1c077bd22073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296610} {:answer/id #uuid "ff804d83-8855-560a-a7d7-c75ff6ee34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296634} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb764e43-8a01-5508-9ece-68c4eebbf1dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f0fe807-1fba-5baa-b6b7-6741269e860e" :question/math-academy-id "q-29739" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021675ce-f69e-5717-beee-62f1b55fe9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295272} {:answer/id #uuid "3e46952e-9cec-5cb5-af26-701cae6d5879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295626} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcb4361-9b61-51c7-9aaf-35ffbda245cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295875} {:answer/id #uuid "e3676b27-8ef7-5622-91a9-2bca7ef2dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296478} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2022c7-8746-55e0-8d32-73482f73ca68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296478} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c909a9-c1e8-56c3-a557-6fc2fdb9e1a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "598039da-8879-5b3c-ab71-e359f8b00731" :question/math-academy-id "q-29742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(\\frac{x}{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{3}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-x)|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3(3)^{2}}{2}-9(3)-\\frac{3(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(6)^{2}}{6}-6-\\frac{(3)^{2}}{6}+3] \\\\  & =[\\frac{27}{2}-27]+[6-6-\\frac{3}{2}+3] \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c985339-e8a9-5013-a7bc-4e49a05cf16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295337} {:answer/id #uuid "34818e95-d9d8-5f6e-89ad-2ac8fabf3e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295561} {:answer/id #uuid "d1461848-8a61-5745-9f24-b274a9ed68fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296367} {:answer/id #uuid "e5952e24-a6e0-5920-9fa8-3940a0d816f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296490} {:answer/id #uuid "edfd70da-f5e3-54ac-806b-e2aafc609d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295561} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9c7215c-4387-5294-8949-1c572219b286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "078e36f8-ac04-5dc6-872a-8aad0a6d9513" :question/math-academy-id "q-29747" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a44ce-4517-57c9-8784-5136e6d9c6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/3}+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295500} {:answer/id #uuid "2e55410d-693f-527e-b63c-efdd8027016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{4/3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295534} {:answer/id #uuid "91074ff5-3842-537b-b69c-20abfee3093d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296046} {:answer/id #uuid "ae484638-462d-55ee-b006-7e7f7e36be1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{x}^{7/3}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296183} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b38dca-ffa5-5659-aa91-113d55a9cced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ea97da-7b64-5d03-a1a8-9448f2925fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9a35970-b3a0-5bae-b863-30b3731d8d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-29885" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and get\n\n$$\n\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, using the power rule in the first term, and taking out the coefficient in the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{\\frac{2}{3}+1}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00628492-eb93-5878-979e-30036d304edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{10/3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295270} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5a70b3-ab83-532b-aa08-1862b689d752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{7/3}-3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295352} {:answer/id #uuid "37558070-eb2f-5f46-8242-8655de914964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2/3}-\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295579} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f41b7f-152b-52ff-9cf7-213f37faf4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{1/3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296236} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e531c-0ef6-56ff-a397-6ea185e85664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/3}-3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295270} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c825a2-113a-5331-8971-e3d904594718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c693357-ab6d-5b31-ab83-a3f2da57f6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-29886" :question/problem "Find the integral of $2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{7/3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7/3+1}}{(\\frac{7}{3}+1)}-\\frac{1}{3}\\ln ⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{10/3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176c0cc3-ba47-5a28-bd33-bd9278429e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295407} {:answer/id #uuid "2f103c39-3c15-50c6-8f4f-b6fcedebd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}+5\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295537} {:answer/id #uuid "58a0d4bf-d3f2-5150-8b06-787b98cd79e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295780} {:answer/id #uuid "73445bd7-7a1a-5461-a8e3-361ce1af59fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+2\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295898} {:answer/id #uuid "970ce0c0-c57d-5784-8a2c-ff97639ff009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+4\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295407} :answer-field/id #uuid "abead380-5729-56aa-a799-0bc9297616ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a09c1ef5-54ac-5fb8-be82-1b666b5326c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-29888" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abba1e8-07a5-5982-8931-920e2b54ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295324} {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "218a6eff-a0a2-5775-878d-fb334d854a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295454} {:answer/id #uuid "5203ee4a-c762-565e-a1e7-1c414c5d786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295738} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a5e23-083b-5d3f-bddc-50fd9a0aed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} :answer-field/id #uuid "65380cf5-c8c6-595a-9584-e99d721414af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-29915" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36e1b866-824a-58a7-814f-c1722a1f56f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295576} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a3d9c8-2289-5234-90ff-247924256a69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b7a2c77c-6afe-560a-891d-7d57e249dc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-300764" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-3{x}^{5}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}$, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-3{x}^{5})=-3{x}^{7}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df96c25d-2134-506e-b9c7-3da262188378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296452} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb32c4cd-4ac0-5990-b2b2-6600e61b4207" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c991e854-9c79-53ca-925f-63f0269438ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-301610" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-20{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}$, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-20{x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }((-20\\cdot \\frac{1}{5})\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-4$ causes $y=-4{x}^{2}$ to be a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})=-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e4bf07-38dc-56de-a3d2-ac26590ef035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295311} {:answer/id #uuid "90e243fd-b4a7-5bb3-b6f7-98ba2d532cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546569}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546569} :answer-field/id #uuid "653c8cb4-516c-5c19-b1da-afc41d292473" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58972e97-803a-5a72-ad54-417f9bf3a2a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-302235" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(8x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(2-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,4x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d9acb3-2434-56be-aa4c-a64c219f0e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296551} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f4d372-a091-54bb-b1c9-2dc96c45bdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9813cef-279a-5fd4-8e50-a7601280fe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-302240" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|8x-7|=-(8x-7).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{7}{x})}{(\\frac{-5x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8-\\frac{7}{x})}{(-5+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(8-0)}{-5+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{8}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7789aec2-7b22-5027-a75f-dd521b847f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295918} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb22bd5-f95e-5679-81de-a720f7b0dba1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f75bce11-338e-5172-9aa9-a70e3ac0a182" :question/math-academy-id "q-302242" :question/problem "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ecd9f88-9441-58a6-b4b8-89da5a9b36d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295441} :answer-field/id #uuid "363b88c1-f8ed-537e-a7bd-b476c6701812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58f94bd2-6052-5c0f-852e-d8d0ce5e7319" :question/math-academy-id "q-302689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2):$\n\n![](images/a7/a7e36a5c534a63199ea97985f1301db0c7675adc8fa0d17f33735c61eec9e871.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5112866-46c5-505a-9ff5-4366099f77af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296488} :answer-field/id #uuid "012a9099-f188-5701-8e17-e68c41ec5be9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1394c62-62a1-53db-8987-e127821ad30e" :question/math-academy-id "q-302690" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa401865-4ff6-5e1b-86d2-de457e9ecf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296594} :answer-field/id #uuid "42581d0c-e576-5381-80a4-648bc86b46ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "959dac60-c266-5e5e-bc27-a754d438b32c" :question/math-academy-id "q-302820" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2x}{x}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2+3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (2+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca28647c-48bc-5c48-9036-4d369fe85f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9415b214-7670-5ef9-82ed-74e5c40b1c9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34a796fc-8b16-58cc-88b1-3c76c0415be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-302827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe4953c-0983-5464-9362-45c9474d1210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360} :answer-field/id #uuid "91224f8e-576e-5980-8358-902f8e6b2203" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "251ee9eb-ffd1-5d74-9058-3b8ed977eb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-304068" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(-1,-1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-2}-4{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{3}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{4}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=1$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =1\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275b6be7-476f-5664-8d4c-fb7a3832606d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295483} {:answer/id #uuid "84433299-4f1f-5551-8f4d-432c1612da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295975} {:answer/id #uuid "93487fcb-1176-5aaf-b67c-c9275110a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055} {:answer/id #uuid "a49b7592-7b1d-5172-a1d6-a14ce8a1fe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} {:answer/id #uuid "bbae1e1c-4c20-53de-80d8-7f1ca34b8c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} :answer-field/id #uuid "021cad1d-dfec-5c2f-a8de-ca1eb6ba39db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90ca11f6-8893-5beb-98a2-24781da24a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0870d5ce-5a98-55d6-9968-27ab6e3e3620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295308} {:answer/id #uuid "3502b231-1827-509b-bb20-4f2d57f8745e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295563} {:answer/id #uuid "6549395d-c4f9-5441-9498-edec64a4c36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295844} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a9e775-4dc6-5530-b35c-c803baf7a784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296234} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a3701a-c1d1-5071-b381-b98795512559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296548} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfd5693-ddc4-5f7b-84e1-313b056e3370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f95328a8-4512-5fae-94f0-1be783b35629" :question/math-academy-id "q-30771" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ on the interval $[-2,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-{x}^{2})|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}-{1}^{2})-(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{4}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095165cd-68cc-5092-a541-017374c5c841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295315} {:answer/id #uuid "391a79ba-1514-5b3d-a0fb-45581aae517b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295593} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9c65aa-a057-5380-ab7e-6c41fff6e617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295789} {:answer/id #uuid "841eb217-bc22-5ed1-82f6-7e5a037937c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295974} {:answer/id #uuid "beb18a80-31c2-5374-bb9e-11f4d64b5d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3276f9cb-f55b-5959-8434-6aaa7aa3a121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72cc8fee-c912-578c-b6ad-a221076e3800" :question/math-academy-id "q-30776" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $g(x)=2{x}^{3}-x-1$ on the interval $[-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x)|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{2}-(-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(8-2+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-8) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c44b5185-3568-53b3-8d77-c69fc2155067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296303} :answer-field/id #uuid "26be343c-5dfd-5031-b3d8-1563d7798319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55838d61-7b48-5ee6-9d1d-e85a07554987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295760} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48c190e-f08c-555e-ba42-c4bbbf078a30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac03aacc-0b7e-5a7f-8554-ee3b669a879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-308823" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cae6982-03b7-597e-93e4-369fa68c8f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296102} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26d719b-6dbc-535a-8df8-4e273868c85f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "395be2fc-ff05-5c2f-8d61-c5ab0d5aca86" :question/math-academy-id "q-308854" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=3{x}^{e}+\\ln ⁡x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{e}+\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =3e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =3e+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a21d7c1-657b-5c7c-bc8d-a5a78f0a5e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}3}-4+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295600} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fc67ac-4879-57d5-8bd2-d439a30262bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8688ee43-a87d-5757-aeae-1697946d83af" :question/math-academy-id "q-308866" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=2{\\log }_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x},$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{\\log }_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{1}+5{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4+5e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5aa548-8e4a-5a2d-97f6-5c499bd890a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786} {:answer/id #uuid "7dde1994-c71e-5ef1-be9b-f95aa1f829ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295953} {:answer/id #uuid "86464462-6cf6-5c92-b8e0-b635825871a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295989} {:answer/id #uuid "e3dcad0b-7433-5fda-bfa5-de31b530e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296482} {:answer/id #uuid "f56f3fda-93e0-5eb4-af15-a675894133b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295786} :answer-field/id #uuid "29249e25-9c6c-58de-9fd8-1d3099e0c4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87e893b1-43cc-5695-982d-b173b104605b" :question/math-academy-id "q-30893" :question/problem "Within the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ determine the average value of $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(0+(-1))-(0+1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc7422a-6560-5142-961f-48d22cd38ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "220+\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295348} {:answer/id #uuid "2c04d99b-a86b-5d68-b6cf-6a1d89b39f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295523} {:answer/id #uuid "9673e7a1-5472-5c11-b9c9-7778a76c10d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "210-\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296069} {:answer/id #uuid "fadab96a-3e8b-5396-9d82-c76e16597282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296597} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a7330-8974-56a8-8299-e0f9004a7e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "230-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295523} :answer-field/id #uuid "75acc2e4-2a05-516c-974d-72e69e3612d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c3d43bb3-dee9-539e-921d-9e390abc8b05" :question/math-academy-id "q-30903" :question/problem "A biologist found that the population $P$ of some bacteria in a petri dish can be modeled by the function $P(t)=200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t),$ where $t$ is the number of hours after $12$ noon. Find the average number of bacteria during the period from $12$ noon to $4$ p.m." :question/worked-solution "Since the function is valid starting from $12$ P.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,4].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}P(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-0}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}(200t-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\,t}{4}\\right))|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(200\\cdot 4-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 4}{4}\\right))-(200\\cdot 0-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{4}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))+\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[800+\\frac{32}{\\pi }+\\frac{32}{\\pi }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(800+\\frac{64}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68846e-8392-5a94-a3aa-2686ed8da072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295320} {:answer/id #uuid "2b89a108-9830-5765-ab37-236b6c7a436c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{75}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295517} {:answer/id #uuid "567f2736-63ee-5f2a-b291-57227aca1813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295771} {:answer/id #uuid "98a360bd-7dc7-5172-9965-33a5e01d2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{75}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296086} {:answer/id #uuid "b68c6e0c-5bad-5bf2-bfb7-247965fb865b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97a2636-5f46-5511-ac10-6484a8f5f52e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38f9635e-72c4-5968-9945-fe2ceda9691e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30922" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-2x,$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({4}^{-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{15}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{75}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79239b6d-f719-5c75-9053-bf2a10d2c4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295928} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d81211-9ca6-5ed2-a5dc-1f113f908f71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ca192f94-8d93-544f-8a75-568decc353d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-310171" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{(4)}(x)$ for $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n${g}^{(4)}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the fourth derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-6{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6eb30f-5bfc-5e1d-a01c-0f6eb7e00e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295719} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8643e81-9ec6-59f8-9881-f0c74f9799e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c450049-c1ac-524d-8d79-0a2c2ad034fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-310180" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is \n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd0cb33b-8767-54e7-a818-0bec60f8663e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296341} :answer-field/id #uuid "71661f85-5585-5ca0-ad3a-dc9dadf683ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c578341-fd5b-5ea9-b094-029b8faa3d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-312258" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\right)}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "585614dd-9ea9-5784-93b4-6914d0f259e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡4}+\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} {:answer/id #uuid "81771957-0036-5c33-bdc4-df04751e4fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡4}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295963} {:answer/id #uuid "8598c747-3949-5f9e-8cb4-3d148fb0cdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+\\frac{x}{\\ln ⁡4}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295982} {:answer/id #uuid "aed107ef-e155-5c3a-86cd-e0c4ddc3690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡|x|}+4\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296189} {:answer/id #uuid "b9377cb5-4f12-5d35-bfa2-ab4190b617d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{4x}}{\\ln ⁡2}+2\\ln ⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} :answer-field/id #uuid "33473edb-2246-524d-982e-ed6605f64e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8203576d-9480-5aa7-b8ae-908a8c1708f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31256" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡4}+\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d969876-ce58-5b6e-931a-b010d05fb7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295345} {:answer/id #uuid "37f8ec8e-76e4-586a-b5e7-53e23a676242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295580} {:answer/id #uuid "7320c361-0f9b-52af-9157-5e72a8dfb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295897} {:answer/id #uuid "75cc333c-3afa-5571-8852-527c5dd6eb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295908} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc94bed-ac7b-52a1-96a0-8a931f378d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296453} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f27437-d273-5e98-a4e2-e6a7599a3976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "076a6ee5-4c7d-5342-8736-e5be0b0283bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-31257" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}+4x+3]^{′}}{[2x+3]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f6ba14-67bb-52a5-83f3-90748a808eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{10y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295413} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea47b69-ba23-5df0-89cb-9a0a8a5b3195" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b5b23fa-be02-551f-968d-c9191c07be80" :question/math-academy-id "q-312780" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b269001ff5a3a1a1d86125157ee2f5b77d89ebcf0041f8d403dbc931b796c5e8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(5,7).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$ and $y=7$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 49 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{49}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{49}{5}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{49}{5}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{49}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{49}{10y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6010001e-bb4d-502d-b628-0344b092ec96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295816} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceacdc1d-9a74-534d-a6f9-37fab6581a93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90c4f5cf-db31-54d5-9bff-47ee6cd28950" :question/math-academy-id "q-313364" :question/problem "The volume of a right square pyramid is increasing. Given that its height $h$ is always twice the length of a base, find the rate of change of the volume of the pyramid with respect to its base length $s.$\n\n*Hint*: The volume of a right square pyramid is $V=\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h.$ \n\nThe rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to $s$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We are given that $h=2s,$ so we can express the volume of the pyramid $V$ as a function of the base edge length $s$ by substituting $h=2s$ in the formula of the volume. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}(2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{d}{ds}\\,(\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{d}{ds}\\,({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to its base length $s$ is $2{s}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bedcd8f-d90b-5061-91ac-f10991c0035c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2x\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522} :answer-field/id #uuid "441576ee-9428-5996-8d80-7db6b8216c3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a8e96d3e-9fca-5093-8b9b-5bb4483b8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-313371" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x{e}^{2x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the denominator $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)({e}^{2x})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a9ec9-6bfa-51c3-b293-a2e6c7634c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295302} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cac3-e49f-55a4-a517-b40d5927cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "748fff53-6b97-5ddb-a2b7-7573ce8fffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295904} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b0310-1b57-58cd-bbcf-bbf81a785509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} :answer-field/id #uuid "67494433-5316-509e-bbfc-ae0d8a99e4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d62ded4-2ee6-52b4-bc69-a4642638d154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295710} {:answer/id #uuid "81a7a75b-3985-56bc-ad9c-0316c2348f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a357d65-23c5-58be-868e-2ac0c3d388e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296090} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c28805-4880-53a7-a205-3cbba072dd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296298} {:answer/id #uuid "f605d237-0166-587c-b104-dc53781dd11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295710} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aaa5d76-a174-5c5f-afc2-1ec32957d4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab907b07-4062-5dae-90ac-fc279f3c0e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-31340" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{csc}⁡u,\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-\\pi /2} & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x){|}_{x=-\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e0695-743c-53ea-a0bf-08a7ae6a7e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295400} {:answer/id #uuid "257afa08-d4dd-5c05-84fd-e23c68f3fa03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295473} {:answer/id #uuid "47ef2d54-50f3-5c11-adc5-2e9c8c3f349c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295677} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9bbd8f-12ba-5756-ab8b-55bc35e01784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295803} {:answer/id #uuid "f24f2c93-19bb-5f2c-bb5d-345956dd2577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295803} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb835fc8-dd55-5bbb-9fd4-1f86940f3061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0865b352-f43e-56b9-8d78-8c3ca9422e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31363" :question/problem "For the curve $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,\n$$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},\n$$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db558ad2-5cea-5652-8d34-cc67a45effd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296426} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2e960b-ca24-52de-9ab6-07dcbceaaebf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd13c00d-5dc7-5ccb-a6d3-56bdaaf16d42" :question/math-academy-id "q-313637" :question/problem "Given that $r=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f276cdd-ecf5-5a99-899d-51cd15626a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295357} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5576b47-0128-5872-8fac-e50d447fb770" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f973c2da-4afc-57bc-bbf9-262e7639f470" :question/math-academy-id "q-313938" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-5,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y}{9}\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2(-5)}{9}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2(4)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{120}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247f5c61-8dea-5c72-9001-ec5d7b70591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295467} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9ec191-f4bb-5c86-8e6c-08aee654144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\log }_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295697} {:answer/id #uuid "8c832d15-3e5e-5c99-9339-9f4b3d100902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x{\\log }_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296017} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4de440-cd8e-5ea6-9b03-b62c0fe90378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296031} {:answer/id #uuid "d47dd115-efcf-5c72-a9de-08221fb2d4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\log }_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295697} :answer-field/id #uuid "34099187-cafa-5bea-a9c2-38e91ea94b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "baa80704-3dfa-5ff9-bf20-e404177dc892" :question/math-academy-id "q-31400" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\log }_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18224ab4-1997-54b1-9d84-46c76f50925a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/74/749d466c80d05d054b8d661b6c3405c92f904df65ca7c3442746d19f73394834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295409} {:answer/id #uuid "8b94732d-431a-566b-85ac-520a42fe1feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/62/6212d04b1004e0110d7aff817f03bab0b98f74dd5e334fe6607475ed13fe3fb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296010} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e4b30-9549-530f-b9ff-5fc3fbd7b8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266} {:answer/id #uuid "d546bbf6-c8b7-53f3-959d-73a13e4ee107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296389} {:answer/id #uuid "df1d268b-fd3c-5617-8309-bc2ad0fab779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/44/4441bcb0d11698cff76c8e74c7c34a8109523a581eb5810c388e9f17c09e8176.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266} :answer-field/id #uuid "7108cc44-6ba9-5062-9231-4d0b0008bcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbe47114-407d-5b49-92a6-a4e3739775b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31416" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ee9cbc-5359-51b4-95f6-a26ecb82285b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e888fe9d-38cc-5b84-a5fc-0958eb384574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17dd452c-1a10-54e1-9250-6e49f35c45ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-314246" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5x+1}{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5x+1}{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[5x+1]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5}{(2x+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18309ac9-f513-556f-b337-ba3a246cb6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295410} {:answer/id #uuid "54af7204-8617-58c0-b99c-62cceb31ce44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295756} {:answer/id #uuid "73a3360c-ea84-5190-b608-15da107f4283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295900} {:answer/id #uuid "8ead18ad-43a0-5380-bd6e-cfbd3694845f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296027} {:answer/id #uuid "d796e37b-e7df-5f38-be96-9b9936b222a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295410} :answer-field/id #uuid "06900e27-2400-5334-ba59-9c36ba5a30cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96033acd-67cd-543b-8482-d4f4d5a08b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-3146" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6b/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e30d62-ad76-58fe-b63e-becec8699503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296404} {:answer/id #uuid "25319bb3-e024-45f3-b2a4-7560c2661f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\left(x^2+5\\right)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186748140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296404} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd994f66-bf62-5f46-b267-9ec2a9a54874" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78ab970d-f949-563c-b0af-4172efd56054" :question/math-academy-id "q-314738" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(2{x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{({x}^{2}+5)(6{x}^{2})}{1+4{x}^{6}}-2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76aca1b6-5661-51da-99d2-f8c22f5b96ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295912} :answer-field/id #uuid "317eccc7-ddd1-5b16-9d57-f970d8ad8624" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b48b719-9f12-5c32-8d2e-c9f7b2979bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-314762" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\right){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d116e5-bda3-5bb6-9073-4a756892bfc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295493} :answer-field/id #uuid "6116ff27-77f4-5dbc-b0a0-eb44a9d20d1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "69493ee6-a225-5106-b233-5e8265954b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-314900" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) & ≈L\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7a25c4-3d33-50f2-a790-4a274c690727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296191} :answer-field/id #uuid "373f48fb-86c0-545d-9d87-158ca081e5f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6af32da1-8814-5eb1-b187-e72ef6eddd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295863} :answer-field/id #uuid "8136e1a9-a97e-5e0a-8c82-ab6608d7dd7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9d543bb-496c-551f-8dd1-0746724816e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-315550" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-7){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d5b2fa6-381a-5d5d-bef8-7d79886dce3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6j}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295872} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7de079-4ed6-5563-b506-283566b45488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ed8b4ad4-c2df-5a80-8184-0f46540123d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-315701" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=2$ and $b=5,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6j}{n}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b49b220e-9fd5-51d8-96f0-d25d920276ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{j}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296215} :answer-field/id #uuid "67782cc0-027b-537d-a830-7413951d2e11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1487daa-866b-50a7-afd5-9a9671f89a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-315717" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're given that there are  $n=10$ subintervals.  The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$  so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{10}=\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{10-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}f\\left(\\frac{2j-4}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{((-2+\\frac{j}{2})+2)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{\\left(\\frac{j}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc9e862a-fda4-556e-b421-592a2ee2c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296438} :answer-field/id #uuid "a45999b9-6b7f-5cd4-bc67-53f760c95ae0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ce341729-3a59-5798-9fad-f886c34df302" :question/math-academy-id "q-315735" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t+\\frac{4}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+\\frac{4}{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =2-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =ma(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db4917aa-c356-5abe-b9a4-e3639a57ac70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296425} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c244552-fc1a-57a0-8d9d-1087222040b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d16d840-e772-54e8-bd9e-bcce6a940ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-315830" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=9{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815b59ac-66aa-50b6-8f74-d5f9c6d253c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295962} :answer-field/id #uuid "619e4438-91be-5a7e-9594-a0155f226327" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41aa5101-c6e0-5e2e-af55-4009a409fb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-315985" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2\\cdot {3}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43669ea8-4439-5c52-932e-09963c9aed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295646} {:answer/id #uuid "68ac4fcc-e9cd-56f7-9ba5-512586a67a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295855} {:answer/id #uuid "90f6ca85-5f26-52db-bbfc-d1f9279260bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296045} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e455bc-7676-59e0-8421-fc92303b6dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296199} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c847f9-8cd7-5aad-ae9b-9ef397a14e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296549} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe39cd51-4508-5a79-ba59-d1e9f1443e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e847ac9b-6147-5e36-9747-365456716188" :question/math-academy-id "q-31600" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x+2]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{x}{x+2})}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)+\\frac{-1}{-1+2})}{2(-1)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c96cf270-030f-5409-91ca-ac1d0462ae23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6}-7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc597fc-6f39-5ed9-a51b-361613150160" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7f1d1b78-a91f-5b2c-9c58-c656ac210846" :question/math-academy-id "q-316004" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(({e}^{2})^{3}-{1}^{3})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{6}-1)-(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d295d5b-60d1-5782-8664-c37de7a04be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295952} {:answer/id #uuid "86d84b87-e847-5627-b2c8-7431aca89887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295991} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0c482e-168d-5a8f-b036-192f6be5273d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296094} {:answer/id #uuid "d0586360-51f6-5b81-b5f1-689bad305a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296362} {:answer/id #uuid "ff29c031-3d6e-55e3-8917-ccff8fee18db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295991} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f70dc6-bebf-5d3e-8c79-3942528669e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4cee0a4-4d54-5d5b-88b7-af1da57b4e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-31602" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{-2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b020db61-bc7d-550d-af95-405cb768e4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296195} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ef4377-5327-5e85-be0e-3645dd1642f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96fe6acf-39fc-5928-b3e7-6b1c83c18a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-316022" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5432ed129d85a3718f8e5ebe0cca141731c366d1b32e8e72d0916c60c34de2c.png)\n\nA region in the $x$-$y$ plane is bounded by the curve $y={\\sec }^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}$, and the $x$-axis between $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, as shown above. \nFind the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({\\sec }^{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sec }^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)){|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi /3}{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53539640-bf8b-506e-bdc0-a98614bbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f62b30-0d7e-5226-bbfa-f9da2b0d309c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e8278e6-1f98-55c4-88ff-9d0d17e17bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-316024" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d282e4afd0a66dd79fecf474da9f618a5970197943044d24208f86c4c43781e6.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-(\\arcsin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\arcsin \\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd6e52a-b3a5-5dab-aaaf-60326f6f6578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295705}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295705} :answer-field/id #uuid "0209d7e8-81e1-518b-a4ef-79ca16ee088f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b90bcf5-c930-580a-b51e-8cb14af68bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-316137" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3aa68477e9e8f3bc688981cbd227e21af798364b8c0334ee9a22163b400b4064.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{a}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{4} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x-1)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm {9}^{1/4}\\,=\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ is $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSince $1-\\sqrt{3}\\in [-2,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1-\\sqrt{3}$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=1-\\sqrt{3}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{1-\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3ff6ca-468d-56ec-ab0d-3b805e5ffdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296100} :answer-field/id #uuid "39395d66-c8e2-5085-afae-809ec5288689" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd6c73c9-bd3c-53be-accb-e4b86f6cd635" :question/math-academy-id "q-316226" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a7a32f856d63403d7125f1c6bb9acc9a5fcd182c2f7fbd3f3ebdfa42f9c9c2c.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-8$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ (shown above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-8\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-8(2-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{56}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caef1662-b802-571e-94e7-2100591edec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180+0.5{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296330} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a317d8-cf34-5d96-bff6-63043ecb3677" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8f85d18-c70d-5c3e-acb8-1dd6b68eae84" :question/math-academy-id "q-316254" :question/problem "In $2005$, a group of scientists started tracking the population of sea turtles in a coastal sanctuary. The initial population was $180,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $4+1.5t$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2005.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of sea turtles in the sanctuary at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)=\\underbrace{4+1.5t}_{\\text{population increase}}-\\underbrace{(1+0.5t)}_{\\text{population decrease}}=t+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of turtles $T$ years after $2005.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(t+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =180+\\frac{1}{2}{T}^{2}+3T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=180+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f22f82-3f4c-56a7-8fca-b1699296b03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296486} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ebfc2e-32f9-5714-a71a-21c80776c9ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e5a2c9b-15eb-580e-9b48-bc6a0c515d66" :question/math-academy-id "q-316309" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c69b5a43e445861a728fcd8faed9e89b3bcb61141ad35d99e60f2a43983244d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-2,9],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments and the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(5,0)$ with radius $3.$ \n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[0,8].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,8].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8-0}\\cdot (-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe850c1f-321e-5276-88cb-7d482781d345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}({e}^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296622} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca9f592-ba28-5b85-9260-3e1587d0b3a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "20bda5cd-e0bb-515d-91ed-20b569d621cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-316401" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({u}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({e}^{x})^{3})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4480e35a-317e-5f13-9d79-0ea6484141a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{33}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d77290b-906d-5536-ba0a-f485d7077eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873e6730-fc19-5919-afbd-4b7c9bcc1c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295995} :answer-field/id #uuid "6268d035-7cba-53de-93d8-bda0ed33d42b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c38fe81-9d94-5607-850a-3f19bd20f73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295800} :answer-field/id #uuid "865b6673-30fd-56dd-8bab-d5785581b3e9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11a02f05-1ba9-5780-8f1e-0d85159aa8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-316469" :question/problem "Let $c=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d=⟨$  \n\n{{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}},\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(0)+(-2)(5)+(1)(-1)}{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{0-10-1}{9+4+1}\\,⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{14}\\,⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{33}{14},\\frac{11}{7},-\\frac{11}{14}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n`"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24552f75-9740-5a26-aba3-f5d20cd614af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295465} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0c02c0-f2a5-5ce2-a4b7-a3fc16d8bf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} {:answer/id #uuid "a94fb410-5e70-5609-8697-f14dd7e6d10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296158} {:answer/id #uuid "dbef18fd-ac58-5011-9cde-83e458a1f8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296434} {:answer/id #uuid "fae167b2-b7ab-5d81-ab78-94771500a109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8c845e-6c52-5fec-abb7-6de3f19ea8df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "17401c5c-84e7-5182-8398-ec9e279cae2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31653" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1},\\,{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2t,3{t}^{2}-12t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ 3t(t-4)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162d0fe8-5764-59eb-ae4e-f4b56e3ba0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295399} {:answer/id #uuid "29a898bb-c5fe-5580-a94c-996d7dc232d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295504} {:answer/id #uuid "36f64579-754d-5320-9883-bbb57c575a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295578} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6906c6-f755-5212-84e7-7f253891c01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296344} {:answer/id #uuid "ef67fbd0-807e-5f8b-bd7a-7bc080e590c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295578} :answer-field/id #uuid "7502c6d3-cfd3-5cfc-950d-00aa2235e4b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82b265db-264e-5810-9acb-572bc78fdb47" :question/math-academy-id "q-31654" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}+6t-3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,4t+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),2(2t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-1)(t+1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 2(2t+3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is no value of $t$ that satisfies both equations, so there are no stationary points."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c47da02-0de3-57ba-80b0-c125c05b20e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295525} {:answer/id #uuid "4c228dda-3c12-503a-ae76-b6dbd65e3cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295701} {:answer/id #uuid "710ae787-1902-5217-9c26-724daf023aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2d8a19-1869-5d11-b6b2-5d7224310807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{33\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296333} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9d3052-96a7-52e6-88ad-4c8dcf860d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0467277-3365-50ac-9fc9-dd5fec40a456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7168c66b-e063-53c6-92aa-cf6372184dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-31669" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{\\log }_{5}⁡(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={\\log }_{5}⁡(5x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot 5=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{\\log }_{5}⁡(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\log }_{5}⁡(5x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb33962-3352-5442-a000-56fc39f28fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296103} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e626c24-efa4-5da1-8a23-1f42836548a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24e02d71-25dc-54e7-a4b0-aeb294c81fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-316692" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=2-{y}^{8}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-{y}^{8})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1}\\,-\\,{\\frac{{y}^{9}}{9}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1-0)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{9}-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4416b63a-3930-5bfe-a022-fd8c0a465bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295652} {:answer/id #uuid "56187eba-a7c6-563e-93b9-2e918239ee15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{52}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295766} {:answer/id #uuid "79e2da52-d315-5430-adee-2be43a0dfc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295933} {:answer/id #uuid "ddafeec2-d7ad-5757-98e0-b57d2b45ae6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296445} {:answer/id #uuid "de4459bb-3eaa-5d8f-8eaa-994693b5cdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295652} :answer-field/id #uuid "c556763d-7ccc-52b8-868e-fdb90ad960f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d854fcf1-769c-563b-98b9-fcdfe43a4f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-31671" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨6t+1,-3{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6,-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨-6,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 36} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2578a5b6-a0d6-59ab-9d8f-ea3ab2f04b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{625}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295472} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb3c9bf-3805-5408-a87b-98d04e97792e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c89117c1-0d6b-5b6e-8bf1-11d2608dd39c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316714" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-10y+25) & =0 \\\\ y(y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/59/593d1168ce7328aa93edd61fcabc116451fcda8ef24faeac0949fa0a8e686b51.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=5.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\,({y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-10{\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+25{\\int }_{0}^{5}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}|}_{0}^{5}-{\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{3}|}_{0}^{5}+{\\frac{25}{2}{y}^{2}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4}-0)-\\frac{10}{3}({5}^{3}-0)+\\frac{25}{2}({5}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{1250}{3}+\\frac{625}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb05d088-db32-5e5b-9607-44c554a02e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296331} :answer-field/id #uuid "2845ade6-f642-5524-b12f-1c43c8351f1e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18765ce6-ff83-5855-a4da-01b8dc7b88cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c1459c-a7d1-54e5-8c4e-d4b4317ed20c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8116279a-006f-5701-aca9-d9c79bb44b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-316736" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c3a45ac200d1f65c326354297e6e03b5347b4d4ec3fbb2a99bb7cde98376db8.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}$ (plotted above) is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3x}\\cdot 3)x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$ is the domain of $g$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for $x>0,$ the denominator is positive; thus this reduces to\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)<0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{e}{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, this critical point is a maximum. The slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}x & (0,\\frac{e}{3}) & \\frac{e}{3} & (\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ) \\\\ \\hline g & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ \\hline {g}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{array}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aec00a7-04b6-5c70-8ef8-74977de85a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295790} {:answer/id #uuid "60becdb5-5d79-55bb-b11b-9fc35ff0ccff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295821} {:answer/id #uuid "b48e9582-a8f6-51d4-ad24-cb3e0c9f5fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296214} {:answer/id #uuid "b729eebe-6f31-556b-ba69-e4b806683bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296228} {:answer/id #uuid "e04c70c6-92ee-59a5-8f05-07d633d2d45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295821} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b2356a-d65a-5a0b-8ae5-a16a6569a4d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac3c1af1-d531-5585-83c9-f48f03ae136d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31674" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,6{t}^{2}-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,\\,12t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨2(0)+2,12(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨2,0⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe8cc4d-79b0-54f4-a4b4-402238d2f931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296254} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf316c9-9c3a-5ad3-a098-ccbd0d67af6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1d1ee30-1190-5b35-acf5-2df3a55be76b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316742" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a988c243bcf4b82cd05b1ed0292e0595141dbe37a470abc34b183f0ce184fe3f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2 \\\\ y+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8} \\\\ (y+2)^{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =(y+2)^{3}-8 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-2}^{0}\\,+\\,6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+12\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{16}{4})+6(0-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right))+12(0-\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =-4+16-24 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-12|=12\\,.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "3f390c3f-3d8a-5215-902a-2111e1cb9064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297800} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb08d3e-745f-5d5e-a6f0-813f37e8f5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298028} {:answer/id #uuid "9d01e28b-2bec-5906-ac6d-fdf1bdf1ffed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298241} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d799e0-e3ef-5bec-9aa6-900bcf220160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87639db-d7a5-514f-8198-b0f0f6675caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298354} :answer-field/id #uuid "324a496e-6e5e-5c57-ac60-3784be903611" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41766091-90d5-5cdc-b80d-0dbd1cf16493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297815} :answer-field/id #uuid "67a7c24d-4f16-5de6-85a3-3b9fbdbb5d5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64c6a767-31e7-5aae-8b73-f518b85f2ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297983} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52d2484-92a8-5937-bf47-dfe47bbdff77" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7cb2167-c20d-5eb9-8b34-9fba8390a665" :question/math-academy-id "q-317653" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨2,1,-3⟩$ and $d=⟨4,-2,1⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{d}\\,c=⟨$  \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{d\\cdot d}\\,d \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}\\,d \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,d \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2-3}{16+4+1}\\,⟨4,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{21}\\,⟨4,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟨4,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\,\\frac{4}{7},-\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c878d605-5860-58df-a4db-831299b47352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298427} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc9c03c-0f8d-5754-add4-5f23386fd70a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c15c0c7-a327-502e-80d0-bd4f96a13b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297788} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf2837b-e0b0-5ce2-bd2c-2ddbe3b15ed7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f64f65-1c0a-5906-bc9c-22a0a410ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297767} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f9320f-b73b-5f8e-8244-ae01349f9e1b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad7a52e5-666f-5bc0-be39-af6199a52182" :question/math-academy-id "q-317654" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{6}⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\,5,-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d61422-1475-5621-ad7d-a849aaca114f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298546} :answer-field/id #uuid "0984c5b8-6bfe-56e8-9582-60026aa3bd96" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9f7fec6-5215-539e-8f83-5634b3c4076a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298431} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e38d24f-0d2e-5b55-b975-90a7ef2d3a53" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298686} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24aadbe1-8097-598a-9d4b-d9e79efcd1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297709}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297709} :answer-field/id #uuid "9689e94a-473b-55d0-80de-83ad1a852f62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0810600b-ad06-5852-ade5-064bb8170efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-317655" :question/problem "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{s}\\,r=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{s\\cdot s}s \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}s \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}s \\\\  & =\\frac{-17}{5}⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\,-\\frac{17}{5},0,\\frac{34}{5}\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93889542-cdb7-5205-b40e-5de012a35041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298189} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7d00bc-c7b8-56f2-a2ed-8537aea7acf0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a47dbd1-9413-50ea-9bd4-8fbefb0c711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297605} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce79871-b6bd-5948-884a-1ece9351e7e8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81df3b98-83b4-583c-96b0-c8ced3f78bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298112} :answer-field/id #uuid "d96c6a63-c019-5f74-921c-56bb4cbb21bd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "785e36b9-0bdd-5b31-a4f6-b44338195a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-317656" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $s=⟨5,1,-4⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $s$ onto $r.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{r}\\,s=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{r}\\,s & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{r\\cdot r}r \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{{r}_{x}^{2}+{r}_{y}^{2}+{r}_{z}^{2}}r \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-4)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}}r \\\\  & =\\frac{10-1-12}{4+1+9}⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{14}⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\,-\\frac{3}{7},\\frac{3}{14},-\\frac{9}{14}\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406c9253-8200-5ad3-8d4b-33726202a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297809} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d8666c5-0963-50a4-bc97-4b5c38053501" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df406d7-a2cd-566a-9e5c-dbf376476be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297681} :answer-field/id #uuid "d08d1232-d14d-502d-8d1a-ea40bec46176" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ad9205-623c-5b5c-8b1b-874ef60380c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298055} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9147c73-f105-5d8b-83ed-2692f2208968" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1656678a-52ed-5990-af8d-fd8bfe8d48c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-317658" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-2,1,5⟩$ and $d=⟨4,0,-3⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot (-3)}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0-15}{4+1+25}⟨-2,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{-23}{30}⟨-2,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\,\\frac{23}{15},-\\frac{23}{30},-\\frac{23}{6}\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b686c0-2736-54b5-8b3c-b487c19e9d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297705}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297705} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b94c1e-bef4-535d-be22-4916491f4a9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea9202ac-8801-52e2-86b4-596e2b355837" :question/math-academy-id "q-317978" :question/problem "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5b5855-e173-5cc0-be78-e8c7ed94613e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6dd1a867-f879-56d0-b4c0-d0297b5035d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319806" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=(x+2)\\cos ⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=\\cos ⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sin ⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-\\sin ⁡x)+\\cos ⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)\\sin ⁡x+\\cos ⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)\\sin ⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\cos ⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609f7b5f-824f-5147-9406-7af9613b62e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297963} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a79ff4-55ea-5211-91a9-33767972b15d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53380aeb-e3ec-5770-a193-a3eec01995c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-320550" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-5⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,6⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75dc16f3-b24c-59e8-aac9-c1ea4729e37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066} :answer-field/id #uuid "70277a6d-54df-5d86-932e-e42ed6a7a735" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6597267d-a0da-5d5e-b28e-9dd4c1c2688b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320553" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨-\\frac{4}{3},7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,-9⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8f4c87-04b9-5012-91a0-2fa1e73539ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298449} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10035ab-a533-5291-9723-0bc6c8afa230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5584afb0-f812-5610-86a2-9366973652b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2071233-48c1-53b8-9e87-ce0c7ce6bb94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "80110151-a320-56a4-96e5-9888e94599a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-320926" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{t}{-2t+1},\\,t\\ne -3,\\,\\,\\,t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+3}\\,⇒\\,t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=-3+\\frac{1}{x}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(-2\\,(-3+\\frac{1}{x})+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{2}{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3+\\frac{1}{x})}{(7-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x+1}{\\cancel{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x}}{7x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+3}$ has range $x\\ne 0,$ we must have \n$x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\text{ }0.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1-3x}{7x-2},\\,x\\ne \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b761f73-9699-5009-890b-c604bb787aa6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb33573b-f770-5250-8223-d49dca5de7e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce51648f-7c56-5fd1-a391-8983dd27aa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "276bf0ec-7469-5b52-b357-e7d5b9732c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300149} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1e6ccc-be4c-5152-8bd2-b482ccf07e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300683} {:answer/id #uuid "b1155a51-ab6a-5d93-bda9-1de280b175a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300846} {:answer/id #uuid "dac2c371-fb64-5d55-bd04-6c42351b6a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859cacd7-5021-5714-8b7c-c7c18ad73a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300632} :answer-field/id #uuid "774bc989-caf7-5a2a-bfab-9ca69ee4fb27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5e99420-7491-5062-87bb-ee0a63b9ee4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300798} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba3c015-b759-53fb-9917-fb16a142f241" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "309f8df2-ff1b-5330-aaa7-587c18b468d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321306" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =14(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{3}-7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198804bf-af9a-5fc5-aa0a-80dfba4c5066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300073} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ddd749-cb3f-55d1-9232-9e4165ddb9b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05d2be4-10af-58b6-a6b7-5ce48f39fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301001} :answer-field/id #uuid "d104c14d-f848-548d-9d29-6d5a082dd642" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24514d85-9074-586d-bd86-5782009ad2cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-321307" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =8-8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbdb1aa2-ee88-505b-b0f5-11a4884aebd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300902} :answer-field/id #uuid "50dddaf7-bda4-507c-a0ba-dc6c23800b73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5026dc1f-44d6-553f-896e-a2ae4c316b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300381} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9354bb9-e5b5-562f-a405-f1896c8ec358" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cabafb25-d8ad-503c-847c-e869adfd1bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-321308" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =18(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{18\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-9+9\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "633c874c-66b0-5dc9-9deb-b2099d9d9e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300464} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eee240a-dc89-5352-95ca-e10ac4dfc669" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94efa30-7642-5ade-9551-fe8db98ba682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301193} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc5f858-af5f-5935-a5b3-6e09633833ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5d42d22-afab-5657-a00e-582139185cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-321309" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =20(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-10\\sqrt{2}+10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d96af1-063c-5a93-ae4c-fbf94d822feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813} :answer-field/id #uuid "a258f07c-da2c-5ef5-a60c-3b82e992a21f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce45ba4b-7d8c-5fb0-bd44-a0bc831be2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300990} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2251ba3-99cc-528d-a718-347e04a261c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38098932-4e7b-5400-bb4a-1a39003f645b" :question/math-academy-id "q-321310" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =6+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a04fd90-c804-5fd9-8fa1-659e2bb10ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300077} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9eff38-aeac-5cb1-9523-bc9bdbea8f7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c913b645-6400-5a0c-a995-145873544dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-321311" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =7(0+\\text{i}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0+7\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc50beaf-4f1b-5232-ade9-c9d9974970f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "746bed7b-e090-56a7-9ad1-4c0bfac39908" :question/math-academy-id "q-321314" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ad1466-19e3-5aaa-b651-784e21d815fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3eac432e-a9f4-5e40-ae91-1f28e72debdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321318" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad07eac-4531-5e39-ba60-3955048b52f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ace053b-c257-5ef7-b1b8-ad2acacbefcf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2665b984-2caa-50b4-bc88-bddfbe523e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300145} :answer-field/id #uuid "668ae0d5-ad90-55c4-b03b-4d6b7e3879d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa75b670-46c2-5a24-9eea-0f9253228ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300816} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0aca5ef-02e5-540c-9440-d60fefe80029" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "091fc971-f53b-5795-a1ff-bf6281c0d800" :question/math-academy-id "q-321383" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}{(1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}-{(\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4i\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ and $y=-\\sqrt{3},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36dd9d9c-d1f0-518b-9b47-a6b3afa02f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce2024b7-a71f-5b6f-ae78-cce7af1981ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1fe2936-ce2d-5b95-a874-57358178bd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-321384" :question/problem "![](images/af/afa5a80d73d9d8511cc35804277ad9dc3f1b62b2e1dbe3911d6ea9d665c3baa8.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ {{field-1}} \n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, the polar equation is  \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e64462-1873-527c-97d7-71858cd935a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299977} :answer-field/id #uuid "6023a8be-8b48-57b8-ac63-0a72f7c6b2ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053cf31d-bf3b-5e8e-b7f3-41eeceb5f463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299956} :answer-field/id #uuid "905c2b65-d5e0-5d46-b370-c8ab90bbdc07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df48145a-0c8a-5416-b553-50aafde6b411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301073} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2476875-c02f-5d4e-b65a-f0911730382a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89a81dab-08e9-5bc4-b7bc-65bffdc14b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-321420" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1+\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf2b55df-9b82-5289-aa52-ca93401fe545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919} :answer-field/id #uuid "481cf864-60e6-5fbd-9440-3fbc24bf7b2c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7afbbc-1ab4-58c8-ba60-118f316bd98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300011} :answer-field/id #uuid "580f9bf7-ed2e-5130-a167-0c9266f3537d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8897463-bfba-5bb1-b48b-20964139c591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301113} :answer-field/id #uuid "80cd1d89-8562-50db-a9b0-aa4e20b2509f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "384bec95-fb53-5352-981d-5e071dfd5a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-321424" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i},\n$$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}>0$ and $y=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{|y|}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897f3f0b-a588-5471-a52f-a424811c8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300657} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9ede85-5773-5b6f-8fc6-d1d365cc2324" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2daf815d-258e-5d34-bd23-854fa3794503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300183} :answer-field/id #uuid "9415610a-525b-5d6f-8a5e-df2545146639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9029a697-8d69-532b-b921-5f4702dab115" :question/math-academy-id "q-321918" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af86b077257cd7a6052de0a0a89d720935eace0ac87dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc3655a7-f8db-5846-af3c-25460c132af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf81aff-e496-5b8c-9552-7373708bbd68" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc7372f-b6d1-5b9c-9751-ff4f797dc123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300514} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa2e725-2b7a-5a48-92ef-54503711ba85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea11e213-e469-58c8-9667-84ff5e30c91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321947" :question/problem "![](images/11/11f1e6651d86e0b00884091338029395cd6d2ff44f25b97d010ec750d13bef59.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/2a/2a281b909e4a3577ea3774e9d75c43d97e4d4a69d0f673eed4189834e1ed1c43.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a70675-ce33-5a1b-83ef-f7af2fc8bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300330} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d87c6d-9b20-5d5e-86a2-8e5143f0b1e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb078694-909a-57c5-91b1-91793f4484df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300899} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3087335-a091-5af9-afd7-fc154d3fa50f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "849c57f2-d360-5ce8-a933-fa42e7401299" :question/math-academy-id "q-321948" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/68/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b09d730-d6f8-5cf7-a8bf-f99e04015682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbb153e-6b4d-545b-afde-8ca7a7eb19fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6aa180-7dbf-516f-b362-4b746a2f18e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e86db1-e1dd-57f3-bff4-6c02780d1fdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1036ad5f-680e-537b-8426-836a619b5091" :question/math-academy-id "q-321970" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e29d99fc6ae3a848bfe59ab9e77595ab9ca2a605d0d03139e8599adf15e6fd9.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=9$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801e3126-6f97-55f1-ad47-e2afc002d4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fbc73a-3d60-559a-9cc5-5a1225fc2fc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23db9de0-9618-545f-95a4-aa33750b9019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f0bfc6-692d-528d-8a4b-122802a5cc69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53021125-fcea-5da1-87cd-be2698ee5417" :question/math-academy-id "q-321972" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e907a2-031b-5216-92f5-5f4ff0100ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300865} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec9b74a-062d-5eb7-8cce-bb0a8247b758" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301681} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39126ced-d9b7-5da2-ad4a-fae678eab433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300238} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0ca3ae-534e-533c-86b9-d3973cf4037c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d06f28ce-407e-57f3-9468-51dcff74f6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321976" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/28/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4625cd-b5b3-5b8d-9670-dd9b56b8ac39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3a6527-bf58-56ec-a72b-37753e4a00d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3efba986-59fe-5477-a7a1-a5717686607f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300271} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63d177c-ca19-544d-97d0-9ab8f401a7fe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc15257-2218-54e8-81b4-38b448e8f019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300679} :answer-field/id #uuid "df06b6c6-e63e-5bfa-a5f2-b0b512067676" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "553d8835-419a-5f16-a1a2-9829041916d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321989" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6ba4e7-ec53-5571-b404-d7ae8fed7aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370} :answer-field/id #uuid "021e74f2-cbf1-5b98-b2ba-3fa1e24972e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859e57c5-85b4-5628-998b-9a784a91ac0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633} :answer-field/id #uuid "1daae901-9a53-5d34-b502-7c5af4fe8a96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab4ccfa-051a-593c-a805-70f1d216c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739} :answer-field/id #uuid "50afb390-5bc8-5b51-9edb-ccec40f173bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf8141f3-3d6b-54e7-81c4-c29188080b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-322671" :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+\\,i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\sqrt{3}>0$ and $y=-1<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c0fa2f-1464-5247-83cd-cc39bfa96bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300067} {:answer/id #uuid "3d59f873-e23b-5514-b864-5cc43a7101ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300262} {:answer/id #uuid "59b98b1b-d9ca-5302-a5c2-211e88fa8dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300428} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd7f4e3-b607-5387-895d-45b73e03a077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300516} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6bfc63-9d4d-5334-bf2f-31f2cbbc8576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301054} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeea33a4-2fb9-54e0-a7dd-4e4eabae3342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "069ed041-ef82-5d0f-a684-e05f430b2167" :question/math-academy-id "q-32300" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k,\n$$ \n$c=i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k,$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ what is the value of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}+u-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ we must have $u=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35404663-dec5-5489-8342-b207e82313e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300223} :answer-field/id #uuid "015e1910-83f2-5d0b-89e9-fc7f54299903" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91c8cfc-b514-5ec2-9e9f-2152efd87b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301191} :answer-field/id #uuid "2071708d-3f60-5249-9c0c-afba7797e85c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "599665ce-994a-5a05-a433-000c5a08e621" :question/math-academy-id "q-323153" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx-4=-\\frac{2}{5}\\,t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{4}{25}\\,{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4),\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1) \\\\ {(x-4)}^{2} & =-(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c0af0-b447-58fd-8761-71af3c0f95df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea9a655-49ca-533c-8cb7-421e176fbf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060cda53-8668-5be5-8aef-25b100d05754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e37fe1-bd7c-51e8-ba48-a531ae7460a1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b6ea38-f33a-5329-8a75-bcbcd85f7f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f8d121-1dd4-5d54-b226-07216b17964f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3518da80-0db9-569e-b980-2b5efc57c5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-323819" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b727ac-e6d0-5937-a2a9-900eb69c6a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299965} :answer-field/id #uuid "170d62b2-6193-5523-be19-aead18548068" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c74673e-c2ab-5889-8e3e-bec5faa3947d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300508} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbaa290-9941-5934-8411-2ce0c546c610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ca4adc-f255-5b34-b3db-ea777ee49a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8757540e-8f4f-5260-abd1-525b3d5be69f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11135029-ca34-5b73-ac2c-a6935c3fa9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61a5806-8cd6-5114-a5b2-a3cf868806f1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984e3170-b647-5a8d-ac22-b92f42b05a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec91e54a-571e-5963-ba4c-d762c39f850f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a3d03c74-03cc-5bc6-84c9-29bb98c3c0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-323879" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}-4\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-5+\\frac{13}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a4080e-f7dc-5fb2-982f-0a649c5bde3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301119} :answer-field/id #uuid "013cf738-bd58-52c6-a13c-1b66083c00fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2fff5c-71ef-59e6-a147-2a66c5cf9556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300978} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6bdcb8-418a-54d7-b2f1-5358d7d448c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7d1c524-722a-5a9b-8143-1856821a68f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-324126" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}<0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=\\frac{11}{4}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into three subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4}),\\,(\\frac{11}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4})$ | $(\\frac{11}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11-4x$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(\\frac{11}{4},3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)<0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,\\frac{11}{4},\\,3).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d32b7c1-2505-5658-a644-b28c0281b497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300099} :answer-field/id #uuid "7379b78a-11ad-5ae6-9c95-d6b1eee16ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "856142f4-de92-591a-b38f-6186187501e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-324128" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\n$y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =1((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{9}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df486b3-a6fe-598a-b70e-87b1cdd1e41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300367} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0b5796-f808-5ca3-ba68-4e03f610f34a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5107c5fd-8639-5bbf-a780-c1226b0410f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-324311" :question/problem "The durations of direct flights on a certain route are normally distributed with a mean of $180$ minutes and a standard deviation of $20$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes? **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4960\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4920\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the flight duration (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(180,{20}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(178<X<188).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(178<X<188) & =P(\\frac{178-180}{20}<Z<\\frac{188-180}{20}) \\\\  & =P(-0.1<Z<0.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.1<Z<0.4) & =P(Z<0.4)-P(Z\\le -0.1) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.4)-P(Z\\le -0.1) \\\\  & =Φ(0.4)-Φ(-0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.4) & =0.6554 \\\\ Φ(-0.1) & =0.4602.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.1<Z<0.4) & =Φ(0.4)-Φ(-0.1) \\\\  & =0.6554-0.4602 \\\\  & =0.1952 \\\\  & ≈0.20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes is approximately $0.20.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce3962b-9372-53e8-94dd-19aae1e9b687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674} :answer-field/id #uuid "752c40b7-22f4-5848-b34a-2e00b4e7dbd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6829b460-7921-509d-b187-5e5360672369" :question/math-academy-id "q-324314" :question/problem "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the rod length (in centimeters). Then we have $X∼N(100,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(96.8\\le X<102.4).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(96.8\\le X<102.4) & =P(\\frac{96.8-100}{4}\\le Z<\\frac{102.4-100}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60) & =P(Z<0.60)-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.60)-P(Z\\le -0.80) \\\\  & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.60) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.80) & =0.2119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80<Z<0.60) & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =0.7257-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.5138 \\\\  & ≈0.51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately $0.51.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a6b172-511b-59d5-a4be-e3d793af9fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300302} :answer-field/id #uuid "510fc5f1-e97e-592f-b0a4-6e434ea14445" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f632a9-2c61-532d-871f-cfc71a60aead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300461} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcca370c-170d-50be-9a17-36e8d982d9c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "215c16c3-5948-5417-9503-ddb002423a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-324344" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb6f7f4-57a5-52f7-8e64-395b91ab0e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc161ec3-c1d9-5eb1-baaa-2ab67d18a5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aace1ce7-1675-5a50-8ee4-f15b9647304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324349" :question/problem "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a05661-7722-5963-b806-be7baa982a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fef1f38-f1c6-5dcb-ab28-87a9ef1d2f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c76de2b6-dcfd-5626-8f87-83f3745840b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-324458" :question/problem "In electronics manufacturing, the average resistance of a certain type of resistor is $100\\,Ω.$ However, $5\\%$ of resistors have resistance no more than $96.5\\,Ω.$ Assuming that resistor resistances are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the resistance, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.25$ | $0.20$ | $0.15$ | $0.10$ | $0.05$ | $0.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$Ω$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resistance (in ohms). Then we have $X∼N(100,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 96.5)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 96.5) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{96.5-100}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.5}{1.6449} \\\\  & ≈2.13\\,Ω.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ef80ee-161a-5a7e-8232-8f11f4ab0591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,7,\\,-4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299960} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6f401a-1af1-5902-8ecf-8f185e9e12da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300092} {:answer/id #uuid "3d489b9a-c7ce-5bb5-9c1e-bb2fb01f89fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,7,\\,4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300261} {:answer/id #uuid "7630cb27-95dd-503c-86cf-d9a96b47156b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3,\\,-2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300546} {:answer/id #uuid "875ea830-aed4-554d-85ae-d63a3f76be32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,6,\\,-4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299960} :answer-field/id #uuid "610d999b-36f3-554e-acdc-7690f0627a2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ce8c747-438b-5a4a-8cfe-ec69b8a216ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-32499" :question/problem "Differentiate $2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3t-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-t,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the sum rules, we have\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-3),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{g}(t)-\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}6,\\hspace{0.222em}-4t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}6-(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}-4t-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}7,\\hspace{0.222em}-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31df8d49-ce69-5cb1-a2cd-57e59e1196c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2t-1,0,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300209} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2a4bfc-5f35-5ae9-a821-d3bce3a9c6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2t,0,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300442} {:answer/id #uuid "7a714d86-ceb0-57c7-a902-31a1b72e5f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300565} {:answer/id #uuid "87911319-25bb-5038-bd99-46da453974fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨t-1,0,t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300646} {:answer/id #uuid "9e03f1e2-6fb5-5862-a16d-73e44a1f2e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2t+1,2t+1,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300209} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e2c0bf-91fb-53b7-a114-172b95036f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f67b45fc-cd86-5ee0-8299-de9e4060f565" :question/math-academy-id "q-32503" :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨-t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,t⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨-{t}^{2},0,{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{h}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{h}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩+⟨-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aaa2f26-f6e2-5045-99b6-6e56727e402e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300344} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d68a22-b87f-57cb-891e-6da9bbc1952a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d53565bf-d2c4-5e5a-8659-e7dab8f5253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-325116" :question/problem "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25\\%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$\\%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83\\%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f3bd62-ddb7-5adc-ba61-df9d1b1cefd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300481} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77eacb4-8703-5ba3-a8de-2e5f69a5491f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d7053778-cd34-5d92-bb60-d0cacb0e2655" :question/math-academy-id "q-325129" :question/problem "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813ab495-feaf-5234-8811-0d46af39a9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300603} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfe54a9-2e5e-586d-bfbf-883bd97f382d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75df603e-79fa-5447-bc71-746d4d4d3360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300544} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d7174d-28a8-561d-a458-ed2e56e74e3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30f62290-05c5-54c1-8968-f2a46b935b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-325154" :question/problem "Find the **Cartesian coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis.\n\nThe point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{5}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{13}{5}),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/86/86158ced718c422c9b02d620ca4544eb7d2ff0e110a04fe4b7f749160fc46649.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $(0,\\frac{13}{5}\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b71b3df-f5d6-5c57-8f76-09856b8a7d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299989} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a1fec8-bfd1-5633-b93e-f0e08ccbe550" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e58f3f33-939d-5fca-92a2-5af8ebf126a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c446d8-6b65-53e6-ac39-7c774dbebf1b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6804e529-a43d-5a85-a338-641666526279" :question/math-academy-id "q-325160" :question/problem "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ intersects the negative $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{2}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/23/2399108905ce184b2b2d2d299f5e52539af01228b484316ece8b2efdd6a3b852.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{13}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b96922e-5ad5-5bcf-b37b-ba70ba0e2809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300670} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abc5e38-cc2a-59ff-b464-e518e6a0f3aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6306de16-e869-568b-aa76-f4b4a2ffbb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300462} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a61cb7b-abfd-5c1b-8c1f-9c6565686cbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34aa541d-528e-5947-8566-8089c39f0390" :question/math-academy-id "q-325162" :question/problem "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{23}{6}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cab0604a3a8a45de244519cd407d310e8add3978e13cd20e1ef6b3d38e191fd.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{23}{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d55cc83-8d69-5494-8bd6-5b33bebb6245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300181} :answer-field/id #uuid "3048d132-39e5-5e00-89b1-e5d2285347ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301377} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105aeddf-54b0-5d16-b221-b57ea9d24cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300027} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7045ef-ea93-5520-b720-517a4e4be80e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62d63af2-d66f-5fa9-a04a-0aa1ef3d6685" :question/math-academy-id "q-326042" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6\\cdot {0}^{2}+12x+24\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & =0 \\\\ (2x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-\\frac{3}{2},0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256d8fba-f1fb-5262-a714-0b04a58d57cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300139} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b2ff846-4ffa-5313-bde2-e8e8b663bd6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b770fbd5-2f1a-5282-ba94-e2a31ba49258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300879} :answer-field/id #uuid "3045ec56-6fee-5c79-bbe6-fd0a105d744c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771dd2cc-b51d-57d1-91e8-dd85eb645924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300551} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0cada9e-e53d-5bbb-b887-6a5a212f2ca9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aee0bf07-1691-5e80-899f-ef400a0f41d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-326141" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle ]⋃[\n$$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-3)(x+1)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive  on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,]⋃[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a15151-4bc1-53af-b966-e56abe369a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300532} :answer-field/id #uuid "1744daf7-e0b4-52d5-ab4f-ce219e8b9f21" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85842be3-33ec-57b0-a1d9-8e1079338a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300630} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e239c7-61f7-535f-b22e-d9c9fb4e751b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4806f3-c968-5e92-8ac8-cc02bdfd0968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300091} :answer-field/id #uuid "b583b04f-612f-535e-a012-5b4ab8b521a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "231069bc-0578-5e9f-8f53-9a2feab4caea" :question/math-academy-id "q-326289" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ 108\\,(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}) & =108 \\\\ 81+4(y-2)^{2} & =108 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & =27 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{27}{4} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,2-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5643807c-d4c5-58db-b9a8-0e91bf1fc0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300412} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f037b32-157b-5a89-ac68-dc5335ace5be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d9e700-fd5a-5caa-89ae-1fc4cd1821d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300289} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2da76f1-2620-5a45-94d7-98754e763a09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4832012-403b-56b1-80a4-399af628e376" :question/math-academy-id "q-326334" :question/problem "![](images/28/28c189efc805ec6aef299c4a89c1bb9299db7ad9f8544b86ed7509e6abc2e6a5.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32440ad6-7f7b-5196-b1ab-98d39e61a9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300212} {:answer/id #uuid "55772350-0e9f-5ed8-86b3-1bf60cf29e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300404} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1ccf3a-f7dd-532a-adbc-dd756f877bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300437} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9a36ce-5c2d-5925-b668-8d23e32923ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300817} {:answer/id #uuid "ee149e2e-60ee-50ed-aa3f-5a5cc9613c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300404} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aab5f86-6a49-557c-8f61-783f431a5db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3cc0c68-3553-59ec-9740-0e51c6886431" :question/math-academy-id "q-32662" :question/problem "If $x=y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(8,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(8,4),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(8,4)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{4}-4+2)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276bf0ec-7469-5b52-b357-e7d5b9732c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300149} {:answer/id #uuid "6043a253-bd46-5eb8-9ba8-ce0000d516d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300457} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1e6ccc-be4c-5152-8bd2-b482ccf07e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300683} {:answer/id #uuid "b1155a51-ab6a-5d93-bda9-1de280b175a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300846} {:answer/id #uuid "dac2c371-fb64-5d55-bd04-6c42351b6a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300846} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f9a9ea-6d1c-54d4-a5b4-d257c8265058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb4c52c9-56c1-5911-8844-218ed25165be" :question/math-academy-id "q-327111" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ **NOT** evaluated during this iteration?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,-2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only point where the slope is **NOT** evaluated is $(\\frac{3}{2},-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf5ac96-20c7-5c92-8ea9-216a5837b7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300903} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d135473-41e6-5c2b-8ac5-36fcaf74185e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301528} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32a77848-9b9d-506b-b225-0beb706458f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300215} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b5a821-79a7-570e-b523-ab066a1d2f4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8ea97f79-2635-519c-82bb-e437d3073998" :question/math-academy-id "q-327146" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af5f3426-4280-52f9-b5d8-07309a6e9907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300837} :answer-field/id #uuid "08bbac88-c412-5d7c-b48e-46c5741ce3e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f2c90e-28fb-5130-9ca7-f7336e9ef893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300307} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c72261-3330-5c1e-a071-fd874e5f55c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0e6d437-c042-571f-a8a4-bf2beee49c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327148" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-12(0)+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,).$\n\n![](images/94/94a5bd2df6ae6177e62b66a0a978a358d73fed255e425337812887c81b9d2d10.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9bdc1b2-8ea1-5127-89c0-6b997d1343aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c7c1d5-e064-5947-ad92-edb4b63aee4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fc8ff6e-fc33-5efd-ab02-f17a877d633b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327247" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =[{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)]+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d53bf1bf-3240-597c-8ba6-91c426822271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301022} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2fa77f-5bcb-5df5-8444-35c66cd13a26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c456891a-3eac-5005-ae95-48c26bfb98c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-327277" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58722931-7a9c-51fb-b515-dee313825033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465975} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e9de92-470b-54aa-b104-88c38fbe918b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465976} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4dfa34-fa5f-5e70-9a91-47a39c73d7dc" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beb1d7b5-f785-5bc3-b4ea-969a943a627e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465977} {:answer/id #uuid "0efc561a-3ddb-5641-a4bd-3b5a0dbcb23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465978} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b77119-d12c-5899-abaf-43ce62409b63" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466832} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3eaf501-e9d6-592f-bbe5-e3364f8b6f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465979} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7a7e0d-3265-5b01-9b2a-bc64949c7e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465979} :answer-field/id #uuid "598cb171-6a52-5baf-9743-c8479c663822" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{8})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72c780dd-c7ca-58af-8e2e-0a4e9d41e44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327582" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(0,1).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$; $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b64c24fe-86c0-528a-a80a-d441c9376663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300870} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f40c8d-649b-5cc5-8611-1a3900cfece1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a88295e-a9f4-5381-9209-7cdc6c3b161d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327716" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4283091-a412-5b53-a1ae-2a2cc3242c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301168} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf06143-dbf4-5508-b8c0-01ed12656707" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "234490c5-571f-5c2b-abd3-fc18f2b6c526" :question/math-academy-id "q-327763" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle 14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right) & =7\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63df4a4-ed4e-594d-a87c-7a4085320bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869} :answer-field/id #uuid "00924e31-27a5-58f1-944d-ca579e1c3713" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de50fd-2ab8-57fe-9cfb-ece3bde403ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300541} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a73176-b3ae-58c8-81f2-699e6e8ba975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7055cfca-1048-5afb-ba28-b8851f0876dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-327822" :question/problem "![](images/31/31b10310b585d867ecf883db2a5d778ec5ec9160df03fc4487d2ef7fd0b9a3af.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/73/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff88e02e-cfda-5128-8c1d-f91479a569f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227} :answer-field/id #uuid "5814c550-0d18-5b22-a1e2-06d2501b0824" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6680920-1294-53a9-9623-086e46da3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300952} :answer-field/id #uuid "c481516b-af38-5789-9db8-86dd24b8d0f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d17e2397-6da6-568e-b067-e70d0b979de7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327828" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f29c8db431035fda7e50771b3baaf6d19f51a60e26582957bbff0f70dbfdc5ed.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/24/24b56c8addea0d8a0f3effeeefc2788023c452e98b05d10db7893aeebde9ba5e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa59ec8d-cc80-5c21-9a98-1a321275e779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b1ece4-081f-5e29-9bb2-7e66dd6a50dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3ca0ddb-ad18-53c8-8fa4-f75ebc48934e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ef5771-df19-54b8-9c2c-99c1a01e2acc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50c33ba2-db10-58a1-9a90-99dd50ad5588" :question/math-academy-id "q-327831" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5bf161de62bc1b38aaeb87a327df551e8a7043f2867caa060d652cd065bece9.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6398a1-b09b-5fe5-8b22-062bcee1ef3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300971} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bfcebf-053f-576b-a004-657c68248546" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140964c7-df0e-5db6-94ce-6a0240be4dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300040} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439ef2-f460-56da-9ba7-f298fa601bbf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c61885c3-83e2-5a8d-b365-c4d1e934aa7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327847" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](images/df/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1be446-3da9-58b3-a812-0605b95990d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300107} {:answer/id #uuid "32d9ac64-0542-5055-b1e9-6bb4066c0534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300216} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc47ad0-9751-5257-9126-88536709155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300908} {:answer/id #uuid "d57f47fc-1f9d-59be-aebe-b95c91b0223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301025} {:answer/id #uuid "fd00cfa2-fcdb-51f0-b98f-8000ae55ba7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300908} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af4d97b-faf9-5f88-9b39-8c8fbc7ad730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9af4fe7c-30e2-5561-acae-f8071602310e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32785" :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t-3 \\\\ y &=7-9t \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t-3 \\\\ 7-9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ -9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-3,7,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d76fab-2f56-5b67-bbb4-3fac0ef14a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300864} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1830fc0-d9af-5d64-8c03-c71ba621745f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1369c3-c5a8-5903-b97a-51feef5a8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.675" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300102} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f23c1f-daa6-52df-92ad-335271c423fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e16b2152-6577-5fb2-90ae-6762bd67fd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327980" :question/problem "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.675\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =16+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.675)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a400b3-1805-5a4a-a1c5-a10cd56e3a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300125} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a8c55d-0187-5967-b5c4-488aec645fd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2126e4b6-9b8c-5eca-8bd7-19f152c15bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.381" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300119} :answer-field/id #uuid "3056aedc-16f3-5242-9a76-69e73fc879d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89941d18-05e5-5495-94f7-02c35c889216" :question/math-academy-id "q-327982" :question/problem "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude–phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.381\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFor the amplitude,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ & ={2}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =29 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{29}\\sin \\,(x-0.381).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "387abbd5-7a35-5c1e-b84f-4cf4f6316359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300234} :answer-field/id #uuid "112ad757-0c3a-5732-ba3f-165b48a2c516" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d1823d-388d-5e43-a6da-a2e4fa7cce44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90e3f64-e1e7-583c-adde-24054cbfb4a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46d62efa-444f-5b8c-8b2d-8175168be70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327988" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ ϕ & =\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)≈1.061\\text{ }\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)≈\\sqrt{21}\\,\\sin \\,(6x-1.061).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c58237-1e79-538c-ba35-64864273d7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299953} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c6a4ff-0ad2-5a80-99b9-227f85929a6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fabc70d-25ca-5a30-9a52-90a915524efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-328087" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{24})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{24}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17886196-33a4-5bd5-b696-1278d1f2f573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300058} :answer-field/id #uuid "464540b0-cf17-54f7-a7c2-e37a231f1dbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee30a758-7016-5793-a211-943189d101ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-328241" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx={720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={150}^{∘}-{150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={210}^{∘}-{150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & ={360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={720}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={120}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={120}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef9030ad-74b7-5b17-8c43-02b69c98b5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301148} :answer-field/id #uuid "0168c77c-6df5-52ee-bdb6-794664e31131" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5731fad9-ebde-5b11-9201-0bc52d077579" :question/math-academy-id "q-328248" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{6}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{12}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecdf539-f4c9-5e2a-b1d3-147515693798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145} :answer-field/id #uuid "908f284e-1908-5bfa-9308-7074a7ede550" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301687} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4981ef-e57a-51b9-b310-d03c74ee1689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34a02c9-d0c9-59ab-b604-48db38e67691" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2e29b9a7-ba3d-59be-8f85-186fc6a70498" :question/math-academy-id "q-328327" :question/problem "![](images/52/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](images/1b/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb30d24-e5fa-5ff3-b793-37ff771c864a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300014} {:answer/id #uuid "26a29261-3951-5f05-aa91-50190f871a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300147} {:answer/id #uuid "54f4bc9d-9659-533f-bcde-0d8203d7cee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300401} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8ad0ce-f737-58e3-9a2a-e7e35fa2da52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300509} {:answer/id #uuid "849785f2-76ee-5ce4-8123-ab009fa163a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300624} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d48190f-ac7f-5e0c-a042-b8a82e88e0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38bbc30d-2715-5fd5-8cc3-adae49e8b272" :question/math-academy-id "q-32835" :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}-1⟩$, find $({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write: \n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩\\cdot ⟨{t}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}-1⟩+⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot (2t)+6t\\cdot ({t}^{3}-1))+((2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-2+0+6{t}^{4}-6t+4{t}^{2}+4+9{t}^{4} \\\\  & =15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d946acc-1690-5312-86d7-7cf32eab80f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005} :answer-field/id #uuid "36376771-433e-5cb4-ad85-7a583159d896" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6911461d-1eec-524f-81d5-980cedf8faec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300490} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b0e770-aab7-549b-9a99-36b7bc6bc735" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17905033-ab88-5a2b-a0a3-a96238af287a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300059} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d05137-0c1a-53eb-8dc8-440d4dd570bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89353c9a-0abe-5319-848d-3b0db5e80514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300653} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6144399-9a57-51ec-8e3b-e87679b69d3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8798d95-6336-5f26-817e-c5e7e84cb46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-328350" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $3\\tan \\,(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd40c1a7-4737-5ac7-92b7-ce2972ce3347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301056}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301056} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e034008-ac5a-5473-888b-7e2ba963d6bb" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86e943-101c-5124-a19f-105ec58471bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300004} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e34bb2-4091-5928-bf66-3f66dce646a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b702f711-7bce-5dac-bb9d-73265281cac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300875} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ae288a5-673d-5cde-a257-75c65d7d68ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301666} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573319c8-7f02-5219-8b54-b3b5e0c0fda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300418} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fba2120-20ab-5593-804e-aaf75643e822" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0f1ca25-ee5a-54e2-bf36-a5709bf211bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300929} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9453c95-094e-577b-95b4-6dd66d8d2d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4290c01a-0e74-5195-b7ef-186fd98b2266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300286} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0389b8e-6242-57fe-8e9a-d8e04ca7e1d9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a73a181-9ab0-50f0-81db-220b10f64e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300432} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e27c2a-e67a-5387-87c8-6e3730c2c7bf" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301918} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9725ae-4c24-51c4-ac54-5062ccc5f3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300182} :answer-field/id #uuid "f779dc53-589f-5133-a3e9-01490173baaf" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "718e7839-05f5-5267-b703-e30edd06aa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-328359" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}\n\n${x}_{5}=$\n{{field-5}}$,\\,{x}_{6}=$\n{{field-6}}$,\\,{x}_{7}=$\n{{field-7}}$,\\,{x}_{8}=$\n{{field-8}}." :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(3n+2)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\n- Setting $n=7$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}, \\\\ {x}_{5} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{6}=\\frac{17\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{8}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "078d569d-3dbc-59b7-9e41-fa53454320b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{8s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{21}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302506} {:answer/id #uuid "11c1913a-69ac-5c89-9a03-2dc9c611e265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302573} {:answer/id #uuid "199ad8b5-d7ea-534c-867c-5c36cf4560bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{7}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302626} {:answer/id #uuid "254776bc-c348-5f99-b166-75f86a6c1dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302697} {:answer/id #uuid "27dd8e92-2960-5bfd-adea-66065610d61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-14s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{23}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{130}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302713} {:answer/id #uuid "2b22c3c8-246d-53fa-a3d3-a39b18ba2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\