#uuid "1f0b5a71-4d2a-5e78-9865-6f100e3bdf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "sF(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302653} {:answer/id #uuid "33cac2e1-bdaa-5990-8a81-b1359205e70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302777} {:answer/id #uuid "75fa7a96-28b1-5086-ac2a-11ad5ec18cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{F(s)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303179} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1b90de-33ba-52d0-b256-b0a05282dba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{F(s)}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303917} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e19ddc-f545-553b-b6eb-abe7804a2c5e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304472} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2bc3f2-61b7-533a-a9ce-834e3662dd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{st}\\text{ }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302634} {:answer/id #uuid "f0932c02-5203-56a1-9170-3e3442c1257f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\text{ }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba079457-39a7-5341-8294-fffcd198c31f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32d46b6e-c28b-5a1b-acb4-7332fefea078" :question/math-academy-id "q-331084" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}\n$$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ={{field-1}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={A}^{′}(t)\\text{ }dt \\\\ dv={{field-3}} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({{field-4}}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ $0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-5}}\\,\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6467cbac-ff00-5e32-9f5d-aebaeab94452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303067} {:answer/id #uuid "65f07420-42e4-5280-8d11-28571ab51f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303073} {:answer/id #uuid "685514e2-58d8-56ae-8d6f-af7c648ca58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303090} {:answer/id #uuid "78a6cdb1-cdb7-529a-a206-b4bc8b01d490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303200} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6cd337-ba7e-5e70-87d7-a18c15b4add5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303524} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13bfe01-a3e8-5357-ab16-aadea8774e7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16545366-fc02-54d1-b5e5-0bdfaeb6e5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-331213" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y+3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-y+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-{y}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-{y}_{2}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{17}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-\\frac{17}{6}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{8}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{16}{6}+\\frac{28}{6}+\\frac{30}{6}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{87}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{29}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\,\\frac{11}{4}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(1)≈\\frac{11}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f71240-bb0c-5f88-aa8f-bad78129051e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302809} {:answer/id #uuid "7f81f627-4a94-5605-a992-b30fed9a6094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303252} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58c8d8-2add-56d5-8748-cc156eff765d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303442} {:answer/id #uuid "dd537db4-0c59-5d7b-aae8-ef32ea7dd103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303840} {:answer/id #uuid "e45fa54c-5745-51ae-bf69-a766b6080e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303252} :answer-field/id #uuid "72276f0d-6681-58d9-8d57-f2ba1345262a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3cf5589b-b0ad-5c9f-91a0-818d6183c44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-33135" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(4,0,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4t &=4 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t &=0 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{(1)},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(4,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c1913a-69ac-5c89-9a03-2dc9c611e265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302573} {:answer/id #uuid "199ad8b5-d7ea-534c-867c-5c36cf4560bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{7}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302626} {:answer/id #uuid "80435062-08c8-593d-a513-b338e6b3c1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303258} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdac532-ad6c-595b-8d59-d5932925698f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{12}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303826} {:answer/id #uuid "e367908f-e645-597c-ac1f-53f754a7a7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{12}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302573} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b10fcb-727f-5894-a339-e3b18d6aae08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ed58ae3d-4e2e-509f-b710-dac0bd55a1aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-33136" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩\n$$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\pi )^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e6fb0b-7752-5ba1-b6f5-0f4503a56e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{277}{96}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303799} :answer-field/id #uuid "65711027-2279-5b75-a24c-086ac42c9727" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffaaba3f-4385-52e4-92b4-7a7eb4082ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{181}{96}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304069} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2a5557-f619-58f4-8cb0-81505ac28562" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdb8b42c-fd27-5292-b7ed-10bb5edb212b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331396" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{31}{8}$ | $\\frac{51}{8}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{31}{8}+\\frac{51}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{181}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{181}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{31}{8}$ | $\\frac{51}{8}$ | $\\frac{181}{96}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{181}{96} \\\\  & =\\frac{277}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{31}{8}$ | $\\frac{51}{8}$ | $\\frac{181}{96}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{277}{96}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{277}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddfaa1cf-9094-50a9-80b4-b125f881ac8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41/48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303846} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d308e1-8fed-52f7-b89d-12d1f79a26c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca5fff6d-9c5e-596e-a71c-dded2c4e4557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41/48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303697} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9ee164-7095-5b93-a71f-8e2e9ca68760" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceac110d-a7cd-5143-9b38-6d1fb86b4aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-331399" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+1,\\,y(-1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{16}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{41}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{41}{48}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+\\frac{41}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{41}{48}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{41}{48}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0)≈\\frac{41}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b793d66-c38f-51a3-97b8-f9fa92be7d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9/32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303113} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4ec419-a64a-5ed0-9733-715a54bdfa87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d49336a1-0536-5ca9-9990-b619dd2b3cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-331413" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(-1+\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\left(-\\frac{9}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{9}{32}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7657e8-c789-5492-a182-917f3fed13ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2/125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303221}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303221} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8c5284-7f80-5c15-873d-7f7be1840f33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "821cb3f0-7b89-5496-be23-95c1fac0f5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331415" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{4}{25}$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\left(-\\frac{4}{25}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\left(-\\frac{4}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{4}{25}$ | $-\\frac{2}{125}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edc917f1-2459-54da-9e00-52674da8bdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303948} :answer-field/id #uuid "187643d2-fc90-55b2-a5fa-398d838be42c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "672dcee4-150f-5fe2-a11d-4b9803bbdf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303082} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3562ef-cf5f-58e0-bd48-fe47324a441c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97470737-5822-596d-8f26-760348eca043" :question/math-academy-id "q-331416" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-2y-2,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{7}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{9}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71df2766-cecf-5134-9a9a-3077bedc603f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e5a59f-6190-5f59-a1d1-238d25e0cd28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7511a65-5c20-5b44-9ad2-de1b2ee180fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-331494" :question/problem "A fair eight-sided die is rolled $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $7$ or $8$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{4}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =15{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.297,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a81a47c7-179d-5483-a227-55f039ceab1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303492} :answer-field/id #uuid "b703b978-722c-5e0b-95fc-55b9de7046c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7557cacf-886f-55fb-9076-1837e9a0913c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332138" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\cos \\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1704f202-ad10-5a8f-b37a-2571e0cb54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302606} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f219ae-f2f8-5acf-af63-4e88382630fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24bac5e7-d993-51f0-aa0a-781406c4bd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332139" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\cos \\,(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943ada75-e8c9-5d64-8cce-ce3d2d08c85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2517b9-ed76-5cd1-9530-d352f6239167" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64b6ca5b-7886-5c02-8389-aebf02e6026c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332147" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$1-2{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d11a4f8-ffee-5767-9368-4c4c6f3d7aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302940} :answer-field/id #uuid "51165ca4-38a6-5ad0-bb61-4d9760434384" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3454171-a1b3-502e-bdda-985c2af86469" :question/math-academy-id "q-332150" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\cos \\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2} & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d347f734-6ca7-5952-bea8-22d7f06f35a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303767} :answer-field/id #uuid "f93642d3-ae42-5979-8df4-3afa14978559" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0d3b4ee6-98d1-5207-a0a1-2482e40fe54d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332151" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\cos \\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}^{2} & ={0}^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db407194-1963-5243-8d79-cf5a3f72be48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303820} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbccc8ec-a5eb-5258-9187-bb43d0322dc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e371d2-f34b-5f6a-8fcd-44fa665e2761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303879} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf6a6ab-6c25-5904-834e-b935f1492c43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87336710-8c90-5846-a70d-1640898f0eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332188" :question/problem "Express $\\sin \\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\sin \\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sin \\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f822acb5-ecac-5f49-b866-51e4b3f931d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304010} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed13432-5e59-56ca-8d4b-ec53c607d7c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304151} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95222c59-a1bc-52c3-a480-7a68d8df4df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303381} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b53ffe-e010-5741-9c93-865cbe86ea58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "78623ec6-7143-54b5-8ec3-a1bf17721f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332191" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162361a3-dbb8-55f1-8044-208f8dd3353a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302599} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd25f35-a51b-5083-921b-43c9ceede301" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2b7c23-5b66-5dce-ab8d-359a56f7bc9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302952} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d7149c-fafc-5466-a016-dc86da34b333" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f6d9223e-755f-520e-aa29-e860e8e723c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332195" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a27733-ae3e-5d29-837c-05dccc75e005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302586} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf5f7311-ab17-54e7-8e5f-438c11ddcd2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "720d9b9e-459a-5820-84a2-68055ba82c56" :question/math-academy-id "q-332319" :question/problem "A bag contains $10$ balls numbered $1$ through $10.$ One ball is randomly selected, its number is recorded, and then it is placed back in the bag before the next selection. The process is repeated until the ball numbered $10$ is selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ selections is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came on. The probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on any single selection is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on the $5$th selection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{5-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa099851-646a-5448-a9f4-7669c29075bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303505} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b55fa5-5e9d-53c5-a21f-9799cd92636c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac860a6a-b0e9-53ea-a402-4f5d52f1de41" :question/math-academy-id "q-332354" :question/problem "A board game uses a special deck of $10$ cards: $4$ cards show a star and $6$ cards show a circle. A player randomly draws a card, records the symbol, and then puts the card back into the deck before drawing again. The player keeps drawing until they get a star card. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards drawn until a star card is obtained. The chance of selecting a star card from the deck is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections before obtaining a star card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{3-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =[1+\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}]\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =[1+\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{9}{25}]\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "935c4d79-68f6-58db-9093-7064c3dda3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303369} :answer-field/id #uuid "090e23e4-d7cd-50c3-89cf-b84384fafa1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc593c3f-7ba8-56d3-8356-96b47c5e5070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303613} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb85910-e97a-5bf1-9fd2-32f70c9d9f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2915c497-6e62-5a26-8916-051acceb249d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332518" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b7fa38-39fc-5dee-ba14-cf49ab9dfe40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303462} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5c46a8-650a-5828-9cae-cd8ebf112745" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3715d30e-925f-5e2f-ab76-d7441d961492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302798} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83c44a2-b522-5f28-a13b-fddb56eeec07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2fbd7b19-3db6-511d-9b76-1b94d19c3553" :question/math-academy-id "q-332521" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5028c2c9-a69f-5a0b-8737-ba477eb3a980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302960} :answer-field/id #uuid "74faffb4-f645-59d3-9e78-630ec34eb937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e379055-0024-5e69-88e3-193d3d4d6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302549} :answer-field/id #uuid "b23873b6-4140-558f-9725-9c5701597d74" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "094c30c7-640d-5075-b2ee-56000c80b71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332522" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+0\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fcf309-c37a-50ed-bddd-32665199d43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303388} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a485cb-3a5d-58b2-8210-c2e46d9ee097" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9ae330-2bfc-5c5c-b80d-80c42450baf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302641} :answer-field/id #uuid "82945e92-a7e0-5095-96f5-9795df618379" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9aca0bad-fb3b-5bb9-b62a-e54827012cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332524" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\ope