n/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edc75cc-a655-5f39-a948-71fa367a6215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302849} {:answer/id #uuid "603fc7c9-56aa-527d-9014-5e5a248674ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303042} {:answer/id #uuid "76bd3396-81a0-5e9c-a971-d928a196bb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303184} {:answer/id #uuid "93ee005e-c0c3-5ed3-919a-ce496f2f1597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303376} {:answer/id #uuid "a2351e4b-9c09-540d-ade1-8e32b50795d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54ba66f-0b9a-596e-b61e-f7c6549dfefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1a86a65-5d24-5f83-8695-88153c4cb0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332858" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-1,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{15}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2-\\frac{15}{16}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{21}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{7}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(2)≈\\frac{15}{64}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "07d14831-aea7-5347-84f9-25f41978fb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305224} {:answer/id #uuid "154048e9-acbe-5f59-9e18-820fa18ba04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305335} {:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} {:answer/id #uuid "4232283d-ef4f-5093-9603-9b055cd7601f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305804} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaff3a3-015e-5ac9-af35-ee4636f31af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306017} {:answer/id #uuid "637c5e5e-6ce7-5938-b14c-a011022a3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306115} {:answer/id #uuid "7f64699b-83aa-58d4-93aa-cd90febe5c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306371} {:answer/id #uuid "80bcb621-3c43-5efd-84e5-b398b7670b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306377} {:answer/id #uuid "858bfa6f-4a26-5ea8-bf05-c82787e1d16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306424} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b03e9-0749-562a-b299-e3af23995ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨{2}^{t},\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307187} {:answer/id #uuid "f30b8be4-1b5a-550e-a1eb-f3bf87eff617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d9d7b7-dae5-58c1-a087-1d82ce4b62aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306703} :answer-field/id #uuid "06bd937f-c92d-54a1-92e8-7d1c6743aebb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473f712d-2122-5bc5-8c6f-6795e30e8a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305842} :answer-field/id #uuid "88fe4f1d-3947-5ab2-a5d3-2062570c5afc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aff09e41-4b9b-5be3-9a45-df2596ac9842" :question/math-academy-id "q-333010" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1/2 & 3/2 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+\\frac{3}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1)t+(-\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3) \\\\ (\\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2)t+(\\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1 &=0 \\\\ \\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2 &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3 &={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1 &={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{7}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{8},\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{11}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c202ea7-be08-58b5-acd2-9a5d180907c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306187} :answer-field/id #uuid "238eea2d-8afd-5ec5-9f14-72e88a699aec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81ea3fc-50e1-546b-a338-7220adc095a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307155} :answer-field/id #uuid "44744128-85b3-54f5-a1e9-5d773985daa7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607a64dc-0bf9-592a-9c6c-0414cd973799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306081} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b40cf5-8f74-5bc6-8dc3-e211e812acc1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8e082c60-50f1-50ca-a231-91775e0a6957" :question/math-academy-id "q-333056" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-6.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)-(-6))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{17}{12}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06da7c36-3115-5dd1-9bd9-47462558d38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305214} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2ca8aab-7c64-5002-947a-e27b54bd0ab0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15b2918e-6e2c-583b-b498-2bef41a00100" :question/math-academy-id "q-333083" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c66a7e-bc86-5548-b87e-89bdc127051b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305387} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc8a6b9-1f2e-5e63-b39c-68636a8566dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cad24787-42bd-556e-b85b-0a04c69e3d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333091" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd2a713-45cb-59c9-ba53-12a413122219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305603}]