pe at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f66ab269-10d0-5040-8b86-8dddde2addc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307438} :answer-field/id #uuid "db13446c-db74-5df4-a2a9-586955c1703c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6eabd0e-cfa4-573b-a4d6-b19f7678ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-333094" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-1,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dba1fdc-16d5-5d8f-add8-30a9fc6224ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305747} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca1a5d4-c5a0-5a61-80e0-c04fa105c10e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90c0a163-184b-5c83-aeb6-c854884b750e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333095" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{5}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=-1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee6b9fb-eab1-586b-b148-7fda1a1e04fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306642} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5fd01fa-0c66-5134-a1d3-15fc77a7c210" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12da890d-c373-5ff0-a52c-86f1f4e91ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305317} :answer-field/id #uuid "d61c9470-2bca-5b9e-9e53-c99182a7f12d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e8ef154-d084-5634-adad-a8eb382e2284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306502} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a17020-b250-5da5-a408-9d855778d107" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4debed5-2d7a-556a-bfa2-5f79fb9939e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333210" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.17$ | $0.751$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.34$ | $0.738$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.51$ | $0.724$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.17$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.751.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.17)-0.751 \\\\  & =((0.17)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.17)})-0.751 \\\\  & ≈0.024.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.34$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.738.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.34)-0.738 \\\\  & =((0.34)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.34)})-0.738 \\\\  & ≈-0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.51$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.724.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.51)-0.724 \\\\  & =((0.51)^{2}+\\frac{1}{1+2(0.51)})-0.724 \\\\  & ≈0.031.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.17$ | $0.751$ | $0.024$ |\n| $2$ | $0.34$ | $0.738$ | $-0.027$ |\n| $3$ | $0.51$ | $0.724$ | $0.031$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8231d974-b6a8-5330-a92e-a90e85a60928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306397} {:answer/id #uuid "b9230692-b642-50e1-8b9f-2d28c93b5e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306878} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d758a4-95dd-54b9-af2a-99da2d9ab07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306397} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de8ac47-b07f-5558-ad94-789dc471c9ff" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c9a7d4-b06a-5cdb-9962-e193a476a1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306161} {:answer/id #uuid "80fcd8c4-7079-5a79-b11e-246507fc0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306379} {:answer/id #uuid "c86481a2-cfb2-51e9-bfcc-dc141bbc3761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306161} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8076bbd-36fe-5d52-a336-7da7064e9284" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ccdf84f-90ab-5a80-979b-0303df5f0003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306192} {:answer/id #uuid "db4e9531-cd38-55ab-a08d-40b36176a533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307182} {:answer/id #uuid "fd274a1e-b045-546e-9a9d-9d2f190755c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307182} :answer-field/id #uuid "e809d04f-da61-5f8e-90f5-3c243f460cf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422e20f0-d02b-5e00-9697-46b5b29662fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305802} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc59a0b-44d7-50d7-a416-f6709f5457ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307070} {:answer/id #uuid "fa325514-97c1-571d-84c1-eab9d10edc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307070} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcaedc5-f99c-561a-bac5-5fb6dc6f47fe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c772074b-831b-5d42-b11f-0bacd35780bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333253" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{3}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 6+8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 42 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{17}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed2c971-651e-5d56-ada2-84bd3cb100d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305597} {:answer/id #uuid "3c166efe-ab22-53e7-b8cf-6a31dc4bf04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726} {:answer/id #uuid "9093d7cc-1ee0-5ba0-a467-0a9a86e3d62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305726} :answer-field/id #uuid "053a1d32-1848-5017-9355-a805ad738f60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d17fe4-1972-58a4-aaf1-f0ed6976f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305991} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6ab164-1c7a-580a-9c14-74f58a7e7f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} {:answer/id #uuid "b4643b1c-12a4-5e0e-88ba-fa77c45a3b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305991} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bafe18-37f0-5fb0-98d8-df878247e043" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412651a7-361a-553a-93e8-958aa7f394f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305793} {:answer/id #uuid "60a59f91-5da8-54e0-9534-06c7466710b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306082} {:answer/id #uuid "fa158e8f-6e8c-5999-b041-d4371db07b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306082} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a2a9e0-218a-52ae-8da4-4a6ba530f33c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053e4e0-6f48-59b4-942e-283a7a924d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305301} {:answer/id #uuid "36f3dbb9-1dbf-5545-9700-4d0fec18877c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305682} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbf2f2a-7d63-5084-af0f-96a19808cda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305301} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8787d8-d9f7-592d-87d9-a78344009511" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfae395a-5327-5bd0-a343-e2d83faa338d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333254" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+1)(y+3),\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈-\\frac{9}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)({y}_{p}+3) \\\\  & =-(2+1)(-3+3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,-\\frac{9}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-3.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 0+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-(-3))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=-\\frac{9}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=-3\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519486c5-90c3-52fd-9c0b-a8381128adbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305945} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f50ede-b58d-5fcb-952c-56fec8d57d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306685} {:answer/id #uuid "d81347b9-4983-5d32-b880-a805ebf1219c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305945} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d7cf23-ce04-596e-ac93-f9d3415a13c1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1107c8-0638-5c9e-b2f5-7b690c0fd832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305282} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3dfe3e-ba6c-5d59-90b3-11b9ef6b0f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{182}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305704} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f03b72-e704-5d97-9521-dbb13d9d88c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{218}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305282} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be80b50-de19-51f1-a542-bd65a7b33ae9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e4f446-c732-504b-89f2-c91770db9f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305361} {:answer/id #uuid "385be8db-3780-5125-8bd4-78b77a17d783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305692} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e6232b-295e-5406-b539-50f755c46a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306823} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bedc529-168e-52eb-a40b-97b7962cab50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80fe31d0-b716-5786-9e09-adfdd27752d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306380} {:answer/id #uuid "924cde06-b8d8-5853-8309-a8bd5914cd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306533} {:answer/id #uuid "93f741c4-37d4-5840-874d-e7def79852b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{46}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306380} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe696558-5b68-53de-86c6-a9fffd01cf40" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e25e9ef6-8ec1-52d8-9aae-49a5bbeb895a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333262" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{8}{3},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=8.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+8=\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{8}{3})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{16}{3}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{128}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{768}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{64}{3}=24\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $24$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈24.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d531f41-03d1-5f8a-b99c-4fcb2439b767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450384} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8f83c-d5b8-51f9-8162-dadf9d36ee63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450384} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5b8e14-c5b0-5c4b-95ab-4b819237e981" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ebf1794-dd01-555e-96c7-a7ae089c89bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450386} {:answer/id #uuid "363209f5-ad30-5800-8a06-ae8f84e8efde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450386} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7fb117-7117-5d26-990a-f7852a87ead6" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4803b3d9-920e-5622-935b-02beb52674a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450388} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3c8d02-5e8c-58d5-ad2b-c2793761cc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450389} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ba31bf-db1d-5a3d-b242-c4484fb48ed9" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae417908-c0fa-5671-acc5-d71f00848463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450390} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3309eb-216a-5b7d-90c0-aaf606aeb4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450391} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6aa78c-584f-5b2b-aa83-d6d6710c377c" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86209978-f558-5e50-81ba-e94e00e9e46b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333340" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$” is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$”; “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c0f79f7-7a42-5f0a-acea-ee182e1d0884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450392} {:answer/id #uuid "486c4858-9e53-5f4a-9101-0a156eaeafd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450392} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9b856c-9269-5a16-a3e6-0ebe2c77f23d" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7ab8b7-0954-5970-abd1-6f3a716e14d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450394} {:answer/id #uuid "eccac1e7-dfdb-52a9-b414-4362130d31ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450395} :answer-field/id #uuid "337ffef5-6497-5a9f-9f84-d0609429354f" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451151} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6818baf8-b861-59f2-bf70-f5ea4e70f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450396} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0ca3d9-f6fd-5f7a-80b8-18c015cbc775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450397} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3a15ae-9eac-5d39-bddd-260180e09ee4" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc724bf1-8f4e-589b-a4d6-9b5c793f4b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450398} {:answer/id #uuid "3036aade-b645-5e9d-a805-52ebdc44d8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450398} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8a67c6c-8687-535f-8104-89e446eb04a8" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6837c06-642a-5ef0-97aa-16a1aa0e164f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}y,\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)((1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =5{(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{3}{2}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5cd71c-7b4e-52e2-948f-2d2e8a954945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450400} {:answer/id #uuid "644a37ca-5958-567b-a6c1-1f89ded41cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450401} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6de4a9-a754-57e7-9bba-3e4cafc89a6e" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f8c4d7-dcd9-500c-aa07-3ae311e655a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450402} {:answer/id #uuid "08fdbaed-23fb-5d03-a339-faa2cf1739b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450403} :answer-field/id #uuid "7315f5bb-8e4e-5908-b7b2-2b415490cec5" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beea1d0a-12f1-530c-8072-1c69a9959000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450404} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1055b5-e8ef-59bc-b1a7-cb5dca85cc9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450405}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450405} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfa1247-0e8f-5ec0-9c34-9a0e9ee5a13c" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9fafa5-80d9-5ee0-9073-4740bef21b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450406} {:answer/id #uuid "c630c288-0420-5f1b-a108-ee81fdf124e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450406} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e7a1a0-71f4-55ee-a17d-4a5bb7f1501d" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7bf24239-c81d-5a71-bce2-db470e9ad2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $$\n\\displaystyle y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{9}{4},\n$$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{9}{4}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$” is true.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)((1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{9}{4}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.6$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$”; “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b7a747-95ef-5439-8a0d-a5d0bc972bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305178} {:answer/id #uuid "071d818f-582a-511b-8e1a-da0e02e5e527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305217} {:answer/id #uuid "39c46305-4821-5d35-b86b-257d73a82b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305701} {:answer/id #uuid "60474004-6f0b-518e-bd0a-d1fd73598741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} {:answer/id #uuid "79db6f46-1f9b-5903-82ac-d8484423c92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7db7aa7-b12e-53b6-82a4-b2d5f20ca566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a9de11b-dc85-5ae6-9285-a0d6cc055460" :question/math-academy-id "q-33336" :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+(\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}t)+(\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}t+\\operatorname{ln}t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c024c8-cbcc-58a2-939e-a40af31a54a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} {:answer/id #uuid "a2372d5c-536a-5236-bac9-dfd679209e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306674} {:answer/id #uuid "ff097213-fe54-52b5-99e0-b87fddae6322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307505} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de85b24-4dcb-5394-82d5-69aa9a855c0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4066d9-710b-5013-9774-538b0c0f86a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305257} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ce066-1dc3-57fd-a2c2-1cfc803a719a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{218}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305428} {:answer/id #uuid "735f3754-9260-585f-af03-898b2c50df50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{812}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305257} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b743a1-0582-5cb1-adc6-1d67d3465602" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394d274d-d00f-5073-a974