ref 17592186307491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d3b770-d5b4-5b3f-b8f0-e0faa47c6574" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ff0b66-8902-5813-a9a1-67be227aad94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305188} {:answer/id #uuid "1f76106f-cb78-5786-89dd-2544055c0706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305456} {:answer/id #uuid "59c31e33-294d-58b8-826f-8c7231574bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305188} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a45c69-d0ad-5866-907b-58035013235b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50ec2ab5-76b6-51aa-84ab-6c9b886fff22" :question/math-academy-id "q-333375" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=3.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{21}{4}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({3}^{2})-2(9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 9+8\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 78 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{13}{2}=\\frac{41}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{41}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{41}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0785630f-c4a0-5eaf-8fa5-9797620d11b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450408} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae98ef-55c0-548c-b59c-d0d848ec2dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450408} :answer-field/id #uuid "992e87f4-b99e-55ec-9820-8e8617158600" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451158} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f97064-9aa9-5c08-beb8-e4b4a48d1d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450410} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae283f3-e1b3-5994-83d2-bc4f5f27c96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450411} :answer-field/id #uuid "309b3c61-7793-5746-a36a-8bc33cbee8b9" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b316ab-f347-5d0d-8694-c4f892fe0a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450412} {:answer/id #uuid "5c79fc57-b06e-544f-9ab7-6e4500203853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450413} :answer-field/id #uuid "257a5dca-a0c6-55a3-9d39-56ffe4b3b894" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc77eac4-c83e-5e6f-8c90-73674eb7fcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450414} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffdd7d9-0412-56f1-9b62-7b22d9d6d115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450415} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca4e6b7-f12b-5e4a-b82e-ba01c54504a0" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4be1bdfb-f8fa-566e-b0ec-d9b21f97e58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333398" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{8}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{3}y,\\,y(0)=6.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =6{(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{8}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.8$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$”; “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4232283d-ef4f-5093-9603-9b055cd7601f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305804} {:answer/id #uuid "45fd93c8-6f87-5160-8972-b02bf9a60f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305836} {:answer/id #uuid "5b41bf73-dc12-5668-a7ee-168afe51266a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+Δx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306027} {:answer/id #uuid "636445b1-b341-55b6-98e4-12e40e6470dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306114} {:answer/id #uuid "cf509c43-1335-5024-952d-2092c9886d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305804} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f0f3ad-6bff-586d-b7b8-40dff6f4b80c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10354088-c0e4-5ab7-aa11-decc50fd2a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-333498" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554a3f25-c6d4-56f1-97a1-e72f6e996868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450424} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf7d503-a537-5980-a459-e3d845c2a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2668b372-5058-5bcf-87d1-b3dc18aa9e3e" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d305cf-a373-552c-84d7-e16b441a5d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450426} {:answer/id #uuid "20b79c3e-cfe8-5182-8d84-c0226c536bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d7e34-4fe0-5a4b-be27-0a630b6ca736" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d4d-5653-50dd-8c45-7098cb48f439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450428} {:answer/id #uuid "05afd7a7-372e-5f9d-8fe6-0eaf11538d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caa9e2-7fc4-57e0-84db-0278682927f4" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75636e46-7f14-5059-a314-ce331ac05304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} {:answer/id #uuid "2a562ffb-8f60-5832-8dc2-4814364ea999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64ab8c0-b38a-58c0-8fc6-2259dd4a7a0d" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333572" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$” is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$”; “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6581fb-1cd9-5f47-8ff9-5979ac885085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} {:answer/id #uuid "523d5a9b-bdc7-5943-9f2e-bd9519abc022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b68c3fb-619e-551d-af87-e5d8bc615d9c" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883b44a2-bf01-5f77-a3ef-4667d3673879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450442} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d407e9-bdde-51d4-838c-250cb8be5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e6b0e4-794c-511c-a8b6-8176c4d57f20" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b751b7e-6ae0-51fb-a6ba-d823ccb81759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450444} {:answer/id #uuid "f0455e55-18d0-5a46-be37-2c94e8c15bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bd6593-a175-58b8-8df8-17cd965f7378" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d9c5801-9019-57ca-93c4-8d5e18dac042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} {:answer/id #uuid "03fa487c-d0a5-5d09-b69c-f51e9fd595b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e87aa0-6c37-57e0-9829-c738cc1d85a5" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55adb6e6-36a2-5272-9189-7161d38a92cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$” is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c140106f-5cc5-56fb-a07b-0c27c00c43f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{2x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306956} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c0ce59-7325-5b1e-8b75-a9c7ccd6bcf6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3fd41e67-ef00-554f-8117-753d53eaf907" :question/math-academy-id "q-333583" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({e}^{x})^{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ca0b38-8592-5e25-83db-db5b2fa7ed10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305180} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4618be-838e-5a48-97f2-3dedf2f0743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305268} {:answer/id #uuid "a459d91e-9a26-5fd9-b3a6-cb1ffb6f598d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{1}{5}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306699} {:answer/id #uuid "c09e5ec1-fc5c-5a7d-a6b2-a2be1020b63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{1}{5}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306948} {:answer/id #uuid "ef31755f-d02c-5d14-98cf-896d4a53e4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305180} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a23da73-a444-545d-9906-29a487bc2a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bfedde66-6790-5018-90d2-508742648cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333648" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y+2x,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ 5{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81dea119-18e3-5987-bf9b-8bac05ab7dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306392} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae9667d-f895-5cc3-a493-8eb9b0e79487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{69}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306476} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbb8fdb-1eb0-5ff9-a5fb-37ad57733adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306902} {:answer/id #uuid "bd18733f-0179-5771-873d-38d8cc2acceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306916} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f43d40-1142-5dea-a203-1a71cf717fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddac4028-9358-583c-89c1-83b1a59ad5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e92e1b49-1def-5e1c-b19c-ef83416fc895" :question/math-academy-id "q-333686" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6x-2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =6{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{7}{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x-2y \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{7}{3}+3=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =6{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{58}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{29}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{71}{18}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{71}{18}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca8f026-ee86-50b0-930b-37d693a6f098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{97}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305579} {:answer/id #uuid "35d1c6a8-b2e1-54f6-b378-a3364cb9f32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{109}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305668} {:answer/id #uuid "383627d1-70ce-58b0-8dd5-1154f6bea098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{121}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305689} {:answer/id #uuid "88fad9af-af24-545e-8172-f8712e211c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{105}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306461} {:answer/id #uuid "d5725f7e-ea38-5f9e-8506-e723217bda2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307131} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7dd41f-c1eb-557e-b294-de8e0b5098dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96f9e463-c5f3-54b5-a23c-48c898be91f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333687" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-10,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-10 \\\\  & =1-2-10 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{9}{2})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-10 \\\\  & =2-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=-\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-10 \\\\  & =3-\\left(-\\frac{17}{4}\\right)-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{11}{4}\\right)+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{123}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{41}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{41}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n| $3$ | $-\\frac{113}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-\\frac{113}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f38c6f-56a8-5851-b1b5-7f2ed760931f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305168} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad7f15a-33fe-5dc2-ae2f-1754937da348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305872} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebe2178-d6c2-5fc3-a295-c8c4ddb2939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306062} {:answer/id #uuid "b0489d82-6cef-5442-98bd-b9816410bd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306812} {:answer/id #uuid "d75de5f3-64a4-52e6-85ec-e7c5f318533a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306062} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f61863f-66d4-5938-9de5-9e7d5b9629e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cb7ed5f-cda5-5880-beb7-620f899a9264" :question/math-academy-id "q-333689" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈2\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =3{x}_{-1}-4{y}_{-1}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{11}{2}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-8)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}-4{y}_{p}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-6)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{59}{2}+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{11}{2}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{32}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ca6901-556b-5960-a3bd-8d0552587579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307252} :answer-field/id #uuid "09352193-2085-5ed0-9dac-3b45c1b2f367" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2961f6c2-30e1-5479-98d1-86e05fd1e48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305541} :answer-field/id #uuid "743ad797-c4e6-5a90-81e7-9503d1668174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eff0b088-3db3-5966-a1d7-4ea54d40635c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333715" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t) &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t) &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3a89e14-c8e0-5e44-a9b8-f3ac0f0360aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+3{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306972} :answer-field/id #uuid "01037f4c-7c37-5634-8e6c-20ee29f65e71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ba9ac3-1f20-5468-95ac-d8ea55e98b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307098} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b79de22-fe0a-53c0-a42e-6c9aaddfcd83" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "257aed5d-b769-55be-9201-2e8d67dfb051" :question/math-academy-id "q-333918" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $0.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3+3{e}^{-4t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $3-{e}^{-4t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a909763-5b8e-57e9-a134-f7912c30f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305708} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c56ea57-664a-585c-98e2-b7233665a4b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307566} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75d313cd-25b3-53fe-95e7-3a8ddb996348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306272} :answer-field/id #uuid "125f8328-549c-5b9a-9290-4156fb68434e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d259e94f-fd62-514f-881a-1710eb3fba54" :question/math-academy-id "q-333921" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-2$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ | $-2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t) &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t) &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325ae0b8-b12f-51fb-bf37-0ad049b140b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450448} {:answer/id #uuid "3f07c521-0c42-5feb-a2f9-a34c8e920b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e341d65f-3bb5-5181-bc1e-a5b09f7fbcec" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7130268a-ae24-5efa-a2ae-0809fb7aa80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450450} {:answer/id #uuid "18d1796c-d34c-50d6-a020-999c82bd213d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450450} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbf22b0-0965-5e9a-b603-6445eb41911b" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468f66ec-a0fc-5dc6-b336-b9e20bcaee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450452} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4e1c5b-7772-5cd1-a2eb-1eaf0ff1d57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450452} :answer-field/id #uuid "a422fa74-2d88-57d0-ad36-8b90134fee2b" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "334d336b-10d6-59d7-aef6-de8a06343ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450454} {:answer/id #uuid "ed16a682-5357-584a-afb8-68366e4bb05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450454} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb9ec87-bbc0-52e8-8de9-b614a3a68681" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d872692-c2e4-5ebe-a433-d44857eb60bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333930" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}|<1.$” is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "978033bf-6d3c-5fc0-9a25-a661031ac38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450456} {:answer/id #uuid "727e683c-c551-5aeb-b0fb-8f2c3d784e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450457} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c3637c-d804-5401-9392-eaf16c8e4c12" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd21065-4ea9-5ce2-8017-159c0a70bc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450458} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb2ddb5-a367-56d2-a946-7fc336dfab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450459} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67f1f28-b93a-58e2-b625-3f11d3fe6912" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6334c2bc-4740-5e7c-81ec-ebf66178be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450460} {:answer/id #uuid "48d1670c-d889-56be-9a1e-8f5ccf1dec4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450460} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff35c0ce-dadd-50b5-aae0-31384fdaefa4" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2e93bc-bab4-592a-a59a-128c7b393d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450462} {:answer/id #uuid "77b9ea2d-e848-5bec-b67a-2f543f11e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cab212d-a598-58be-a114-76dafe7042a6" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "632bafc0-a6e4-5e65-b220-22e1ed511764" :question/math-academy-id "q-333968" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$” is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$”; “The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d2dfbd-7daf-5241-a121-e3ce258283ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=cx+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305186} {:answer/id #uuid "3db00208-1184-520f-b504-088520ffcb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=cx+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305746} {:answer/id #uuid "462e1630-e7d2-5b44-a792-2d5c74e6f8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=cx+\\frac{1}{2c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305838} {:answer/id #uuid "9d33178f-320e-514b-9f41-3d0dbb7bb006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306618} {:answer/id #uuid "d12df33d-3ddc-5a4f-a7b4-a25aff857a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={c}^{3}x+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305186} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9eda539-dbe0-5d46-8cc5-f104e235305e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3082b563-6675-5fa7-b95b-17b8bbf9573a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334210" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\frac{1}{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\frac{1}{p})}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c\\ne 0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\frac{1}{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\frac{1}{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c\\ne 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6a00f9-258a-5c0b-bec9-2681d8b65214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305249} {:answer/id #uuid "10366825-a035-5b30-a3b5-bf55f3c5487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)x+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305299} {:answer/id #uuid "4c66e88a-5df9-5f29-8d5b-60080a11ac2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305887} {:answer/id #uuid "6706a73a-7cd8-51ff-8ce6-d9b8ea64f8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2c)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306133} {:answer/id #uuid "959ce7a9-9ef3-533b-9adb-0238705604a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=cx+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305887} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b673883-0eca-52ea-bad0-324aba2fc4ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "610c036d-c63c-589a-acb8-16947a6feeb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334211" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,{y}^{′}>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p))}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c>0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p),$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c).\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b642857-d5c7-5354-9582-e84b796c8d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305568} :answer-field/id #uuid "191d5551-a712-5140-91a3-ad273a6ad519" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfdf9ccd-6d68-52b8-90d2-de98adb98891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306941} :answer-field/id #uuid "69784144-c83c-5567-a773-771397a8e2ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff10c64d-19c8-5825-958b-92dcd06d6e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-334387" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t) &={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t) &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491bcea7-604e-509b-84a7-2a7ef2b97546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305862} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3d4145-687e-5558-90b1-dd695e53f467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306177} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea423d3-b31d-539a-a04d-0938105322e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3{\\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right)}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306366} {:answer/id #uuid "a52789ea-bc7a-5a0c-9009-d3ec390e0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306706} {:answer/id #uuid "e3105d73-1e15-5e75-9af6-10d80dda8afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77e2814-9dc4-5042-a222-52ea6cb26c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a61a3020-c49f-5adf-b3f7-a7d5dfccfd42" :question/math-academy-id "q-334402" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y &=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{4/3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a84bd6a-a252-5e93-80a3-faf6510a6ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305396} {:answer/id #uuid "1f256702-41a7-51cc-957d-dcf8872e4779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305452} {:answer/id #uuid "31327022-0aa2-5f4e-b3e4-1263d3c1bac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305615} {:answer/id #uuid "c08aa8a5-296b-5bac-b092-299552f0c4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306946} {:answer/id #uuid "d467b8c9-b766-5228-a539-d0e56a389bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307122} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8cb3285-6e61-5807-836e-520c3dc4dff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d9b898f-a218-5ae2-bad8-8bb9a7b3c046" :question/math-academy-id "q-334453" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\sqrt{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\sqrt{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}\\right)p+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{p}}+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{p}=-\\frac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}},$ we must have $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $\\sqrt{p}$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} {:answer/id #uuid "567f9cec-a4ac-5823-9ae9-ff55dac8a4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305989} {:answer/id #uuid "858bfa6f-4a26-5ea8-bf05-c82787e1d16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306424} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b03e9-0749-562a-b299-e3af23995ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨{2}^{t},\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307187} {:answer/id #uuid "f30b8be4-1b5a-550e-a1eb-f3bf87eff617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} :answer-field/id #uuid "843487b0-a154-5051-ab18-86349bd94dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "360e7d39-e582-5724-9fad-4481a2f3b45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33446" :question/problem "Find $(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ given that  $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨{2}^{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{2t}+1⟩$ and $v(t)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t+1}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{2t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}2⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}2⟩-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.222em}{2}^{2t}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}}⟩-⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{2}^{t+1}2\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{({2}^{2t}+1)\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}2}{{2}^{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d14831-aea7-5347-84f9-25f41978fb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305224} {:answer/id #uuid "637c5e5e-6ce7-5938-b14c-a011022a3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306115} {:answer/id #uuid "7f64699b-83aa-58d4-93aa-cd90febe5c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306371} {:answer/id #uuid "80bcb621-3c43-5efd-84e5-b398b7670b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306377} {:answer/id #uuid "cffb659a-2557-57d8-a5c5-5fc2ab177da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+2\\sqrt{5}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306371} :answer-field/id #uuid "e25a7c47-96dc-50c7-b0ca-55f528b99c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eafab8fd-4a82-53c3-b03e-eaa84e01b098" :question/math-academy-id "q-33457" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j+(t-1)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=2,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=2$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(2)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(2)+u{r}^{′}(2),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $r(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =({2}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}}\\,j+(2-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =2(2)\\,i+\\frac{(2)}{\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=2$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35cf3b2d-a8c1-5bd9-aca6-f502028c4513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305667} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5930ae-4735-5e73-94f1-709356f8b161" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d995775-d4b8-5be5-ae8b-0051f8760741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306623} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb53663-7d9c-5de6-86b9-c1a530a30928" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51cffc2a-3512-573b-9f6a-f449e54b28ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-334581" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ |  | ${e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t) &={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t) &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}=4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}), \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((3{e}^{4t}-3{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402bbdda-b251-5d94-875d-59e0a91fef09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305776} :answer-field/id #uuid "642c0d75-aba1-5dcc-9534-d6efe883c2ce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307840} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bdc05e-dd6d-5717-a1c9-5f11ff701e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{t}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305531} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c000d2-5a8b-5d66-b457-fa31db823b9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce10f4df-94a1-54d0-b0b8-734df6cd8ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334586" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $0$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2-2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{t}-1$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(0) &={e}^{0} \\\\ r(t) &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=1 \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the first equation, we immediately get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =-({e}^{t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}+2 \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2 & {e}^{t}-1 \\\\ 2-2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062e32d8-f989-5aa4-b581-550625b20a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-22y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305208} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3b4c00-c8c6-5a69-8d74-f0fb0986e66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+19{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306632} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb3aca4-57cc-5cfe-8857-492b5e5c5443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(19x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306937} {:answer/id #uuid "f8199f67-12eb-505c-a3d9-271dee3e4c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-21y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307449} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3bd0c7-cc7b-52ac-9f86-edb23a0868a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307479} :answer-field/id #uuid "316f40c7-3859-5761-af67-0b38d784b0c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74ef19a7-1756-5514-aa85-f55ea229ec0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334596" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-5y,\\,y(-2)=1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=5.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-5y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{27}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f9e5fe-6f2c-5f41-85b4-f23d7215f677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{1+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305535} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bf7d2-8b7c-5d1c-a628-b284f37c7a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-4xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305711} {:answer/id #uuid "815b7f24-ab96-538b-a118-0dd752229189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306386} {:answer/id #uuid "de2124f8-9248-5e6a-8e8c-5a046a989f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307212} {:answer/id #uuid "fded4afe-9e4a-5137-b197-e69ba9533eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307494} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1a481f-ba28-56c2-89bb-fc828949928e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e6fe7d5-1d57-5f56-ad69-d91395e9a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334603" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=3.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c78434-5644-5944-bd2b-237260943980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bb/bb3187e3229b7fc09f530035d1dffe0e8690e8056502ecb27fcb41c274b6b218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305388} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec31079-bc7a-5f3b-88e9-d69e4a13555b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe4863ed53356be2d5a2995d72372271544f55a86846bd0179dd82cf04844f58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305443} {:answer/id #uuid "34431ba8-0a68-53bd-a0cd-45d5ba70e3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/77/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305649} {:answer/id #uuid "495dcc98-fb5e-5f32-a772-e7718fa85ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305864} {:answer/id #uuid "e700c283-af6b-524a-bfa2-8f8e09efd37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b4/b47e03cb4429684acceac875e9c42875af53e0fe362125d2527bb62a24da1143.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305864} :answer-field/id #uuid "27456544-da12-5383-972a-30025449f0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aba52b16-35a3-5bbb-9f22-8bbebc7d0f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334624" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21964d84-a4b3-57c4-baef-7dc90590c3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305473} :answer-field/id #uuid "45384094-ffc6-5100-9d4a-f5e78cf975a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf913454-7ec3-55da-a766-ff44688e8cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307067} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2565be3-a2ee-5353-bc1b-991800facd5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29635f2f-538b-5717-bb55-2555552241c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334629" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 8{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(1+4\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(1-4\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((1+4\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(1+4\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(1+4\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ ({\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c1c3f8-e34c-5913-9027-40cbf9b38b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305677} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6401040-97c5-5632-9c58-3544640ae047" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40844993-f65c-5c72-af9c-ffdf6d82f4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305783} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb369d00-0a03-526e-b8a8-28162f18b93d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f28e1404-987d-53df-a0ad-e0b6d8de5b42" :question/math-academy-id "q-334630" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(-1+2\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(-1-2\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((-1+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(-1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(-1+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7694b518-7142-5675-a89b-c808f56e6519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6fa421-5689-5aa3-8796-026376a17eaf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6300be7f-13fc-51f9-a766-c305037c844a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306109} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a68fda-03fb-510b-ad4a-29ed331aec09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b84514fc-4c30-5d7a-9804-4a0f60502965" :question/math-academy-id "q-334631" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93c30d-56ef-5dbb-a2c3-12f0c02b1cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305891} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0da9f4-1a35-5132-acee-bff9e95cc05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a8d9528c878089370476a45aab127a416a11595ea0b0f972708e5161f208d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306355} {:answer/id #uuid "8a151970-06b2-5c13-989f-71d57aa7a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306471} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1b08d6-6709-5e77-8f04-4e1eb9a5d4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4c7528-85a6-56a0-a678-684c0edfdcb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fbf3cd8-344d-52ae-9b3c-567d393e20b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334638" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t \\\\ y &=9t+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee5b833-9c64-5e6b-aab0-da9f11cd3451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/19/19f9668a913eb5d108bf1043d23ba277035f619eca48f6d428856a5ba77b981b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305448} {:answer/id #uuid "3b15c480-edfe-542f-8483-6b2833fe4565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305713} {:answer/id #uuid "590a2be6-20b9-5f8b-abdb-aa47cd31c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306006} {:answer/id #uuid "daf1a0fb-c311-5d2c-bdd2-fb2a1cba88cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307178} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c08b-8d94-52a5-9594-a17424d77fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c73a9c-a0d1-5afe-aba6-629aee1682ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7a8620e-ad1d-5894-b095-0459bbd949b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334641" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t+3 \\\\ y &=2t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4t-3 \\\\ y &=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4d0cae-0177-5a5d-8631-f39b4951db48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450464} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aff59c-97a3-5e6f-875e-4e2796a585cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3cc60-3787-5506-84dd-27e14fcdcd91" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451186} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd2f5deb-038e-590b-b232-6e3475aa20c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c56064-c15b-5471-a1cb-2509a6b27af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67c096d-3014-5488-8e50-1ea90c5de739" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f89fa4-4b0f-54d4-9391-4960fd8fea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef8fa4-cb17-5725-b52e-611adaac83b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} :answer-field/id #uuid "22117b13-f654-5ed2-83f1-db1f9066dd85" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6c60236-078a-5017-8be7-a68ef13f931d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334844" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1651c86c-e436-5f01-be30-6f86f178c122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450470} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a0e20-73cf-5219-a0c9-9ab473ffb804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450471} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6216e9b-be5a-5902-a8e1-117bad30d0b9" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2da0-ea0d-5d43-8983-8c2c1ff56bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450472} {:answer/id #uuid "19e47932-b493-5280-a793-536c92023470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450473} :answer-field/id #uuid "582fe96a-f554-52fe-9758-256a3985f7c8" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0961aa7-98e8-54b0-95b3-2444bb78655f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450474} {:answer/id #uuid "d0413559-97a8-5a12-813d-f509aaf67bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450474} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db2b150-4480-5dbc-bcd0-3611fa5ed1c5" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3954ad07-64f7-58a6-b0b9-0080fcfa7904" :question/math-academy-id "q-334854" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used the fact that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1.$ Hence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, near $x=0$ we know that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≈x,$ so $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ behaves like $\\frac{1}{x},$ which becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$”; “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050291fd-ee09-542c-ab46-8146371d0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450476} {:answer/id #uuid "1f23d597-2b9c-5980-a71a-67034259bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450477} :answer-field/id #uuid "b67b015c-0d52-5094-8394-43e4b1410bf4" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e1ae7b-3409-51af-bd9e-b9c799d28850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450478} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b0c66-cec2-53e3-a134-a530d1cc5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450478} :answer-field/id #uuid "84be21d0-e1c3-59ea-ad50-63c0a062d31c" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d057d367-93e3-5241-bc29-cc4c19df0e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450480} {:answer/id #uuid "032dd146-059c-516c-a1e5-5a280946b148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450480} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99c49a4-cef0-5c95-8d1d-a868db0ce85a" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3acb3544-bfaf-54f0-ae53-b0963dc83190" :question/math-academy-id "q-334861" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb0517fc-c050-534e-a990-cc4dda2e3c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450482} {:answer/id #uuid "34a22a8d-3d1b-5063-a513-9c72fd120848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450483} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d5f3bc-8ca4-5618-9a0d-2fdd733d0ca0" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0a0722e-0bb4-5b67-ae9d-84488f8e751b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450484} {:answer/id #uuid "29db2d46-2c39-5d80-920d-89424783dd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450485} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0a4e8b-3053-583c-8d05-637d52abed8f" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "735251f3-87d9-5777-a6cb-53e0ffa193f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450486} {:answer/id #uuid "17455c85-e9c9-5ebd-83af-36798cbeae0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450486} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a83c5ca-a3a8-5f5b-9c37-aa3fd61ae062" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c936d65e-2929-5c4f-9009-0c5a872435df" :question/math-academy-id "q-334862" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$”; “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634a107e-b502-52e3-b357-5507484c968d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450488} {:answer/id #uuid "6a18e535-db65-5b3f-913c-76a3fd86c954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450489} :answer-field/id #uuid "416b39ea-7571-5da9-bdf4-bf9980ee5761" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451198} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8994cb-d1c9-5191-a7df-a0820f2790d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450490} {:answer/id #uuid "f6550471-6d05-53d7-a254-f3564aad3865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450490} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d3707-9fc6-58f8-8db5-1c2e0bf6ae97" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451199} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05390a72-4cd4-534a-8697-bb8cea4cf24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450492} {:answer/id #uuid "a64c5d18-bfb2-52b7-95a1-4fbbd8db588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450492} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c1b919-dc8b-5a41-944a-444d61fb38d1" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97b1feee-658e-5bb1-8630-22a1f9c057aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-334867" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1862779b-caaf-58df-ac29-4a353a61ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450494} {:answer/id #uuid "e4618381-61fb-5728-a336-cca2cb72a726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450494} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e236a3d-57cb-567e-a6c7-ee00f4ea65f9" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67b013d8-2df9-53c5-9f96-e3d4541c8d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450496} {:answer/id #uuid "e061dd77-53cf-5b84-9728-610b098807d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450497} :answer-field/id #uuid "e183d675-5292-55fa-ab4b-410cb5402c5d" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a60123-4572-54bb-aca2-076479b2b857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450498} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1006ae-9785-54f6-95e9-1934e4aa4177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450498} :answer-field/id #uuid "943bd999-bdf7-5cbf-8a4d-ec9b8615e8fc" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a21bb853-c833-53c0-be43-6d5a120a6d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-334870" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84c61c8-16be-5e54-9045-467f0f96ab47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddaf60a3-5af2-56a0-b59e-233e6e1a48a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4de1f09-9a29-5871-b400-9e62eb35e8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334935" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bcdb0d5-79f4-56bf-94f7-69ac4afa1b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306032} :answer-field/id #uuid "8837069b-abb9-5a7f-8ef2-31e56eb53d4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8ff8df8a-0811-5725-920c-fa5dffc48561" :question/math-academy-id "q-334937" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${Q}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nQ(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+0+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58eb2280-7b05-5589-991d-1b3794a7b311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t(4{t}^{2}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306005} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17de55c-f95a-50f0-a93d-4534d0192522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "acaa4683-e9fb-5e55-9654-30631f619f8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334939" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =4t({t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =t(4{t}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655594f5-3e88-5af8-852a-bf7725dca986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450506} {:answer/id #uuid "af8b35a9-bd96-5c0a-bca9-63d2085a82b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450506} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e65ed31-9f7d-5536-bc96-d2fe2b2a02a0" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88095e97-e57b-58f1-a53a-9f9f3a5700f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450508} {:answer/id #uuid "d5533cf1-080d-5f01-99c8-f67a63fb7b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450509} :answer-field/id #uuid "c71fd0a7-2a91-55a1-bd83-c156ef52d2e7" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a36a4786-2a36-569d-91ad-775d52af23ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450510} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a691a1-a252-556c-8c82-ec7793afb33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450511} :answer-field/id #uuid "681a004d-b000-5530-b882-21533ae00234" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28363017-b95e-52d8-9173-1894ab5a3d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-335019" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $”; “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c75eac-0251-58fc-9409-e3c4fbb0db53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466285} {:answer/id #uuid "679d0c57-b841-573a-a685-4a8622782fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466286} :answer-field/id #uuid "68da005c-225d-5113-8d8b-13cea0f5abd0" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83972ef9-8c66-548b-beca-5689ea6208b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466287} {:answer/id #uuid "1c51d4c0-55aa-5641-9e5b-281e3384a135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466287} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0a24d0-4e27-5bdf-becf-739116d3d53d" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecadbcf1-48ad-521b-9da0-d951f5ab919a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466289} {:answer/id #uuid "1803bd10-5f24-5896-8105-fbd58c0288ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466289} :answer-field/id #uuid "3273f403-4f50-5518-ab19-640d36d78393" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e125563-3523-5218-87f9-08cecc055fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335020" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afaf95ab-562c-570a-9fc7-33d481908db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450512} {:answer/id #uuid "542f1287-5eaa-54dd-900e-0a4b5ba1d1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450512} :answer-field/id #uuid "019af7bf-aaea-5206-b91e-2f5513edf838" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de502797-dc92-55f2-b0fd-c2329e1cb1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450514} {:answer/id #uuid "3e521fd8-00e9-557d-9787-be6edce426dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450514} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2f259f-4113-5766-8761-09554a66ee37" :answer-field/key "$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95099cd-c612-592c-bf97-a4d14a2abf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450516} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaa7024-50f9-52da-9c5c-4e1c662943b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450517} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f43a077-220d-5862-8069-79971460cfa0" :answer-field/key "$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38e1f503-d528-5681-bd45-38417a3415be" :question/math-academy-id "q-335048" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only."} {:answer/id #uuid "05e90167-98bc-5494-9877-95fbe03661c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308795} {:answer/id #uuid "062343fa-a7e4-5446-adf9-0e582f17946e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308798} {:answer/id #uuid "06387ffc-be0e-5627-bfad-697c32df3dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308799} {:answer/id #uuid "0bab6fed-c38d-5347-ae71-c7210567374e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308829} {: