{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310675} {:answer/id #uuid "fc13a552-61c6-552f-9a18-b4b5b329247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51220cfe-c354-523d-8523-5451a12da282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309362} {:answer/id #uuid "56d7157a-d5f4-5554-9056-49e6de0bdfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1,0)$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309402} {:answer/id #uuid "9a201dcf-b9c6-5100-8e54-377fdd0130e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1,{e}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309989} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a7a750-f514-5110-b363-11ca977cedf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310076} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e8ba54-cc30-5aff-919b-5699852c35a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310703} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b816a3-6ca8-5bc2-a569-dfe4101abb7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a2edb181-6dbf-5979-80f4-12f00da0e0f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-33517" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} &=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t} &=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1} &=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4332b724-0400-5630-964e-201ec1ca6fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309256} {:answer/id #uuid "5143fe24-d030-572b-a92b-847e07a5e317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309363} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f0502-4184-5e94-8cd7-684cdf4cb1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310027} {:answer/id #uuid "db4f970d-9eef-504c-863f-23a1fee33248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310505} {:answer/id #uuid "e5203d5d-e4cc-53da-af11-7ac0b9f2d16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0aa9e4b-44d3-5851-82d9-a6bf32d35ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cef4bb70-8f2a-5e75-916b-a71e12c54640" :question/math-academy-id "q-33525" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\right)⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}[-t⟨1,t⟩+(1+{t}^{2})⟨0,1⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-t,1⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩}{\\frac{1}{2}}=⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e2b089-092a-5eca-9f7e-bf626ffedb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} {:answer/id #uuid "71425229-5e0c-56e4-a3c5-6da6bfb56a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x/2})={e}^{x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309627} {:answer/id #uuid "cd378351-ace0-5392-8e0c-b064ad6a0d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right))=-\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "e71cd363-dab5-57e2-b1a7-f9cdab639247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right))=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310584} {:answer/id #uuid "f08a16be-ff29-5897-9836-28db90a5052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03a098b-971d-55f6-a30b-4a30fe70ddf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dba525fe-7e4d-5319-88bd-1b806feb0904" :question/math-academy-id "q-335278" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2}) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a488da-8074-50a9-ba1d-c7e0f32bf719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308780} {:answer/id #uuid "64a5c638-4dbb-57eb-a6fb-248e76429030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309522} {:answer/id #uuid "893e7fae-64e2-57b6-af19-e61e904ea8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309829} {:answer/id #uuid "9e515d8a-f8c4-5ea9-9b59-674573d38b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308780} :answer-field/id #uuid "785b77fb-6634-5289-8f5e-c7972e2ca683" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223e604f-882b-578c-9005-65a6a468f7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309006} {:answer/id #uuid "3a151fdc-c615-56f8-af30-2dacf342942e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309195} {:answer/id #uuid "7b511ce9-9c0e-54e8-8e7e-93916e342c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309711} {:answer/id #uuid "ad26b27d-2be0-5935-829c-fe759d649c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309711} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c99e1a2-52bb-5b04-85f5-ed344de385bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d36870f2-83c3-53eb-b9c4-cfdc50550717" :question/math-academy-id "q-335409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int {u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u=x,{u}^{′}=1,{v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c590c3-891b-511b-9e8d-1e0317eac036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309024} {:answer/id #uuid "c4250262-bc93-5a7e-a34a-2623ae9d7561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310322} {:answer/id #uuid "c82f0da4-6a92-5001-89a2-66f34618a0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310355} {:answer/id #uuid "ee20a83a-6373-5864-bf59-eeaad3c5fb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310322} :answer-field/id #uuid "26833fad-1674-5264-a99a-1ab167ee5815" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34e87914-2491-5ea9-986c-2fa933b9f42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309150} {:answer/id #uuid "93802e8a-2438-526b-9013-756c85306841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309928} {:answer/id #uuid "cc68d1a8-cb75-5053-8d7d-c7bbccf33bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310385} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f5849-731e-572b-9350-7951b8593415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310405}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309150} :answer-field/id #uuid "842dfce6-3596-5e08-aa3d-f4ed3e02c6ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "039312c7-7497-5736-bf10-d497d1c61328" :question/math-academy-id "q-335412" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51737088-92ab-5b29-82ac-e537449f6038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309365} {:answer/id #uuid "526f6ddb-2bfd-5535-a784-04786b110605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309371} {:answer/id #uuid "942a8856-2dbb-5570-a5b2-9a4b4bd533d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309935} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf600f-9098-5544-a753-cf5c4c16901e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310160} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cd2225-859d-5768-a4c5-fd53001d89c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310353} {:answer/id #uuid "e4182b96-4008-5be0-9afe-5aa7f2c38e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310353} :answer-field/id #uuid "2393a2aa-c8c4-5a83-a2fd-ff31c63108e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ac71c1-9691-57e3-ad15-f4a2c6b20618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309134} {:answer/id #uuid "81d7d041-69c2-5839-92f7-6b1c4224f660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309768} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f58873-ecae-5051-bc88-e8764c3c7081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310435} {:answer/id #uuid "e762e550-6658-5f15-828a-3868f4d37a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310586} {:answer/id #uuid "ee37eb73-9d47-5ca2-8a76-bc2c951671c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310648} {:answer/id #uuid "f0caa4a5-ee28-5e7b-94e6-3ce6faaf3686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309134} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe8c336-3df7-5f13-b0a1-fe8f6f92474c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5654d7cc-291e-531f-8658-88df66ff75f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3 and L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309398} {:answer/id #uuid "92ca5b8b-7dd0-59bd-8e00-109dfcb8cc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309398} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dde75f7-bdda-5074-a948-2e754faae839" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bce4b16-e3a4-5348-9b23-bad945e1873f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309716} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2866fc-bc4d-52f8-a82f-a329ad175d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309727} {:answer/id #uuid "b635707a-e2a5-5834-9ed1-977b517f6c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309716} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3815c6-86ba-5b1b-8cae-ec097bb7034c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172bcc96-0511-568d-93d5-02dd842fe19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308928} {:answer/id #uuid "4f22f86e-db07-50a9-ba4f-3e7c66bc3bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309349} {:answer/id #uuid "70719a3e-27ba-591a-8f36-1481d2e108a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309620} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e67a54-a1b1-5bf1-8639-de55c1eb37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310568} {:answer/id #uuid "e8704c4a-06a4-5550-844c-30942e45edc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310595} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7424e5-fcc6-5a10-9795-fd29c7199ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310568} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40fb636-0bef-5c56-b7fe-be072b8cced7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ee0e09f-2dc3-5d79-8a91-2daee5f981be" :question/math-academy-id "q-335462" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{L1}:\\,f=O\\,(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1})\n$$  \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{L2}:\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}=O\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,f=O\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,\\frac{1}{x}=O(1)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O\\,(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O\\,(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1})\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}=O\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L2}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}=O(1).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L3 and L4}$ and $\\text{the transitive property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05604222-454c-56d7-a398-18b08b4d936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308787} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b59bcd4-4bc4-5887-8461-5bbff4532e24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f5b5d0-6916-5b5a-bd55-63f930855a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309385} :answer-field/id #uuid "8395dd63-658e-5d77-b4a5-a61647e8baae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0750b1fe-1a76-56e4-9c4e-23cb062aa34d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335713" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=3t{e}^{-2t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+3t(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(3-6t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (3-6t){e}^{-2t} & <0 \\\\ 3-6t & <0 \\\\ -6t & <-3 \\\\ t & >\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bab6fed-c38d-5347-ae71-c7210567374e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308829} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b6102-1988-5f44-921b-dab11741888b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309101} {:answer/id #uuid "3171d56f-a010-58c5-9741-0926362161c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309122} {:answer/id #uuid "33c66e55-e3c5-5aa9-a78e-57027777e26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309143} {:answer/id #uuid "82fe0cee-6b18-5b50-ba64-a1713c165400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6736f81-1f81-5c62-8e0a-cc99c0f20ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b1aa988-c2c7-5b7c-8814-04c3301ea2f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33581" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2217ec13-aeca-54c6-b184-b9c6f12eca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309005} {:answer/id #uuid "278ab869-b3ab-5209-b251-49e30ed08f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309045} {:answer/id #uuid "cb884bde-aa7d-5ea3-8269-75c1c85782d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310378} {:answer/id #uuid "e304072c-14d0-5b9a-91ef-e7c21ca3967c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310563}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310378} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4b8151-a2dd-5d21-b3a8-5f1786ce72f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51d8ba46-b7ec-5aeb-9c2e-92c427b44105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309368} {:answer/id #uuid "6e835f28-6c17-5099-9e1d-5557f103db76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309601} {:answer/id #uuid "784a4e7f-4997-59d7-9dbf-2d44fdbb9535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309692} {:answer/id #uuid "88322c85-406a-50de-beb7-e95ff2a7c6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309817} {:answer/id #uuid "f316da64-a8b2-5ef5-a90e-929545648a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309692} :answer-field/id #uuid "9edac9dd-3740-53a2-adc8-4f1b9b142575" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb60ed14-2886-5407-9edd-93c36aa77da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335811" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2y}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},\\,y(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$ \n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\pi )=0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e70df3-e69a-52ce-adaf-9d80576fc124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309306} {:answer/id #uuid "4de60ca3-08eb-5908-84e7-337d050635cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309339} {:answer/id #uuid "60127d29-07b2-5438-b25c-538c97234290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309472} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e91b0c-4363-5464-927a-584f15d25ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310236} :answer-field/id #uuid "964f9aee-69e3-5eaf-ba62-46327eb55e3f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fd4c10d-4104-533e-a7ea-701ca41dc419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309354} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab118d1-7dd3-5f85-b172-3299a04f369d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309705} {:answer/id #uuid "976ecd48-d13c-560a-802a-8e7cf948da05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309962} {:answer/id #uuid "de8dc48e-6d1c-5472-ac5b-8b94b9419130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309962} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba4b49a-a123-5ea4-a04f-61aaf7f9c4c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3bbe03fd-0d2c-564c-9b93-a7997b3f7696" :question/math-academy-id "q-335812" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yx & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2C}{\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb28f63-32ee-571f-8877-c835cd4f56ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310380} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f5fe28-db94-5b9e-bd5b-b6ede1ab4c22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2ed58bc-a791-5e8b-aac9-35b2a977d20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335848" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(5,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 7.84).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 7.84)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 7.84) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{7.84-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P\\,(Z\\ge \\frac{2.84}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a5bf63797840ba4b457ef4f8564b979e106fa916b2247937733157830d6f443d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-P(Z<0.71) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.71) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.71)=0.7611.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-0.7611 \\\\  & =0.2389.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 7.84)=0.2389.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f2d7fd-5094-5680-9bcc-916bf2c37008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309491} :answer-field/id #uuid "af026408-855b-5831-90ed-4b2ee1e6ef32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84bc16ec-d799-5442-844b-2ae98dc27092" :question/math-academy-id "q-335886" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(-2,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge -4.48).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge -4.48)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.48) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.48-(-2)}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/25/2547a2e847c503d8cdf6c2df258edd7c51e1478185f2cb0055f72425ed6fd04e.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-P(Z<-0.62) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.62) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.62)=0.2676.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-Φ(-0.62) \\\\  & =1-0.2676 \\\\  & =0.7324.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.48)=0.7324.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c1338cf-8044-51ee-baee-9eecf733f7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309327} :answer-field/id #uuid "8474bbc0-9389-51db-8946-30aed9c23d3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "017068dc-43b7-5a74-9e0e-ac9e2605f2f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335887" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(8,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1.88).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1.88)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =8$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1.88) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1.88-8}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c12f91dda20714f4b97a061a52d760249c0f9d9ce5029343af3e4c216981f43d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-P(Z<-2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.04)=0.0207.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-Φ(-2.04) \\\\  & =1-0.0207 \\\\  & =0.9793.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1.88)=0.9793.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311638 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea01709-12b4-58a6-ab2e-4962013826ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309345} :answer-field/id #uuid "31904c2b-2a8f-532b-964d-ea9d4fd7f84e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a594be-b7bf-590a-ba97-77efa196cdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309240} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dce419-ace0-5455-9896-3d1050329377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "21ef4a3a-3143-5d28-9b7b-5c36663b99e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-335917" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\ln \\,({t}^{2}),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\ln \\,({t}^{2})=2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(2t)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,({e}^{-3/2}) & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\ln \\,({e}^{-3/2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(-6+2) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t={e}^{-3/2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4{e}^{-3/2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606ae851-a7d5-58a3-8e79-a9296cf345d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}t+t\\operatorname{ln}t{csc}^{2}t}{t{cot}^{2}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478} :answer-field/id #uuid "d339cd93-b3fc-51f1-96cc-794e009be99e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "123379b4-b283-5d57-933b-9e195e37586e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336130" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}.$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t(-{\\csc }^{2}⁡t))}{{\\cot }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{\\csc }^{2}⁡t}{t}\\right)}{{\\cot }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{\\csc }^{2}⁡t}{t{\\cot }^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04802603-7504-5546-a3be-3c58893aa391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308779} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da0e59-e758-50ad-b223-814077cc3178" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16287804-f34d-52ea-a18f-36c3f78a5bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336415" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}.$ Calculate the acceleration $a$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3{t}^{2}-5t+2}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+\\frac{2}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+2{t}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+2(-{t}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be0c5a2c-a74b-5512-8f9f-977ca51bb45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310284} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae1f409-352b-5339-a220-ca5b3458dfc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71d0f7a0-435c-5051-8dc1-239c5e6adc49" :question/math-academy-id "q-336452" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=5+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5+3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =6t-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "692a0e65-4fd6-5027-bf45-6a8661eb9fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{12}{(t+2)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309558} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35bd720-34b8-5509-b012-eaf669e13a40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff489b9d-3c9a-550f-95cc-a5eab9d34477" :question/math-academy-id "q-336455" :question/problem "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{6}{t+2}.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+\\frac{6}{t+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+6(t+2)^{-1}) \\\\  & =2t-6(t+2)^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-6(t+2)^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+12(t+2)^{-3} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{12}{(t+2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1d1fdd-a6f6-5a38-a3df-d7b2a2583f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308987} {:answer/id #uuid "3345984a-6431-5bc2-8a0b-3b698dfa4caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309139} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe0a0a5-7a27-538d-a1c1-12e36b2b78a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309470} {:answer/id #uuid "74254c1b-ae29-507e-9365-aa3345a018e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309649} {:answer/id #uuid "77f37899-ebfb-5a89-8ed1-b0406f53dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309649} :answer-field/id #uuid "9756b53b-26a7-53d0-942d-24a3a520cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e09c97e7-cdca-5648-a0b0-ca6d528dfc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-33655" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ at the point $P(1,1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,4)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4} &=1 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1 \\\\ 4t &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, we get $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(1,1,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3},\\,2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2086e10e-bf6b-570b-9e79-ede9a646fe9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308994} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb11ed9-985f-544c-8920-3388c7a6519f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309444} {:answer/id #uuid "9168ec64-3a53-5b16-9c62-1d367dbacf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309912} {:answer/id #uuid "aa550866-db3a-5d4c-8873-53cc1c2abea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310125} {:answer/id #uuid "fc13a552-61c6-552f-9a18-b4b5b329247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310125} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3a2c81-3ab1-5fd1-97e5-0673d9615ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "730871e0-d608-50a9-8e24-089b6fc0a471" :question/math-academy-id "q-33676" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve   $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t⟩$  at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\cos }^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b83fcff9-b155-591f-95ee-0c33ecb6e183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310238} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a54632-3d44-5eb8-80f1-61e506b9cc79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43329e32-a27f-5472-a41e-d6bebac9271e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336777" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e57333-adfd-5f69-ab27-c8e5ee35309b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310480} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afeaa15-4597-5565-9d95-3bd8ba39fdee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "73bc5fc3-eca8-5d40-a62e-f3da22b5f1ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-336787" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}]^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =3\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =3\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07651448-d3a1-59f2-804c-9a576380c519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308803} :answer-field/id #uuid "26d47c9c-db94-58fd-b360-358c1d01422a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "89613f87-fd71-5736-9c29-f319bb743ec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336847" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(10.64<X<18.28).$ Use the tables below and do not round your answer.\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ |\n\n$P(10.64<X<18.28)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =10$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10.64<X<18.28) & =P(\\frac{10.64-10}{4}<Z<\\frac{18.28-10}{4}) \\\\  & =P(0.16<Z<2.07).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1c1e8b01372821c4ad13bfb1bbfd2a102bb9d247815ace25219d3a3f42c8c2.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =P(Z<2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.07) & =0.9808 \\\\ Φ(0.16) & =0.5636\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16) \\\\  & =0.9808-0.5636 \\\\  & =0.4172.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(10.64<X<18.28)=0.4172.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f2f378-eb72-5c62-8182-25b40fc6899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309959} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ea573e9-cc46-59d4-a3ef-9d8a976e9488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "045b900e-4eea-59c7-bdae-6b9edf672c1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336901" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{′}}{[16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{(\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{′}}{{[4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-2(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16490572-a595-5eca-89ee-f34d90a62a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308915} :answer-field/id #uuid "a061c995-608d-52a7-82b0-3c42c5bd3c61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "564f761a-7e2a-5b6b-bf32-97c437e743f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336935" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\ln \\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\ln \\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\ln \\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\ln \\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}^{′}}{{[(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbb1a690-f279-59bd-bef1-b9ae5aafa638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310759} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bfd36e-fd1a-5b0a-9401-e41918cdd7ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6c4283c-1494-5085-aa47-dfd444fd55a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336939" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{1+5x}-5)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[(1+5x)^{-1}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-5(1+5x)^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5f68ef-d727-52bd-89bf-4cb90b51c0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310266} :answer-field/id #uuid "278980b3-e458-50e7-8ec2-83ce5dd3752b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59aff076-adbf-565d-893c-4346918216f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336951" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9331c81-ff9d-5d11-b893-60225fefb1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310362} :answer-field/id #uuid "61795570-e649-56ea-a05f-e23fb8db81cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "89774f8b-821d-5751-b593-620b5d6a8b80" :question/math-academy-id "q-336953" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10x+\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b05083-12f2-5a20-8aca-84fd66aa9248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309653} :answer-field/id #uuid "7624abe6-7ac7-5d64-b518-c5556f604b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f9578a7-a572-5de7-8775-0a0de428b652" :question/math-academy-id "q-336956" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3x+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6\\sqrt{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1510a419-b68b-5921-a544-0d4dbafbdc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308905} :answer-field/id #uuid "3071a965-7372-57d4-bb77-a5096be409d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b7103803-bbea-5f32-96b2-ff2da6b72265" :question/math-academy-id "q-336966" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a95d1c-4a95-5dbc-8830-347e0ded3e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308765} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba21cdd-1f15-567a-ad8a-dea6d61ce3e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbfa9584-4222-59e9-9ff2-135e4dc942f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336967" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}}{2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}}{2+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/2}}}{2+\\frac{5}{4{x}^{3/2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fc6787-11bc-5201-8dcc-06b42932f745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}(-