-09f7-5fdc-a5a9-a014f70307bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309053} {:answer/id #uuid "428bddbc-7fe1-57a9-b19a-9be2149863bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{d}x-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309053} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51be4dc-70f6-5f93-83cb-4da4f3682b59" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31620a73-9199-59c5-9292-3957d93c8c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309120} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4f2e5f-1bbf-57a2-9854-0768e88af997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309607} {:answer/id #uuid "a01bf326-7cf7-50ef-828d-7c4d8fc369ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310043} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d9ff67-6eb4-5d9b-aee1-713423b5e5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310596} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea40afce-65ce-51ba-a0f9-c3502feadf74" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a976c29-035c-5d40-acc5-bb9e8d96a10e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337161" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=4xy+3x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-(4xy+3x))=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{1}(-4x-0) \\\\  & =-4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -4x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec89f1f6-45a5-5a1b-8562-827597bfc975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8694" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2393c25-add6-5ed0-b887-c64f0ed4d677" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "128be29f-0fce-5c4c-b26d-cd4d316c4e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337164" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5,{10}^{2}),$ compute $P(-18.4<X<16.7).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0071\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0071\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n\n$P(-18.4<X<16.7)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18.4<X<16.7) & =P(\\frac{-18.4-5}{10}<Z<\\frac{16.7-5}{10}) \\\\  & =P(-2.34<Z<1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82e4ea3088176bbd9b03b7baa6aaa9389f21f9e9a286fa223d47bfae4252ccb6.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =P(Z<1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =Φ(1.17)-Φ(-2.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.34) & =0.0096 \\\\ Φ(1.17) & =0.8790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =Φ(1.17)-Φ(-2.34) \\\\  & =0.8790-0.0096 \\\\  & =0.8694.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-18.4<X<16.7)=0.8694.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70448cbf-3686-5d76-a3d4-d2bd4570140e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309616} :answer-field/id #uuid "6654ca3a-e013-5a81-a963-81309c7e84f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7f99ed7-c5c8-5daf-9d23-22c23ed39141" :question/math-academy-id "q-337167" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<13.5).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9599\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9678\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<13.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =3$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<13.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.5-3}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e024a5ac6bdd86c88c6ed6cbe3a647281c63e6934c3830288fa01c1d51997e4a.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.75)=0.9599.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<13.5)=0.9599.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d356bc26-57d5-59ea-80f6-ad3d535a1609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36t-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310448} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e3e1f1-67f2-5abf-8891-7159f370a35a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ed0b294-0a14-518d-b751-a90930d00a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337287" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}\n$$ |  | $30t-26$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 3{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}27(t-1)+(3t+1) \\\\ 27(t-1)+3(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}30t-26 \\\\ 36t-24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65022199-6a0a-59fa-b71e-95b9b1843201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528} :answer-field/id #uuid "92650cbf-bf86-5f3b-a335-e3e58d6d03f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eaf12e35-a3fc-5fd0-814c-953ea7b38802" :question/math-academy-id "q-337307" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$30t+20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fe2ea9-ad09-5630-af64-22131020c4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904} {:answer/id #uuid "84dd9d79-839b-52a2-b19f-2d5cbfbb46f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309783} {:answer/id #uuid "96485065-3293-5ce0-ab20-2103330aa021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309951} {:answer/id #uuid "f828c22e-b382-55e9-826b-56e8a50f3e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904} :answer-field/id #uuid "32615465-7010-53c6-8614-e7498764c988" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7189b501-6981-5d8e-b86b-ce4d54a97351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309630} {:answer/id #uuid "