[{:answer/id #uuid "047944f3-d362-5653-9c31-1326ae272f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308778} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc8cecd-6963-516f-8661-be9df221ebe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309747} {:answer/id #uuid "86381135-6cef-5057-81a2-b74315d11145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309799} {:answer/id #uuid "9eab5566-ffab-5b1a-a066-7f89ba1e4307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310028} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16c3297-8e94-5309-b982-bcfe725a491c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eff5820-e0e1-557f-ba66-9b4633b38e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309106} {:answer/id #uuid "6944ac20-9fac-51b7-8527-ffeba8049c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309561} {:answer/id #uuid "9281fa81-650d-5a52-a326-5c9c50de4b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309916} {:answer/id #uuid "e25d4223-3f88-51ed-8c8d-632419954a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309106} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2815c8-eadc-5a5e-87c5-e32edcb77a09" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ff77e1b-8c3d-580c-a6d9-d90a052f53dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337523" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\hspace{0.167em}{y}^{'}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+6y)=6 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}3x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{3x}(6-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f063173-e6fe-5d2a-99b7-378065d2b1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308984} {:answer/id #uuid "40cef204-8013-5a17-b8d9-ae936e8062e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309242} {:answer/id #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} {:answer/id #uuid "67a87526-29fc-5bf6-8e12-907f098b41ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309542} {:answer/id #uuid "8a51098b-f1b1-5736-8996-e4133727d23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b23d5d-5883-57bd-8005-c893ed0c9ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0163f286-fc30-51ed-907e-500011ffd762" :question/math-academy-id "q-337541" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a43b3012-16cb-5208-bab4-c20770b3f2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310071} {:answer/id #uuid "db134198-ef62-58eb-9844-bd15199bb96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/valu