 :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310217} :answer-field/id #uuid "93da18b2-0f04-5d92-8287-4db5128efec2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f705cdc-41c2-5f9a-8445-edd919c31cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309228} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0481b1-b497-58f4-b105-3f3d55abce0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309878} {:answer/id #uuid "fa34ecd1-5439-525f-9135-2393396f1316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c8a9a3-d29e-566e-98ae-8919fefcf054" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e90167-98bc-5494-9877-95fbe03661c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308795} {:answer/id #uuid "062343fa-a7e4-5446-adf9-0e582f17946e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308798} {:answer/id #uuid "26a3a9dd-860b-5c80-af07-44e21596f9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309037} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a1317-f7f4-5800-95ee-65866a9a09b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62f2e9c-b823-5d73-8121-225ae6240239" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43a2c42c-804d-5119-bec4-269847a33364" :question/math-academy-id "q-337619" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{3})=6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xy)=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}-(-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2y}\\right) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}(2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dd3513-0642-5de4-be16-d4bc511b2663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308782} {:answer/id #uuid "40109527-2da6-5399-9922-7e9d518987c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309237} {:answer/id #uuid "609d8f66-0ba6-5f9a-8e8f-b8027b9814d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309483} {:answer/id #uuid "e126aa57-9a75-5745-b52d-fda7d4fa7971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309237} :answer-field/id #uuid "878292fb-d89c-5d5b-a679-78ab004bc741" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bad373-7338-58aa-906e-eb34efc7bf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309513} {:answer/id #uuid "a9003b3f-7a31-5040-b685-09977b462248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310114} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fcd743-a7be-56d3-bd31-82e8db685454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310427} {:answer/id #uuid "d22db97e-e112-5d17-a5e0-7ec914407346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310427} :answer-field/id #uuid "df51da9d-27f4-5dcc-802f-21eb4fe9d87a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24f77cc5-eae7-5d74-9490-65584fc368cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-337637" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}[(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two expressions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055e1fc8-915a-592f-849d-e505bc5d7841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=12+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308786} {:answer/id #uuid "075f7d5c-29bb-5c01-9e06-079d3b73be05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=12-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308802} {:answer/id #uuid "076651f6-c3a1-5d95-b6eb-5ad4ea6151e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=9-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308804} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fdeba2-9381-5542-8f53-6c63631fe3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=12-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310344} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6bc4fb-eefa-5060-9137-7ddd78466ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=4-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308802} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f0696b-8f7a-5234-be84-d44c7b4f12b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1473c225-873e-51f5-9bb2-6f7541afad6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337654" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, let $u=\\frac{t}{3}.$ Then $t=3u$ and $\\text{d}t=3\\,\\text{d}u,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (3u){e}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\int u{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9(u-1){e}^{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back $u=\\frac{t}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =9(\\frac{t}{3}-1){e}^{t/3} \\\\  & =(3t-9){e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(3t-9){e}^{t/3}+C \\\\  & =(-4t+12){e}^{t/3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx=-4t+12+C{e}^{-t/3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+12-4t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=12-4t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43db9c4f-09ac-5726-abb0-9c3e10295f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309264} {:answer/id #uuid "53fc2dd4-6677-53b0-8afa-2eaf1f098593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309386} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98b50f-aa8f-56a7-ae48-c3e43c87eb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309704} {:answer/id #uuid "8628dabe-0b8d-579d-8578-57c1c49dd7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309797} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e2b971-981c-5a94-aaf6-a5af90c69723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309264} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a52627-2e7d-50f9-b71f-c481a2332973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59e9cb29-7d37-5d4c-9754-fdc1e96d8fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337683" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t=t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{2}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|}=(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =1-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =1-\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad280c2-db33-5b17-8ea2-ac18b43898e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=5-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309197} {:answer/id #uuid "6323f61a-bb37-510a-aa62-63053e089258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=4+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309505} {:answer/id #uuid "88e42f9a-67a2-580a-b86f-cdef663d7ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=4-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309824} {:answer/id #uuid "a614d826-7900-576d-a893-62dc32ac2a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=3-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310088} {:answer/id #uuid "e073e492-94b9-5f73-aee1-96e918e47740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(t)=4-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310545}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310545} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42c0423-10e1-55a2-bb37-ebd1a4704761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f34ae243-5d21-54e5-a082-840e708d60ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-337699" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =(1-3t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =(1-3t){e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & ={e}^{t}-3(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =4-3t+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+4-3t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=4-3t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffdd0a1-0a35-5b24-99ea-94b9973f954a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309235} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd31a46-ae29-5e01-a12b-8c7a675b7c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309325} {:answer/id #uuid "55b44314-7d8e-55e8-8a7d-181fa35e6a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309393} {:answer/id #uuid "c764cc9b-fcda-5258-add1-27d9639340d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310351} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ab2035-ec62-5df4-b79d-b94f2c4b9423" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ebc0024-5280-5498-a8be-7ad22bc3c12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answe