orrect {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308832} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5c1f11-3986-5bce-b93f-72a6cd32d426" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2815bc26-aa2e-5adc-93b5-1a5a01b7bd55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309051} {:answer/id #uuid "546e5487-01e9-5357-ad32-b0a780b686c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309387} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceed58ee-7e74-5566-9183-8298849cbe94" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8bb4f5-708c-5d4a-9cfe-1eb53003f2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308857} {:answer/id #uuid "14372060-3cf8-5326-af5d-62a0565b9558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308896} {:answer/id #uuid "3997fb81-d70b-5b9b-bca3-623b90b84aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309186} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d0fa66-65bb-55c2-b1d1-a4eb9b43199c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309186} :answer-field/id #uuid "deec0c55-c23d-5ed6-9f01-5868ac0b477e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c34cbae-eabd-5c38-b4a3-0b29397c07e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337739" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+b{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2}+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & +a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot (2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+{y}_{1}]+b[{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+b{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d27930c-602c-5196-8866-cd94b48075b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309214} {:answer/id #uuid "49999dc7-4350-52b8-af49-7230a7888bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309310} {:answer/id #uuid "8de384a9-4726-5f03-852e-53a28139357b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309875} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9b82b5-0a53-5eae-86bb-448bd0919ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310037} {:answer/id #uuid "b9adb670-f1ea-5eb4-861d-2023e15f0fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309875} :answer-field/id #uuid "90aca743-2d45-595f-ae09-1f6cff33b494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d419628-3d10-5b6a-acc5-bd80bf2e3d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-337830" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=3,$ and ${a}_{0}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{2x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})-2\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{2x})+3\\cdot (2{e}^{2x})-2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2x}+6{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =8{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ece56a7-2b9e-5963-82f3-afd35b2ef55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308852} {:answer/id #uuid "18bbe9be-2d41-5c1c-b79c-cf4637c7fbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308946} {:answer/id #uuid "827f3d8f-9357-5033-a261-d291e7358e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309771} {:answer/id #uuid "b33023be-155f-5252-ad31-05ef1a857408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310197} {:answer/id #uuid "e53ddad0-75e6-5572-8437-d745ea97b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308852} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8bb3f36-623f-5108-95b5-f9fd39c91460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "89358ed4-7921-5b21-8db0-91c53ef07d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337831" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=-5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+2\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016363b1-eb00-5b79-8366-af2b32a79bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308754} {:answer/id #uuid "4235b69b-509f-5572-9d5b-ec5fae71aec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309250} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f34df7-f1ac-5f54-b3dd-e1c05a719601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310094} {:answer/id #uuid "e2585ecf-f274-528d-95f1-5c11b3d41298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310558} {:answer/id #uuid "ed995dd7-a597-54fd-be73-f03af792b4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309250} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01a4e64-f9ae-5ed9-b412-935d0f2d5845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce4febed-fbee-57ca-b73c-257ea0810837" :question/math-academy-id "q-337833" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+1,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=5,$ and ${a}_{0}=1.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e40b26-d2f9-5cd6-9a2c-d7bfb363528d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309548} {:answer/id #uuid "93ab6bef-be6f-53da-9917-0e91fdb02461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309931} {:answer/id #uuid "af154900-e954-5877-83b2-7e3b27ca2593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-9{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310164} {:answer/id #uuid "b173285b-51aa-5ed0-9092-bfb15385c575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310182} {:answer/id #uuid "b881c2dc-d489-595e-bc22-9d2c55d6d7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310182} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e49fc8-062b-5db9-ab29-e5de4f4db21f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "242b8b06-4e20-5f4e-9a4e-7dfd4cd1c55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337835" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=-3,$ and ${a}_{0}=4.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4){x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{3})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+4\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-3\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =6x-9{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c1ce28-8bfe-5b09-a15d-7c4b76ecfa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490} {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} {:answer/id #uuid "a553f444-e68c-53b4-8d93-575a4cc7cf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbe008-7f1a-5a31-9cb7-f9dd2c67fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310318} {:answer/id #uuid "ec46c1a1-e0b3-5094-a3be-1a97aa9a9b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1bdc7f-51da-5393-bc6a-4b97c1c1ced8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a1235f2-6c7a-5f14-b04f-a38d354a3d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337837" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec99442-f19f-572b-9ad6-07a898801684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309884} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bb0709-bd19-534d-b00d-f7fd55310f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310327} {:answer/id #uuid "d1709c2c-6731-5882-8117-b6e8f6a6ba0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310430} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30d71a-2676-5888-b45e-91d98c799fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310512} {:answer/id #uuid "ed697464-4ef2-5a28-9000-ab8c7e06d2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309884} :answer-field/id #uuid "823a51a3-459f-5056-a690-9e7c11e60f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be622853-5ea4-5dac-aa70-1569f1b24b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-337840" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{2}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2)({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}-1)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1)+2({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+3(2x)+2({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 2+6x+2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+6x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0087b403-c7ca-5331-b8d9-439842b47d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308747} {:answer/id #uuid "2318a1d4-07cb-5825-b016-5a6bedb71369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309013} {:answer/id #uuid "24eaf44d-47ed-5093-8aac-20086979fb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309025} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfbec37-9f6d-5505-8a86-50fe7b1a3f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310272} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dfd225-ae4e-51ac-b1b7-2a7e89f67ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309013} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb1b820-e722-50f0-8bee-2ae6008feb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46fe989c-8dfc-559c-bcd3-bca514dce992" :question/math-academy-id "q-337843" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2x,$ and ${f}_{0}=-3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+2x\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{3}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dddf0e-34f0-5031-aace-45115faadba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308891} {:answer/id #uuid "813a884f-f14f-5293-a32a-c190c4747e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309764} {:answer/id #uuid "e91e684d-07b6-5b85-88ac-e716b699e9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310601} {:answer/id #uuid "e96d9ccf-b280-5cf5-9cdc-a247f8c7ce46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310606} {:answer/id #uuid "f37f64c5-7f8f-50e9-85e9-d765c6a1145b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310606} :answer-field/id #uuid "597b35d6-580f-5059-a5c9-8d1b52889a60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c0969102-3c14-5cb9-9e7f-c9144ee9388e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337845" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=4.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}}\\right)+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}}\\right)+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot \\frac{3}{4{x}^{5/2}}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{3/2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b88092c-f1b0-5b21-bcff-0702438385c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308963} {:answer/id #uuid "2fee2ada-5084-5073-8452-95d03b28478c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309112} {:answer/id #uuid "527371ae-d20c-52b2-9071-7ea6db04e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}-t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309372} {:answer/id #uuid "8d717be2-f15a-57cd-87bb-a23bf9117914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{t}^{2}+t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309865} {:answer/id #uuid "a22e65d8-8e69-57de-b4b5-f3f78a287e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+t-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309112} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7330ace-9f1f-543a-8c09-feec4f1b8176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0fdb2e98-21f2-5497-8668-b601a875ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337850" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3)+2{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t=(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3}) & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}+C \\\\ x & =-4t-12+C{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t-3)+2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)(t-3)+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t-3,\\text{ }g(t)=2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ecf3689-32a8-52f4-8a09-f4efc24f8764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309885} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6b4fd9e-ead4-5366-bce4-4d509dde33b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48af113f-49e5-5d9a-b4b3-8a74c6966ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338016" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{5} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211bba12-49fc-51ab-a59b-2db720317018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308998} :answer-field/id #uuid "6693efde-855b-5dd6-8714-f3f849f78007" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "596a720f-02ee-5301-9a2a-a877f3679eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-338017" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-9n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-9n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-9} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-9} \\\\  & ={e}^{-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9352516-6d0b-5e72-955c-90b0b607dab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f086ae1d-0f0c-596f-a3a0-7f9d803aca0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81de37a8-cdfc-542c-9aa2-4efc556c12b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338019" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-7n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-7n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-7} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-7} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "806504e9-2427-536c-95d3-a1f04459c648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(x+y)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309756} :answer-field/id #uuid "459c6091-89f2-57c0-b8b7-668978c32297" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c590dcd-1532-540d-ba51-3f7d0902891d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338037" :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-3n(x+y)} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(x+y)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2928b4b1-785b-59ea-a92b-79c70118fbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{xy}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309059} :answer-field/id #uuid "74715de4-0ead-5349-9341-8e9e949d5303" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c0a6248-969c-53a2-a7ae-a82a71f54bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338040" :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-nxy} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{-xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3bee2ff-300c-592e-aa19-289b355153e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310317} :answer-field/id #uuid "168e1622-34f2-507b-bfdb-86c85cd8ecc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b306e550-2916-514a-a64f-f860060bf5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-338050" :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{e}^{u}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=x\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x\\sqrt{2}}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n\\sqrt{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x\\sqrt{2}})}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6646f4ad-3070-5d5e-a73e-a8c9aa4bf957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309535} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba2a543-9f5b-5df6-9537-ee8be82e0bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309851} {:answer/id #uuid "c2aea39e-077a-58bd-94f8-dcd87e82da37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310310} {:answer/id #uuid "f7abe7b7-5969-551b-a9f2-5bf72b123aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310718} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e4286d-736e-5a47-98a2-d5df445a7847" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310968} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9849d753-4231-5961-a0a9-0b8eebe191ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309974} {:answer/id #uuid "9a74e47f-d900-5fd7-949b-844833fa12f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}-5{y}^{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309998} {:answer/id #uuid "d1848d46-5e76-5a3a-9842-b825578153fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310432} {:answer/id #uuid "f2af46e2-c00d-5e52-8c4e-3e6522464657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310432} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac807c8-917b-5f37-9a1e-f53b5aaaf652" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2024595f-0932-52b3-a267-c0301403f4b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338070" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\nxy\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=xy,\\,Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}-5{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\right)}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322cf370-ce3f-561b-accf-85265e19129f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({a}^{2}{y}_{1}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309126} {:answer/id #uuid "3e646299-2a6c-54d5-9614-b43a63dd052b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309220} :answer-field/id #uuid "18be70a9-1b49-5372-8e60-afe7ce55a136" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114612ca-0213-50da-bb0c-8760b8a077e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308870} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7bd6b1-601a-5d4e-8a82-58c07912f8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308870} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e43c63-82b3-5bb2-8ce4-de01c319df49" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76824752-6ee7-5078-b991-e1b6edbe5d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309674} {:answer/id #uuid "7a25c8fd-526b-51a7-b38a-344bd5c0a690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309674} :answer-field/id #uuid "689ec7ca-4402-52b1-95f9-4ab5e866fe53" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b398575-e53a-54d2-955d-40b34bdafef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309071} {:answer/id #uuid "6339a875-a2aa-597f-be02-0a935a162f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309508} {:answer/id #uuid "b6353c0b-4c0f-530f-9754-8d1489724451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310221} {:answer/id #uuid "d771f50d-dd1b-5d11-86c7-b5a1f1338db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309071} :answer-field/id #uuid "957d63a3-fcdb-5e82-afe9-6d9dd854e90d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36e484ef-910b-5267-82c5-de26a1ddb7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309165} {:answer/id #uuid "953c436a-ae1b-515e-a2ae-c89401c7067f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309942} {:answer/id #uuid "e906ecdd-13fe-555b-80aa-a745e5d9e988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310598} {:answer/id #uuid "ff77c7cb-4d8e-5d80-af3f-bd0168202f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310598} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb049af9-a86c-5889-9326-62af3dd9bdc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f8aeff-c9d9-552a-8102-d9fb9d414d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309265} {:answer/id #uuid "fad8517a-cef3-5d39-ad35-4ac2700dc9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310745} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a9997a-1147-5e57-a22f-60bfde64717e" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d6f9b10-9d35-54d2-b573-00911193c658" :question/math-academy-id "q-338094" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={{field-2}}-2\\cdot {{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2a{y}_{1}-2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2\\cdot (a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & +ab\\cdot ({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a08bf6-bbd5-5c7a-a458-cc2f28795f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450518} {:answer/id #uuid "bc472246-cf39-501b-88ba-aea193a2227d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9ed7ec-92aa-541a-9bea-f3cdc0ae0b0e" :answer-field/key "$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae1de41-bd54-5836-8d70-b2f7e421082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450520} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9484dd-5f72-59ba-b9bf-a220d37834f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450520} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad3fb9a-3bbe-53ce-a61d-00b578edb53b" :answer-field/key "$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfb148a-1391-50e5-a4e7-bcf13b54ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450522} {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450523} :answer-field/id #uuid "187f487c-26a4-5852-ba9e-dad42fa9e85d" :answer-field/key "$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”; “$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2da01cc-d314-5f60-8fba-10973e328ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310562} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a1764e-e5f6-5095-9b21-9bdf74e71f29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2870fd1f-672a-5c18-b50c-82ac76026af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338496" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "19318467-73e5-540d-b86c-017adc969dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312153} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3e3562-fd5d-54d4-8caa-88d822f330b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312200} {:answer/id #uuid "97b676be-c4c7-5257-8509-970da1a7f325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313180} {:answer/id #uuid "d409218f-4886-53c8-97e0-f50143a25677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}-1)}^{1/3}+1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313643} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c59a0c-2468-567e-b011-f60b5ef6112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{1/3}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313941} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd8c0c4-189f-5114-bd6c-75ce244a3e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{3/2}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c648e38-7cc5-5dcf-9021-2394fd32b96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312319} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce1fc5f-2da5-5b0b-80ba-f8df3b61c73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312324} {:answer/id #uuid "33913f9d-f88f-57ed-97d8-78055dbc85b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312390} {:answer/id #uuid "744ef512-134b-571f-9ecd-17d43e03b0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312900} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb5671d-5993-5a82-866f-3ac133f5ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312319} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfa1250-d18e-541a-ad3a-13bb3e49e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "280f32a1-77b0-558e-a146-5c2de5be7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338528" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={\\csc }^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={\\csc }^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{\\csc }^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{\\csc }^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{\\sin }^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{\\sin }^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\left(-\\frac{1}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x}\\right)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2208ccc5-5934-51e2-b52d-8ff31bed4e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312232} {:answer/id #uuid "65d3df9b-2970-5299-ab99-547382b11901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312777} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce9c1bd-fb44-5c82-b8e8-5b81f7969c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312977} {:answer/id #uuid "a95d6aed-c1ec-5c63-b9a8-8a0ba8e816ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313302} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c14440-88e2-525d-85e7-2db79078355d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2t+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313302} :answer-field/id #uuid "9762ce0e-0f2a-5c5c-ad7d-6b8b6bc424c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "732ad1b9-5772-5700-8754-f590c45d555d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33857" :question/problem "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\operatorname{cos}2t,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2t+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2t+{t}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03551aaa-ca90-52f9-8782-7275d1995f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f89725-88f2-5da5-9858-ae34dc226b92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af4eccce-a74f-5bb3-b316-f577c07564aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-338675" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67d9d6a-29f3-513c-8d73-324d6715fe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313280} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef7f8616-8a75-5cf1-81fa-f2f0c0c5f78e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4ef0cfb-cd70-5ecd-8dbc-77f3eae090ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-338676" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x/4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aedd9bd-ed1a-5e77-b2f7-f30da0a5f713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312306} {:answer/id #uuid "57ccbd57-fa23-559b-98b8-b5e841180c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312681} {:answer/id #uuid "cdef54e5-6300-5adf-8b67-f67f3d6adf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313582} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e2fcb-966f-50fe-933a-7d7bca5b43cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313823} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c06983b-ddc0-5fb8-876c-9a1b2e5a5d20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e2552c-69a6-579e-bbf1-16c8783aa693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312231} {:answer/id #uuid "570993af-0477-5d4a-a51a-93ab09600014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312675} {:answer/id #uuid "8abc34ec-135c-55d0-9650-b60ad2fb71aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313078} {:answer/id #uuid "fbced680-9552-56f6-9aa0-2c4c50f109b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}-y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313078} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b72e68-7474-5f06-9316-c9731a3be22e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e13738f-f5b5-5418-9627-22b39e555f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338870" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\right)}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3c72a7-9815-516b-abd7-ac00d7d82af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313074} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c18ae5f-a121-51ad-9c67-21aad4e4fdfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5553df31-6113-5f5b-a73a-06eefe35a840" :question/math-academy-id "q-339080" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=\n$$\n\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a701b78-2f7b-5869-a35c-a68265d84c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312297} {:answer/id #uuid "35b399dd-b281-56b7-8db7-a76855d06ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312408} {:answer/id #uuid "c01117c8-2cdb-582a-8d44-4d6c98807eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313480} {:answer/id #uuid "e4465b0a-93ed-52f4-b50c-bf89f91e8717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313777} :answer-field/id #uuid "619b5a02-9bcf-5c4e-9804-90020e919aba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8ab38f-ccc9-5c4d-9953-e2511696a304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312314} {:answer/id #uuid "82438980-e317-5426-8c57-7d992347312f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313024} {:answer/id #uuid "a83c841e-df51-5c6e-b081-e26fb120e44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313294} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3cf1a5-b0c2-595f-a092-5ea9a23fd911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313024} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5afcbd6-0c6a-5f61-91f9-74372beb0557" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f406ffe6-dbd4-575f-96ea-c90a9a6d9f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-339140" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y=7,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{5}{x}$ and $Q(x)=7.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (5/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})} \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y) & ={x}^{5}\\cdot 7 \\\\ {x}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{x}^{4}y & =7{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{5})\\,dx & =\\int 7{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{5} & =\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e3d989-efe2-5099-9a9b-85e7153a3b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b165d46c-7ad2-56cc-888a-401d7c0e640d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e75bdea-4506-5a3d-8157-9fb9c7f2fd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-339311" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Suppose that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5.5}^{2})$ and $P(X\\le 18.2)=0.5871.$ Find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 18.2) & =0.5871 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}\\right) & =0.5871 \\\\ \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.22)=0.5871\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)=0.22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & =0.22 \\\\ 18.2-\\mu  & =1.21 \\\\ \\mu  & =16.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b44564d-26f2-50c8-87e0-f2645ad2c37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312958} {:answer/id #uuid "ae77827a-103c-47cc-9b1b-a64042a0e439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312958} :answer-field/id #uuid "9079bb5c-29d1-568e-a495-9cbae6e4daf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "397e8c27-583b-5dc5-be7b-21bdc9ee9f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-339417" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x})=\n$$\n\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x}) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2-3x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 9\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{-3})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/60/60c4ef99a2617fd076e6350ba68e14311ebc41b9b0f33fcc07b871198dfc103d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}=\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79f7dce-86ef-5437-9731-cb076d7a899e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313536} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bdfb0b4-ea2f-515d-a56b-214a694dbd5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "530ff544-ed9d-585e-bfc3-0dae9081b382" :question/math-academy-id "q-339421" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad63655-5741-5783-92a5-8689161f1bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313079} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cc8283-3d3c-579a-9cdb-ebfc85b9bfc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76e07179-8131-59be-8696-de3b1d10efb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339426" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},\n$$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa8bd9b-23ac-5a6b-aa52-08253648c67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313315} :answer-field/id #uuid "775fbe06-942a-5f66-b830-7e6bcac4e1aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f1b72358-e12f-522e-a6d1-8b47c27da23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339477" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f0d6aa8-606b-5bec-a3af-6502cd631d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8cb47d-8737-5de7-ae92-ad8fc0cb2ccc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dced79c7-5f2c-5235-8004-8d45b7bc9bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339490" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}\\sin \\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}\\sin \\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\sin \\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\sin \\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e10653be-867c-5341-a9ce-77ea1b9ee394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313748} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a261e03-e5e6-5da7-844b-0c51a25e9eca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82116abe-5463-5037-8e4c-b820d9de02f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339500" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bf7e6c8-a0bd-56ae-bc0c-e1c77f890628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312969} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b24747-e118-5aad-bea1-00048eb84440" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3551f74-1b2f-52cf-ac61-38bcccfdf9b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339507" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21749827-39e7-519d-b62a-f9fab0f965e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312228} :answer-field/id #uuid "37eb3354-e3be-51e7-b37d-16ee271b0ced" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86dc4167-3401-5668-995a-2c45fdd8be71" :question/math-academy-id "q-339602" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=0.1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)^{′}=-\\frac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}+1)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-1)-{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}+1)}{{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0}+1)}{{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1\\,(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.1)+1)}{0.1-1} \\\\  & =0.144…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}+1)}{{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.144…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.144…)+1)}{0.144⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.157…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}+1)}{{x}_{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.157…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.157…)+1)}{0.157⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.158….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.16$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.16$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈0.16,$ rounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b721041-b6c2-5aef-8bf9-28bb0b74728d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313087} :answer-field/id #uuid "a42dd984-26b3-5282-8c29-060daecfc0a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "626d970f-3209-5977-874c-ff5fe2eb9780" :question/math-academy-id "q-339612" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20ed8c1-6367-5397-a4b8-0aa0377b5e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313756} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef1285e-1960-5db1-afb9-134e870588c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5fd476b-94fc-5bb9-8c11-525e44dceb8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339621" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{9}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{6}-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{6}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c24294b-34a4-5d19-98d3-e45b31b33e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312822} {:answer/id #uuid "9de6e7b6-7290-559c-ac83-f864f0b80f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486047} :answer-field/id #uuid "3319e749-9653-5a21-bf0b-e2fb9dca7f41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53a17b4c-4adf-5239-8971-3a7f7c6e95fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-339623" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275c1d94-964b-5932-89b8-8304e38b5d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312274} :answer-field/id #uuid "38743c40-0f33-5dec-9cb7-ba8abf3b9592" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "632f601c-e4fe-5306-bafd-fe80baef5799" :question/math-academy-id "q-339629" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece19b22-a944-5285-ba77-3ea742f25978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313866} :answer-field/id #uuid "4195e51b-51eb-5838-bc99-bb831c4eb305" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a3ec4d7-bd9a-5b1a-b0c1-e121685fdff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339641" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d31ae2cc-d275-5e0f-b4a5-4701fe26c001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313631} :answer-field/id #uuid "77372790-9879-5319-ad1f-abdbc075cbd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45952499-b188-5444-aa5c-78b8d9c18daf" :question/math-academy-id "q-339692" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386e98c1-73bb-5454-b219-d1a0b2cbcd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06227a-dba4-573b-8ee8-7fe7ab605c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313101} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ccfeb-d107-5fd5-a633-7ce78226c89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313120} {:answer/id #uuid "eba8d785-f5ae-5b9c-bb6e-c13c6ab5bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313855} {:answer/id #uuid "f49e2812-02a9-51d6-8932-6f7fc008518b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e94abc-25eb-5e8f-9907-5fdc535a7393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84d99744-2a44-5b9d-88cd-43a5f0c291b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-3397" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0267a896-74c5-5b6a-b27b-8e244334504d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311976} {:answer/id #uuid "2d57f4be-fff8-5caa-a63a-165fa675b5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312331} {:answer/id #uuid "413590d1-b7ed-5aab-acae-be3b0bfef64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311976} :answer-field/id #uuid "749092a4-9e67-5dc5-8152-305a7ba27dbb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314320} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61000014-4a8a-501f-9389-e2c81c9c94ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312750} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6839fd-d3d6-5b46-83bc-4bad38c185b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312851} {:answer/id #uuid "76547f14-425e-5c0f-b58f-3665b0aa4499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312851} :answer-field/id #uuid "a826f669-c0e3-5d5a-8f2b-3ae1986ceb41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad65dfee-7df3-53be-83ea-f3d46d2d65cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339715" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-3y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-0.6-1)}{5} \\\\  & =-0.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈-0.60,\\,y(1)≈-0.64.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fdaccb-44ee-513e-b61a-14faf21acd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312428} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a83415-5913-5292-a7c5-38bff1aaad48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313353} {:answer/id #uuid "ef06ec6c-fd8e-57c4-ab91-53334a6f4a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313883} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a731d80-f0b9-525f-b669-5ef7871cae54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ebd4b-4c9c-53c5-81ad-65429e66db8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312154} {:answer/id #uuid "98997939-c002-53b0-bd28-d229ac6a731d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313185} {:answer/id #uuid "d6481507-f829-5e1c-bc63-268813bf7b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313663} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd69bc0-5c90-513d-a0d6-ccae925f496c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41ae10ea-8c16-5d43-beba-37b186a54971" :question/math-academy-id "q-339720" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ 2(1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1}{2-3{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=2.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2.2-{1}^{2}+1}{2-3\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-4.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈2.20,\\,y(1)≈-4.40.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2621e6d9-5ba6-519f-a60a-091d579a6438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312264} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc64d42-80af-5a3a-9863-3e3da800ae63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312725} {:answer/id #uuid "7f71a58d-c768-51b9-b11c-7592f0084172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313000} {:answer/id #uuid "87e70513-0a6f-5c8d-913b-54bb8aea519d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312725} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd9f54c-7420-555b-9ae8-ffab64d2499a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab0e7391-8cdd-566f-a4b7-764e0b08932e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339755" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{3}-0=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+2y+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈0.25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa96fbb-4ce3-50cc-b336-e4e0343086b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312171} {:answer/id #uuid "2533da89-522f-5a16-bf62-60fddb9bdca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312257} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd82a24-9c1f-58f9-a047-854b334fc85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312819} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0b2591-cd41-5d0d-8071-eecc837dba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312171} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1bd69c-3f32-5735-98f8-4b6f2bef19fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7d834df-07d0-5001-b5bc-497ce36e914d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339756" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.00.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b22500b-1113-5647-8682-78a1b7ff9910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312175} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce656c9-2379-52d8-8198-51db4ea52923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312193} {:answer/id #uuid "40b2eb9f-c5c8-58ff-8942-d452ed77e325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312494} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6f54ab-f955-5b68-8c37-02e2900c2f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312175} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd100af9-782e-5adf-8c3a-86ad19e2e90d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8932a695-ec28-5f7e-8f25-589d63c887b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339758" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-3y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{3}-(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-3y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x-3y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{4}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & ≈-0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-0.89.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3e3562-fd5d-54d4-8caa-88d822f330b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312200} {:answer/id #uuid "97b676be-c4c7-5257-8509-970da1a7f325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313180} {:answer/id #uuid "d409218f-4886-53c8-97e0-f50143a25677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}-1)}^{1/3}+1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313643} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c59a0c-2468-567e-b011-f60b5ef6112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{1/3}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313941} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd8c0c4-189f-5114-bd6c-75ce244a3e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{3/2}-1]}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312200} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdbc1fed-3a67-5c13-b618-6218f8cc694e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f9201e5-21fe-504c-b7c9-529d005689a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33978" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(t)=3t\\,i+2{t}^{3/2}\\,j+4\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by  $s=2(1+t)^{3/2}-2.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parameterization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $y$-component of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=2(1+t)^{3/2}-2$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =2(1+t)^{3/2}-2 \\\\ s+2 & =2(1+t)^{3/2} \\\\ \\frac{s+2}{2} & =(1+t)^{3/2} \\\\ {(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3} & =1+t \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =3[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]\\,i+2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+4\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $y$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be74376b-f6cf-583f-95b4-d0baeaa08b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313468} :answer-field/id #uuid "daac023f-b3e5-5739-9d3f-7f31e324cbea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb1c657b-33e1-538c-a41b-0a68255fe01e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339797" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dcc27e7-95b3-51a3-8ce2-f478b5639a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312985} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c95f7d-1794-5dae-b0e8-f350d95dcde1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71ed2e26-0e24-5b2f-bb68-bcacff682ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-339798" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3f7c82-cae2-53b1-bfa9-62149e395d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312087} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1117d4-95e6-5832-8a65-21307db4780a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312349} {:answer/id #uuid "6e770a45-6c01-5220-8214-db5a6ee20325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312844} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b620c4-ddb8-5a67-87e7-d555b955294f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313396} {:answer/id #uuid "ca454db5-de8d-5847-af88-d2f058efd57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312844} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cddafe9-672b-573c-8e52-d14edb1f25f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c5988885-b8cf-5d2a-b055-96c1614a3b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3398" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1264a285-c4d1-5d3a-bb63-43c0db639e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312106} {:answer/id #uuid "d5766661-22c8-5618-ae9d-74a97e9e44cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313658} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4cac87-331e-54e9-a2cd-00d0ba9b4bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313658} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b68bfe4-2089-5f38-a971-a1a3b639f90c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ca992b-171d-5fe0-9d01-e6198efc192e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312368} {:answer/id #uuid "8128e534-a869-5780-8494-9ed56b26e9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313015} {:answer/id #uuid "badc3fd5-1e50-50ae-bc51-2e86a71b2bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312368} :answer-field/id #uuid "6038bc88-c9ca-58eb-bb78-00a3c88c35b7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1016d90c-aaef-55bd-b702-1b08e5f96ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312089} {:answer/id #uuid "44c165f2-87b3-57ef-8501-65d02dd8eafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312531} {:answer/id #uuid "799e9d13-f319-5a96-98fd-73c05b740ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312531} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7d1035-2f98-5697-b98c-c8fadffe3983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60ddfc96-62ae-580f-ba85-c7bc3da3aae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first three sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate points, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+1,\\,-1+\\frac{13}{4}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}+{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{4}+\\frac{25}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{75}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2)≈\\frac{17}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170aa240-cae2-52ed-9b4d-d8fff43afeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312136} {:answer/id #uuid "5775b3d2-6d0e-530a-a61c-a0a9036531ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312676} {:answer/id #uuid "dacb89df-9b2e-5d3d-93b0-c305bade6465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312136} :answer-field/id #uuid "076db3a5-eebb-5f3b-b90b-a2d8d8e9067f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3152c09f-adc2-5cfc-a8dc-529f831d41de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312375} {:answer/id #uuid "461e1b47-36b8-5e15-925b-c6a8bb42ffb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312551} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0c68f-fdfd-53ea-be8c-dd34a3ce85a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312551} :answer-field/id #uuid "332a7dee-8fd3-5ecf-8a3a-522ed51e0cb0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4920a58a-1e44-52f2-96c2-3a99dad3b5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312562} {:answer/id #uuid "4fac5fe3-35fc-5c86-9830-825533cd2fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312622} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf387d-eee6-5109-af76-8dccfc4227c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312622} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7fefbd3-7e41-5bf8-81fb-de1311ca3f14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cce8574-92fe-5eb1-8c62-51298ef5954f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339801" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first three sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate points, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =-2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-2+2\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040c6c17-3436-5025-b447-296818133ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311993} {:answer/id #uuid "56263254-dd2b-5b7a-a29f-77245dee9f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312671} {:answer/id #uuid "d974185f-1cc5-5d96-a95b-6ff4fede4f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313686} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bcdda3-e0ff-57f2-9641-cafd6253233b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d0fb172-2ae4-5406-b49c-fb396d0cad12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312834} {:answer/id #uuid "a822b381-7869-5e5b-9a6d-da90caf258ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313292} {:answer/id #uuid "f8738415-31e2-5fc9-96ae-016359fbeb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313959} :answer-field/id #uuid "9579d72a-3120-532a-b352-eeca766b4470" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f393ba-353b-5ca6-941a-2a08ba2f78be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312647} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef5cf0c-c822-5c42-acfd-89877d7473d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312848} {:answer/id #uuid "d94fd1d4-0bb7-5779-b52d-537b3ee73f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36977be-f182-59b9-ac48-a9658473336b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116cdb42-e369-54a4-be22-dcaf9245098f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312099} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce3b02f-05f7-5cc2-8282-36a6728f55ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312325} {:answer/id #uuid "78f10c1b-f182-589d-991d-8baf6891a068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312099} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b86f1e-1201-5fa1-ae9a-e2b161e308b5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da7dfc6b-7e7b-5ae5-a922-426bbe4e23bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339813" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy+{x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $1$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first two sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate point, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+({x}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{13}{8}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+\\frac{15}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{23}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}+({x}_{3})^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{23}{8}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{15}{8}+\\frac{27}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{13}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34083c75-6e5b-50d9-a4b3-3d02a7b06ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312395} {:answer/id #uuid "3893288d-2f1f-5ced-b35f-3fcef1875841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312434} {:answer/id #uuid "7be304d1-8e9b-5b65-8390-0a0e6ef47317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312967} :answer-field/id #uuid "6876a207-93ce-5238-81a1-126858d55855" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105699ff-5c50-5c8d-b74e-bb77b49a9869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498