using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $1$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}^{2}-2{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-2+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-2)+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390133c1-c9a0-518b-a603-5b9b7e8510ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312437} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe79f03-6019-5209-b2d4-236e7ccea6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312489} {:answer/id #uuid "4d22c93e-8539-5fe4-843e-30c08ac16782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{2s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312599} {:answer/id #uuid "78d21419-b604-5de8-943f-39fd19b2df7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312938} {:answer/id #uuid "d0468ebe-2044-55f6-b33c-730b38ac234b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6bebbc-0cf8-5a0f-afb0-2e619117694a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e98268a-2c12-50af-adfa-eb48d558ff91" :question/math-academy-id "q-33984" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=8t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=10t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=10t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{s}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6s}{10}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6s}{10}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4s}{5}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee43064e-0f89-5585-a053-1d63767ced61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313876} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d058b35-5591-5f8b-a767-e8b9b61eb060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa6c947b-9991-5d44-84a4-afcc1fb08ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339842" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{arctan}\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =5(\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})-\\text{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddf94f51-0ff1-50ae-bad8-f27b8dd41ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313722} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d695fb2-0a1c-5813-8633-32337be30f6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45102bcc-b0f0-58bb-8720-e0d524eca593" :question/math-academy-id "q-339843" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3(\\arcsin \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa544ec9-1c8e-59f8-913a-b91dc1add300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{e}-e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313311} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad82966-c6d3-5c50-ae84-9a8846ab1a61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebe55b01-de25-5ef8-8b19-531f984d54e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-339850" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}({e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{e}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{e}-{e}^{1})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =({e}^{e}-e)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{e}-e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2403bf92-33de-56e0-85ef-a581cb1a6312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312245} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2fc704-d0e9-53a6-8dcc-0e036a630979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312456} {:answer/id #uuid "6d581f87-d639-5aae-81d8-ac5d040ae6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312836} :answer-field/id #uuid "5396f264-ea0d-5db9-b353-f86fbe694fe4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7206c63b-c464-5134-84a6-49aa5ac04832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10.59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312871} {:answer/id #uuid "7f92d116-7979-53c8-a931-6899507137ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313002} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed41681-02ae-5438-b215-763092f232ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a15b0a1-53a8-5905-b174-0fe6fa338599" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64ba9a3d-b554-5a79-ad69-4fec723c3b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-339865" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=3.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+6\\cdot (3.25)) \\\\  & =10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈3.25,\\,y(1)≈10.50.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1987c9ee-a7f8-50f9-a545-25e4dcbbcd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312159} {:answer/id #uuid "20a1a86a-cb58-5fdc-972a-4fb0b08350a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312222} {:answer/id #uuid "26870ffb-12d5-5e63-a931-434dc45c6e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312159} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e726c7-c2a4-5b49-943a-bffcdfa1a1af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d3804a1-31a0-54e6-aedd-b16580fa2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312835} {:answer/id #uuid "8750b333-5234-51fb-b4ac-50415c2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313056} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef3dbc-8ff6-5b7b-9bd0-5d1c554139d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312835} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ea1a90-a4f9-5314-ac16-6781e319b64c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fd2de3b-c285-5953-b35a-1631bb0e5b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339867" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =1.30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+3\\cdot (1.30)) \\\\  & =1.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.08.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbc7a91-b04a-5d0a-ab60-07d245bddb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312335} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9869da-d045-5377-9e44-55ed8d6afff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313314} {:answer/id #uuid "b583f664-6d43-506e-85b8-d4aaf90c2bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313387} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2b02ba-b283-5602-8a97-9aad598c3a7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba3893c-fe5c-52da-a6bd-64be557ff52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-124.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312180} {:answer/id #uuid "440ea820-9647-5812-b3ae-9bbf40d7797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-111.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312523} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1d36c-2495-5036-821e-6d50799d57d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-122.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312180} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ec2869-77fd-5286-a4ab-9e9e7155bcae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "766f6cd8-a666-5ac2-92f9-bc5ddd8560c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-339868" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+5y,\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+5y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+9\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =-124.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈13.00,\\,y(2)≈-124.00.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ffbef5-670e-5b43-8085-11ebb12b962e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312897} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab1000-6847-56ca-87cc-11d6d8e28a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313447} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e27a90-0a77-53f1-81fc-51180d0cd9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313829} :answer-field/id #uuid "378a03ef-145b-5a5d-8fa5-e903d464e8c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826de63c-65ce-5b8e-bdf5-ec0609ee7b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313026} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ab2b3f-caa2-5bdb-abd4-bc797d65298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313674} {:answer/id #uuid "e85c5b8d-635c-5190-b6b9-f372df08fde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313026} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d9bd4-f9cd-5c8e-bec5-f50b0b58de10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56bd69c9-5c91-506a-8565-7dbf3ccfd848" :question/math-academy-id "q-339869" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ and $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-3x+y)+(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ and $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4},\\,y=3.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+9\\cdot (3.75)) \\\\  & =4.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈3.75,\\,y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈4.50.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09797b8b-4847-5003-adf4-243e44b2236b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312044} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3b1f1f-f3cc-5f01-b27f-6db2ff81d4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312728} {:answer/id #uuid "762b3b2e-11c3-5a87-82ea-9f879d5dfed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312919} {:answer/id #uuid "bb522802-18f4-5695-b0b2-37dd3b41cc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312044} :answer-field/id #uuid "355e30b4-dcc5-5384-8098-953b613193c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50469212-57da-54a4-898c-5e445181e007" :question/math-academy-id "q-339872" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{x}^{3}+3),\\,y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2{x}^{3}+3)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{\\text{new}}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}+7)}{1-2\\cdot {0}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & ≈2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈2.25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ee19c8-ffa3-51c2-90c8-f32e76bf3ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312005} {:answer/id #uuid "04e1e1ed-ae8d-46b8-ab8d-1085b1802387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11/(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312005} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6760650-0666-56ec-a209-471cd8d6b267" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dffc415e-0ffd-5f91-84e8-ab042b2a6f3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339949" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{11}{4}\\ln ⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77574496-a3d5-5133-b54d-5a41edeef925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312931} {:answer/id #uuid "8d49850c-09b0-4746-acd2-5bace5c80ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7/(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5f9fb9-2594-5780-812f-98f59dc7cafe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eeedc903-d84c-5e00-bc96-dcee7bf9bc01" :question/math-academy-id "q-339950" :question/problem "Given that $y=-\\frac{7}{3}\\ln ⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{7}{3}\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4252b1b-dd35-545d-814f-9733f49b7285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313645} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1326d4-3786-5ce8-bdc5-383026fa0fef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6854d6-273e-50f7-ac16-5801c62a2345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312576} :answer-field/id #uuid "b220aac7-54ee-544e-b56a-707200ae420e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b715659f-46cf-51b3-82e4-5f218178bf7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339967" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}[3\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2] \\\\  & =3\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0))+2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{7x+2}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{7x+2}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7(2)+2}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277904a9-9e9e-53f0-a87c-616303962482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312275} {:answer/id #uuid "fce43cba-9363-5793-89ef-2161d7a4191d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55-\\frac{1}{\\ln 6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525833} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb11537-ff22-5776-85db-c4e4d92da407" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9fb0b98-97be-5fa4-a0a8-73b036fd9d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-339994" :question/problem "Given the function $y=2{x}^{3}+x-3{\\log }_{6}⁡x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+x-3{\\log }_{6}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(6{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡6} \\\\  & =55-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c897e4-b652-55dd-8d72-bc5035453929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24-\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312506} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f3d565-6897-5bcf-9632-a037dba3624d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1eb887e7-89db-539c-9d35-d652e9bb15c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340024" :question/problem "Given the function $y=2{x}^{3}-5{\\log }_{3}⁡x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-5{\\log }_{3}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{3}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{x\\ln ⁡3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(6{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{x\\ln ⁡3})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =6(2)^{2}-\\frac{5}{2\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{5}{2\\ln ⁡3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c560d9f-eb63-53d7-9885-bb7d892bd6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312711} :answer-field/id #uuid "46262c9a-2752-53a2-9e99-9d1d8b76e486" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314204} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90270ffd-730a-5d44-97df-31e6ba1a64c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313127} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45506fa-99d0-5e0c-ac12-1cf43e87ae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ea70379-b7a9-5bc9-bb66-83755fd01a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340025" :question/problem "The largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-3x}-\\sqrt{x+3}$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,[$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$].$" :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-3x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\le 4\\,⟹\\,x\\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{x+3}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}]\\cap [-3,\\infty )=[\\,-3,\\frac{4}{3}]\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101f0087-1aec-50e5-a9b7-d806a0ea860a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312090} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7fc2dd-1d62-5a23-a97f-19baf25d47a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b69d4c2-7ebc-59ed-aa14-566d23bb3592" :question/math-academy-id "q-340026" :question/problem "Given the function $y=6{\\log }_{2}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-3x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{\\log }_{2}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡2}+4\\cdot 2x-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{x\\ln ⁡2}+8x-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{6}{x\\ln ⁡2}+8x-3)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1\\cdot \\ln ⁡2}+8(1)-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\ln ⁡2}+5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d3f58d-d92f-5f2c-988b-dccb67baa3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313807} :answer-field/id #uuid "089bf360-455a-53f9-9d02-ae9eeb2ae855" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc62e09c-b050-5ce8-9552-a16b01968f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313710} :answer-field/id #uuid "3506b889-7a45-541b-a830-48b58a91354c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2e9e97-7890-5db7-b054-ece0f9e72fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312199} :answer-field/id #uuid "4954787b-8446-568f-bec3-99bc8fda1ebb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c57566f6-cede-590e-ba8d-9a9367859973" :question/math-academy-id "q-340088" :question/problem "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ The largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,[\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$].$" :question/worked-solution "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ but the function $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ),$ and $(\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ namely\n\n$$\n[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )\\cup (\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596bfc33-1d46-58a1-9057-d28b147c68d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312691} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac34aef1-8e01-5f48-b043-096d0f12b55a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff04e1e3-e896-53d9-a3c6-32e3bf7042a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-340099" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2+e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566f2bfd-9b10-5889-ad0c-475cdee4aa4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312672} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ea6b00-43f9-4fdc-acf1-c038af2472c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577407} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e78364-3f9e-4be0-ae1f-b00b2376e0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577408} {:answer/id #uuid "300717cb-e670-40a1-91a5-06692e51640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac32+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577409} {:answer/id #uuid "8137b407-62ef-4c62-92f0-82fd04033c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac32+2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577410} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd40dba-217b-416c-8b04-73dccdf7e392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac32+2e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312672} :answer-field/id #uuid "675f05c9-21d9-5557-bbbf-524e71c7eea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4272d4a8-55fd-5380-aaa8-0f20266b3bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340100" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2{e}^{x}+4$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2{e}^{x}+4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x}+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x}+2{e}^{x})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+2{e}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39500646-122f-5de4-a3f8-1b1dfa91aa61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312439} {:answer/id #uuid "c5134f79-de9b-56b2-8b51-7a5d813bddac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{4\\ln 2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525705}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525705} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1560d5a-5716-563a-970a-84ea931d3b0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "905686ba-b3f6-58e7-998d-63ec7c2c2866" :question/math-academy-id "q-340104" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}+5$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2))|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
